window.GIQ={"meta":{"fold":0,"tier":1,"uw":"Chapter","lede":"","top":95,"fac":{"n":95,"nsrc":19},"nonotes":1},"lectures":[{"id":"BIO-C1","t":"Energy and nutrient requirements"},{"id":"BIO-C2","t":"Macronutrients: carbohydrates, proteins and fats"},{"id":"BIO-C3","t":"Insulin and glucagon"},{"id":"BIO-C4","t":"Vitamin basics and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, K, E"},{"id":"BIO-C5","t":"Vitamin C and the energy-releasing B vitamins"},{"id":"BIO-C6","t":"Heme: synthesis, porphyrias and bilirubin"},{"id":"BIO-C7","t":"Blood-building vitamins: B6, folate and B12"},{"id":"BIO-C8","t":"Iron"},{"id":"BIO-C9","t":"Minerals"}],"groups":[{"l":"BIO-C1","u":0,"label":"BMI = weight ÷ height squared","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G027-8.jpg","cap":"BMI formula as kg/m² and 703 × lb/in², with height-weight chart","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-2-1","s":"Emma is 5 ft 3 in tall and weighs 149 lb. She may be considered:","o":["A. Underweight with a BMI of 18","B. Normal weight with a BMI of 22","C. Overweight with a BMI of 26","D. Obese with a BMI of 30"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Overweight with a BMI of 26","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Overweight with a BMI of 26.</b> Body mass index (BMI) is a person's weight divided by the square of their height. Emma's numbers are in pounds and inches, so use the pounds-and-inches form: 703 × weight in pounds ÷ (height in inches)². She is 5 ft 3 in, which is 63 inches. 703 × 149 = 104,747. 63² = 3,969. 104,747 ÷ 3,969 = <b>26.4</b>. A BMI from 25.0 to 29.9 is called <b>overweight</b>.</p><p><b>The two forms of the formula.</b> The original formula is metric: BMI = weight in kilograms ÷ (height in meters)², so the unit is kg/m². The number 703 is only a conversion factor. It makes pounds and inches give the same kg/m² answer. On a question, look at the units you are given first, then pick the form that matches.</p><p><b>The four classes to memorize.</b></p><ul><li>Below 18.5: underweight.</li><li>18.5 to 24.9: normal weight.</li><li>25.0 to 29.9: overweight.</li><li>30.0 or more: obese.</li></ul><p><b>What BMI is for, and its weak spot.</b> BMI is the number used to define obesity. It is an indirect measure of body fat, because it only knows weight and height. It does not know what the weight is made of. So it misleads in a very muscular person, whose BMI can read \"overweight\" or \"obese\" even with little body fat.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"1"},{"id":"G105-3-1","s":"A 30-year old sedentary worker, 180 cm tall and 180 lb body weight, may be classified as:","o":["A. Overweight with a BMI of 26.6","B. Normal weight with a BMI of 30","C. Underweight with a BMI of 26.6","D. Obese with a BMI of 26.6","E. Normal weight with a BMI of 25"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Overweight with a BMI of 26.6","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Overweight with a BMI of 26.6.</b> Body mass index (BMI) is weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared. This question mixes the units: the height is metric (180 cm) but the weight is in pounds (180 lb). Convert the weight first. 1 kg is 2.2 lb, so 180 ÷ 2.2 = 81.8 kg. The height is 1.80 m, and 1.80² = 3.24. 81.8 ÷ 3.24 = <b>about 25</b> (25.2). That is a little lower than the 26.6 printed in the option, but both numbers sit in the same class: <b>25.0 to 29.9 is overweight</b>. So \"overweight\" is the only label that fits him.</p><p><b>How to solve any BMI question.</b> First get the weight into kilograms (pounds ÷ 2.2) and the height into meters (centimeters ÷ 100). Then divide the weight by the height squared. Last, match the number to its class: below 18.5 is underweight, 18.5 to 24.9 is normal, 25.0 to 29.9 is overweight, and 30.0 or more is obese. Check that the label and the number in an option agree with each other. An option such as \"normal weight with a BMI of 30\" is wrong on its face, because 30 is obese.</p><p><b>What BMI is trying to estimate: body fat, the body's main fuel store.</b> Each gram of fat holds 9 kilocalories (kcal). Carbohydrate holds 4, protein holds 4 and alcohol holds 7. Fat tissue is also only about 15% water, while muscle is about 80% water, so fat tissue is nearly pure fuel. In a 70 kg man after an overnight fast, fat weighs about 15 kg and holds about 85% of all stored energy. Protein holds about 14.5%. Glycogen, the storage form of glucose, holds less than 1%.</p><p><b>The two glycogen stores do different jobs.</b> Liver glycogen keeps blood glucose up between meals, because only the liver can release glucose into the blood. Muscle glycogen (about 400 g at rest) can only fuel that muscle's own contraction, because muscle lacks the enzyme glucose 6-phosphatase. Protein is the fuel of last resort, since every protein already has another job.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"2"}]},{"l":"BIO-C1","u":1,"label":"Low thyroid hormone lowers BMR","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-8.jpg","cap":"Thyroid hormone sets mitochondrial activity: low in hypothyroidism, so BMR falls","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"G028-16-1","s":"A 65-year-old woman presents with fatigue and weight gain. Her physician notes bradycardia and cold intolerance. Lab tests reveal elevated TSH and low free T4. Which of the following best explains her reduced basal metabolic rate?","o":["A. Increased lean body mass with aging","B. Decreased thermic effect of food","C. Reduced thyroid hormone-mediated mitochondrial activity","D. Shift toward ketone metabolism due to low carbohydrate intake","E. Enhanced β-oxidation due to elevated glucagon levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Reduced thyroid hormone-mediated mitochondrial activity","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Reduced thyroid hormone-mediated mitochondrial activity.</b> Her labs show a high thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) with a low free thyroxine (T4). That pattern is hypothyroidism: the thyroid gland is failing, and the pituitary answers the shortage by sending more TSH. Thyroid hormone normally keeps the mitochondria busy. With too little of it, the cells burn less fuel and make less heat, so her basal metabolic rate falls. That one change explains every finding: fatigue, weight gain, a slow heart (bradycardia) and feeling cold.</p><p><b>What basal metabolic rate (BMR) is.</b> BMR is the energy the body spends just to stay alive at rest. It pays for the heart, lungs and kidneys, for the ion gradients across cell membranes (mainly the sodium-potassium pump) and for ongoing chemical reactions. It is measured with the person awake, resting, warm and at least 12 hours after a meal. The lab method is indirect calorimetry, which measures oxygen use. BMR is the biggest part of daily energy spending, about 60%. Physical activity is about 30%, and the thermic effect of food (the energy cost of digesting and storing a meal) is about 10%.</p><p><b>How thyroid hormone sets BMR.</b> Thyroid hormone is the body's metabolic thermostat. It builds more mitochondria, makes each one more active and adds more sodium-potassium pumps. So the use of ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency), the use of oxygen and the production of heat all rise together.</p><ul><li><b>Too little hormone (hypothyroidism, for example Hashimoto thyroiditis):</b> autoantibodies destroy hormone production. BMR falls. The person has fatigue, modest weight gain, cold sensitivity and constipation. Labs: high TSH, low free T4.</li><li><b>Too much hormone (hyperthyroidism, for example Graves disease):</b> autoantibodies switch on the TSH receptor, so the gland overproduces hormone. BMR rises. The person has a goiter, weight loss, heat intolerance and more bowel movements.</li></ul><p><b>The other things that set BMR.</b></p><ul><li><b>Lean body mass</b> is the main one. Muscle burns far more than fat tissue, so more muscle means a higher BMR. Women, with relatively more fat, run lower. Lean mass falls with age, which lowers BMR.</li><li><b>Surface area:</b> heat lost through the skin must be replaced, so at the same weight a taller, thinner person has the higher BMR.</li><li><b>Body temperature:</b> fever speeds every reaction. BMR rises 12% for each 1 °C.</li><li><b>Room temperature:</b> BMR is lowest at 26 °C and rises a little in the cold, from shivering.</li><li><b>Growth:</b> children run higher.</li><li><b>Hormones:</b> thyroid hormone, growth hormone, epinephrine and cortisol all raise BMR.</li></ul><p>Race and ethnicity are not among the factors that set BMR.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 8 (Thyroid Disorders and BMR)","L32 Macronutrients slide 5 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Macronutrients lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Papanikolaou): self-assessment exercises","sl":16,"f":"G028"}],"tier":1,"n":"3"}]},{"l":"BIO-C1","u":2,"label":"Rough BMR = 24 × weight in kg","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G027-9.jpg","cap":"Starred rough BMR = 24 × kg; sex factors 0.9 women, 1.0 men","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-7-1","s":"For a male of average build, what would be a reasonable estimate of his basal metabolic rate?","o":["A. 10 kcal/kg weight/day","B. 16 kcal/kg weight/day","C. 24 kcal/kg weight/day","D. 100 kcal/kg weight/day","E. 34 kcal/kg weight/day"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"24 kcal/kg weight/day","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. 24 kcal/kg weight/day.</b> The quick estimate of basal metabolic rate (BMR) is <b>24 kilocalories (kcal) for each kilogram of body weight each day</b>. An easy way to hold it: the resting body burns about 1 kcal per kilogram every hour, and a day has 24 hours. A 70 kg man therefore has a BMR of about 24 × 70 = 1,680 kcal a day.</p><p><b>BMR in one line.</b> BMR is the energy needed just to stay alive at rest: heartbeat, breathing, kidney work and the ion pumps in every cell. It is about 60% of all the energy a person spends in a day.</p><p><b>The estimates, from roughest to most detailed.</b> In these, W is weight in kilograms, H is height in centimeters and A is age in years.</p><ul><li><b>Rough rule:</b> 24 × W, for men and women.</li><li><b>Sex-specific rule:</b> men 24 × 1.0 × W, women 24 × 0.9 × W. Women get 0.9 because more of their weight is fat, and fat tissue burns much less than muscle.</li><li><b>Owen equation:</b> women 795 + 7.18 × W. Men 879 + 10.2 × W.</li><li><b>Harris–Benedict equation:</b> a base number, plus a weight term, plus a height term, minus an age term. Women: 655 + 9.6W + 1.8H − 4.7A. Men: 66 + 13.7W + 5H − 6.8A.</li></ul><p><b>How good they are.</b> Harris–Benedict is the most widely used equation. It overestimates by about 5%, and it is less accurate in the very young, the very old and the obese. No equation is the reference standard. The true BMR is measured by calorimetry (in practice indirect calorimetry, which measures how much oxygen the person uses). Measure it, instead of estimating it, in intensive care or at an extreme body size.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"4"},{"id":"F1010-11-1","s":"Mr. Harris is 6 ft 4 in (1.93 m) tall and weighs 220 lb (100 Kg). His basal metabolic rate (BMR) over 24 hours may be roughly estimated to be:","o":["A. 1930 Kcal","B. 4800 Kcal","C. 3000 Kcal","D. 2400 Kcal","E. 5300 Kcal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"2400 Kcal","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. 2400 Kcal.</b> The rough estimate of basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 kilocalories (kcal) for each kilogram of body weight each day. Mr. Harris weighs 100 kg, so 24 × 100 = <b>2,400 kcal a day</b>.</p><p><b>Two traps built into the question.</b> The rough rule uses weight only, and the weight must be in <b>kilograms</b>. His height (1.93 m) is extra information that the rule does not use. His weight in pounds (220) is also a trap: 24 × 220 gives about 5,300, which is more than double the true answer.</p><p><b>From BMR to the whole day.</b> BMR is only the energy spent at complete rest. A person's daily energy spending has three parts:</p><ul><li><b>BMR, about 60%:</b> the cost of staying alive (heart, lungs, kidneys, ion pumps).</li><li><b>Physical activity, about 30%:</b> the most variable part. It can change several-fold from one person to the next.</li><li><b>Thermic effect of food, about 10%:</b> the energy cost of digesting, absorbing and storing a meal.</li></ul><p><b>Total energy expenditure (TEE).</b> To estimate the calories a person needs in a whole day, multiply BMR by an activity factor that describes their lifestyle: TEE = BMR × activity factor. The factor runs from about 1.2 for a sedentary person to about 2.5 for a very active one. A standard example: a BMR of 1,400 kcal with a factor of 1.6 gives 1,400 × 1.6 = 2,240 kcal a day. For Mr. Harris, a sedentary life would mean about 2,400 × 1.2 = 2,880 kcal a day.</p><p><b>Why TEE matters.</b> It guides diet planning, weight management and feeding of patients with burns or wasting. If the chosen factor is too high, the person gains weight. If it is too low, recovery suffers.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)","L32 Macronutrients slide 9 (Methods for estimating BMR)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":2,"f":"G119"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 16 quiz","sl":11,"f":"F1010"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":30,"f":"D006"}],"tier":1,"n":"5"},{"id":"G105-5-1","s":"John is a 6 ft 1 in (1.86 m) tall and weighs 243 lb (110 Kg). His basal metabolic rate (BMR) over 24 hours may be roughly estimated to be:","o":["A. 4300 Kcal","B. 2640 Kcal","C. 2400 Kcal","D. 3210 Kcal","E. 1930 Kcal"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"2640 Kcal","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. 2640 Kcal.</b> The rough estimate of basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 kilocalories (kcal) for each kilogram of body weight each day. John weighs 110 kg, so 24 × 110 = <b>2,640 kcal a day</b>. Use the kilograms. His height (1.86 m) and his weight in pounds (243) are not used.</p><p><b>Why 24.</b> A resting body burns about 1 kcal per kilogram per hour. Over 24 hours that is 24 kcal per kilogram. So John burns about 110 kcal every hour at rest. Knowing the hourly number is handy, because activity questions ask for the energy spent in a set number of hours.</p><p><b>What this number means.</b> BMR is the energy for basic body functions at rest: the heart, lungs and kidneys, the sodium-potassium pumps that hold ion gradients across membranes, and ongoing chemical reactions. It is defined for a person who is awake, resting, warm and at least 12 hours past the last meal.</p><p><b>When the real BMR differs from the estimate.</b> The rule only knows body weight, so anything else that changes metabolism moves the true value away from it.</p><ul><li><b>Higher than estimated:</b> a lot of muscle (lean mass is the main determinant of BMR), fever (BMR rises 12% per 1 °C), an overactive thyroid, growth in children, and the hormones growth hormone, epinephrine and cortisol.</li><li><b>Lower than estimated:</b> a high share of body fat (this is why women are given a factor of 0.9), an underactive thyroid, muscle wasting and older age, because lean mass falls with age.</li></ul><p><b>Better tools.</b> The Harris–Benedict equation adds height and age to weight, and it is the most used equation, but it still overestimates by about 5%. The reference standard is calorimetry, which measures the BMR directly from oxygen use.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"6"}]},{"l":"BIO-C1","u":2,"label":"Energy for one activity = BMR × factor × hours","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-13.jpg","cap":"Energy for an activity = BMR per hour × activity factor × hours","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G028-14.jpg","cap":"Worked example: 1400 kcal/day × 5.0 × 2/24 of the day ≈ 583 kcal","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-1-1","s":"Mr. Harris weighs 100 Kg. If he spends 90 minutes a day eating his food (with an activity factor of 1.5), what is his total energy expenditure during such activity?","o":["A. 50 kcal","B. 100 kcal","C. 150 kcal","D. 200 kcal","E. 225 kcal"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"225 kcal","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. 225 kcal.</b> Work in three steps. First, his basal metabolic rate (BMR): the rough rule is 24 kilocalories (kcal) per kilogram per day, so 24 × 100 = 2,400 kcal a day. Second, turn that into an hourly rate: 2,400 ÷ 24 = <b>100 kcal an hour</b>. Third, multiply by the activity factor and by the time: 100 × 1.5 × 1.5 hours = <b>225 kcal</b>. Remember that 90 minutes is 1.5 hours.</p><p><b>The rule in words.</b> Energy spent during one activity = BMR per hour × the activity factor × the hours spent. The activity factor says how many times faster than rest the body burns energy during that activity. A factor of 1.5 means one and a half times the resting rate. The long form gives the same answer: daily BMR × factor × (hours ÷ 24) = 2,400 × 1.5 × 1.5 ÷ 24 = 225.</p><p><b>Hourly activity factors.</b></p><ul><li>Resting, such as sleeping: 1.0.</li><li>Very light, such as eating, driving, typing or cooking: 1.5.</li><li>Light, such as level walking, housework or golf: 2.5.</li><li>Moderate, such as brisk walking, cycling, tennis or dancing: 5.0.</li><li>Heavy, such as walking uphill with a load, digging, basketball or soccer: 7.0.</li></ul><p>Example: a person with a BMR of 1,400 kcal a day who does moderate activity for 2 hours spends 1,400 × 5.0 × 2 ÷ 24 = about 583 kcal on it. A person's whole-day activity factor (about 1.2 if sedentary, up to 2.5 if very active) is just these hourly factors averaged over 24 hours. Physical activity is the most variable part of daily energy spending.</p><p><b>Do not confuse this with the thermic effect of food.</b> The question is about the activity of eating. The thermic effect of food is a different thing: it is the energy the body spends to digest, absorb, distribute and store the nutrients. It is highest for protein (20 to 30% of the protein's calories), because removing the nitrogen and making urea is costly. It is 5 to 10% for carbohydrate and 0 to 3% for fat. Overall it is about 10% of the calories eaten, so it is usually left out of daily energy estimates.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 4 (Daily Energy Expenditure)","P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"7"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":3,"label":"Carbohydrate is not an essential nutrient","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-19.jpg","cap":"Carbohydrate not biochemically essential; no specific type required","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G028-21.jpg","cap":"Glycemic index curves: high (over 70) versus low (under 55) foods","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-4-1","s":"An individual is being maintained over a long-term by nutrition supplied via a gastric tube. While a balanced formula diet is best, which one of the dietary components below could be completely eliminated with the least repercussions over the long term?","o":["A. Fat","B. Proteins","C. Water soluble vitamins","D. Fat soluble vitamins","E. Carbohydrate"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Carbohydrate","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Carbohydrate.</b> A nutrient is called <b>essential</b> when the body cannot make it, so it must come from the diet. Carbohydrate is the one item on this list that is <b>not essential</b>. If no carbohydrate is eaten, the liver makes new glucose from amino acids (this is gluconeogenesis), and the body burns more fat. So nothing goes missing that the body cannot make for itself.</p><p><b>What every other part of the formula supplies that the body cannot make.</b> Fat supplies the two essential fatty acids, linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid, and it is needed to absorb vitamins A, D, E and K. Protein supplies the essential amino acids. Vitamins, by definition, cannot be made in useful amounts. Take any of these away for a long time and a deficiency disease follows.</p><p><b>Not essential does not mean free of cost.</b> Carbohydrate is still the main fuel for most cells, especially the brain and red blood cells. When intake is very low, two things happen. The liver burns more fat and makes <b>ketone bodies</b>. And muscle protein is broken down to supply the amino acids for new glucose. This is why enough carbohydrate is said to \"spare protein.\" No single type of carbohydrate is required, because the liver can convert glucose, galactose and fructose into one another.</p><p><b>Simple and complex carbohydrates.</b> Simple carbohydrates (single sugars and short chains) are absorbed fast, so blood glucose spikes after the meal. Complex carbohydrates are digested slowly. Fiber is the part that humans cannot digest at all. Cellulose, for example, has β-links that human enzymes cannot cut.</p><p><b>Glycemic index (GI).</b> The glycemic index says how fast a food raises blood glucose compared with pure glucose, which is given the value 100. It is measured by feeding fasting volunteers a portion that holds 50 g of carbohydrate and then measuring blood glucose every 30 minutes for 2 to 3 hours. A GI above 70 is high and below 55 is low. A high-GI food sends glucose up fast. The large insulin surge that follows overshoots, and glucose dips below where it started. Repeated spikes strain the insulin-making β-cells of the pancreas and promote insulin resistance. A low-GI food releases glucose slowly, keeps blood sugar steady and keeps a person full for longer, so it is preferred in diabetes.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 2 (Objectives)","L32 Macronutrients slide 16 (A 65-year-old woman presents with fatigue and weight gain. Her physician notes b)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"8"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":4,"label":"Soluble fiber + monounsaturated fat blunt glucose spikes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-25.jpg","cap":"Soluble fiber slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G028-44.jpg","cap":"Mediterranean diet: olive oil (monounsaturated fat) protects the heart","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"G028-27-1","s":"Which of the following dietary interventions would best mitigate postprandial hyperglycemia and reduce long-term cardiometabolic risk?","o":["A. Replace whole grains with refined flour products","B. Eliminate all carbohydrates to induce ketosis","C. Increase intake of legumes, soluble fiber, and monounsaturated fats","D. Switch from unsaturated to saturated fat sources for caloric balance","E. Prioritize trans fats as they reduce LDL oxidation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase intake of legumes, soluble fiber, and monounsaturated fats","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Increase intake of legumes, soluble fiber, and monounsaturated fats.</b> The question asks for two things at once: a smaller rise in blood glucose after meals (postprandial hyperglycemia) and a lower long-term risk to the heart. <b>Soluble fiber</b>, which legumes are rich in, handles the first. <b>Monounsaturated fat</b>, such as olive oil, handles the second.</p><p><b>How soluble fiber blunts the glucose spike.</b> Soluble fiber (pectin and β-glucan, found in legumes, apples, citrus, oats and barley) dissolves in water and forms a gel. The gel slows the emptying of the stomach, so sugar reaches the intestine gradually. Blood glucose rises less, and less insulin is needed. In other words, the fiber lowers the glycemic index of the whole meal (the glycemic index is how fast a food raises blood glucose).</p><p><b>How soluble fiber also lowers cholesterol.</b> The gel traps bile acids in the gut, and they are lost in the stool. Bile acids are made from cholesterol. To replace them, the liver pulls LDL cholesterol (low-density lipoprotein, the \"bad\" cholesterol) out of the blood, so blood LDL falls. Bacteria in the colon also ferment soluble fiber to butyrate, an anti-inflammatory fuel for the cells that line the colon.</p><p><b>Insoluble fiber, for comparison.</b> Insoluble fiber (cellulose and lignin, found in whole grains, wheat bran and celery) does not dissolve and is not fermented. It works mechanically: it holds water, adds bulk to the stool, speeds transit and lowers the pressure inside the colon. That means less constipation and possibly less diverticular disease. The daily fiber target is 38 g for men and 25 g for women.</p><p><b>How fat type changes heart risk.</b> Monounsaturated fat (olive oil, canola oil, avocado, nuts) lowers LDL and raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein, which carries cholesterol back to the liver), so the risk of coronary heart disease falls. Saturated fat raises LDL. Trans fat is the worst kind: it raises LDL and lowers HDL. Refined flour moves things the wrong way too, because it is digested fast and has a high glycemic index. Cutting out all carbohydrate is not a good trade either, because the body then breaks down muscle protein to make glucose.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 25 (Soluble Fiber: Metabolic and Colonic Benefits)","L32 Macronutrients slide 21 (How do different carbohydrates affect blood glucose levels?)","L32 Macronutrients slide 44 (Key Protective Components of the Mediterranean Diet)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Macronutrients lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Papanikolaou): self-assessment exercises","sl":27,"f":"G028"}],"tier":1,"n":"9"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":5,"label":"Kwashiorkor: low protein, adequate calories","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-35.jpg","cap":"Marasmus lacks protein and energy; kwashiorkor lacks protein with adequate calories","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1010-13-1","s":"A 3-year old male is brought by his mother to the pediatrician's office. The mother and child are refugees from Somalia and arrived in the United States one week ago. Physical examination reveals a listless child with a rotund abdomen and positive fluid wave. Notable lower extremity edema is present. This patient's condition is most likely caused by a deficiency in which of the following?","o":["A. Protein intake","B. Vitamin intake","C. Fat intake","D. Total caloric intake","E. Carbohydrate intake"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Protein intake","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Protein intake.</b> A listless child with a round, swollen belly that has a fluid wave (free fluid in the abdomen, called ascites) and swollen legs has <b>kwashiorkor</b>. Kwashiorkor is caused by too little <b>protein</b> while the calories, mostly carbohydrate, are roughly adequate. The edema is the key clue.</p><p><b>Why low protein causes edema.</b> Follow the chain one step at a time.</p><ul><li>The child eats carbohydrate, so insulin stays up.</li><li>Insulin blocks the breakdown of muscle protein, so the muscles do not release amino acids.</li><li>With little protein in the diet and none coming from muscle, the liver runs short of amino acids and cannot make enough <b>albumin</b>, the main protein of blood plasma.</li><li>Albumin is what holds water inside the blood vessels. This pull is called oncotic pressure. With low albumin, water leaks out into the tissues and the abdomen. The result is edema and a swollen belly.</li></ul><p><b>The other signs come from other missing proteins.</b> The liver cannot make apolipoproteins, the proteins needed to package fat into VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) for export. Fat stays trapped in the liver, which gives a <b>fatty liver</b>. Too few immune and repair proteins give an itchy rash, dry skin, poor wound healing and frequent infections. Kwashiorkor is often set off by an infection, because infection raises the need for protein.</p><p><b>Compare marasmus.</b> Marasmus is too little of everything: protein and total calories. Insulin is low, so the body freely breaks down its own fat and muscle. The muscle amino acids let the liver keep making albumin, so there is <b>no edema</b>. The child looks wasted: prominent bones, no fat under the skin and loose skin. In short, a swollen child points to protein deficiency (kwashiorkor), and a shrunken child with no edema points to total calorie deficiency (marasmus).</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)","L32 Macronutrients slide 34 (Objective 5)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"},{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":3,"f":"G119"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 16 quiz","sl":13,"f":"F1010"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":32,"f":"D006"}],"tier":1,"n":"10"},{"id":"G028-36-1","s":"A hospitalized patient has been on a low-protein diet for weeks. He now presents with edema, skin lesions, and poor wound healing. Labs show hypoalbuminemia. Which of the following interventions would most directly target his underlying metabolic issue?","o":["A. Increase medium-chain triglycerides to reduce fatty liver","B. Provide balanced amino acid supplementation rich in essential amino acids","C. Administer glucose to spare protein from gluconeogenesis","D. Restrict dietary fiber to enhance protein absorption","E. Supplement with excess omega-6 fatty acids to restore nitrogen balance"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Provide balanced amino acid supplementation rich in essential amino acids","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Provide balanced amino acid supplementation rich in essential amino acids.</b> Weeks of a low-protein diet followed by edema, skin lesions, poor wound healing and low blood albumin (hypoalbuminemia) is <b>kwashiorkor</b>, which is a deficiency of protein, not of calories. His underlying problem is that the liver has no amino acids to build proteins with. So the treatment that goes straight to the cause is to give amino acids, including all the essential ones that the body cannot make. High-quality protein such as milk and eggs does this.</p><p><b>How each of his findings comes from missing protein.</b></p><ul><li><b>Edema:</b> the liver cannot make enough albumin. Albumin holds water inside the blood vessels (oncotic pressure), so with low albumin water leaks into the tissues.</li><li><b>Skin lesions and poor wound healing:</b> skin and repair tissue are built from protein.</li><li><b>Infections:</b> antibodies and other immune proteins run short.</li><li><b>Fatty liver:</b> the liver cannot make apolipoproteins, so it cannot ship fat out as VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein).</li></ul><p><b>Why calories alone do not fix it.</b> In kwashiorkor the calories were never the problem. Carbohydrate keeps insulin up, and insulin blocks muscle breakdown, which is exactly what starves the liver of amino acids. Glucose, fat and fiber contain no nitrogen and no amino acids, so none of them can rebuild albumin. Only protein can.</p><p><b>Kwashiorkor and marasmus side by side.</b></p><ul><li><b>Diet:</b> kwashiorkor is low protein with adequate calories. Marasmus is low protein and low calories.</li><li><b>Insulin:</b> kept up in kwashiorkor. Low in marasmus, so fat and muscle are broken down.</li><li><b>Albumin and edema:</b> low albumin with edema in kwashiorkor. Albumin relatively kept and no edema in marasmus.</li><li><b>Look:</b> a \"plump\" child whose swollen belly hides the muscle loss in kwashiorkor. A wasted child with prominent bones and loose skin in marasmus.</li><li><b>Liver:</b> fatty in kwashiorkor. Normal in marasmus.</li><li><b>Long term:</b> poor immunity and impaired mental development in kwashiorkor. Stunted growth and wasting in marasmus.</li></ul><p><b>Treatment caution.</b> Both need slow refeeding. Feeding too fast causes refeeding syndrome, a dangerous shift of electrolytes. Kwashiorkor also needs electrolytes, antioxidants and control of infection.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)","L32 Macronutrients slide 32 (Amino Acid Balance and the Importance of Protein Quality)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Macronutrients lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Papanikolaou): self-assessment exercises","sl":36,"f":"G028"}],"tier":1,"n":"11"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":6,"label":"Monounsaturated fat lowers LDL, raises HDL","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-40.jpg","cap":"Fat-type table: monounsaturated (olive oil) lowers LDL, raises HDL; saturated raises LDL","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F0114-17-1","s":"Which of the following is associated with lower plasma cholesterol and LDL, higher HDL and decreased risk of coronary heart disease?","o":["A. Butyric acid (contained in butter)","B. Lauric acid (contained in coconut oil)","C. Palmitic acid (contained in palm oil)","D. Stearic acid (contained in cocoa butter)","E. Oleic acid (contained in olive oil)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Oleic acid (contained in olive oil)","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Oleic acid (contained in olive oil).</b> Lower total cholesterol, lower LDL, higher HDL and less coronary heart disease is the profile of <b>monounsaturated fat</b>. Oleic acid is the monounsaturated fatty acid of olive oil. It has 18 carbons and one double bond, written 18:1. Every other fatty acid on the list (butyric, lauric, palmitic, stearic) is saturated, which means it has no double bonds.</p><p><b>The two lipoproteins in the question.</b> LDL (low-density lipoprotein) carries cholesterol to the tissues and artery walls, so high LDL raises heart risk. HDL (high-density lipoprotein) carries cholesterol back to the liver, so high HDL is protective.</p><p><b>The four kinds of dietary fat.</b></p><ul><li><b>Saturated</b> (meat, dairy, coconut oil, palm oil): raises LDL and leaves HDL unchanged. It may raise the risk of coronary heart disease.</li><li><b>Monounsaturated</b> (olive oil, canola oil, avocado, nuts): lowers LDL and raises HDL. It lowers the risk.</li><li><b>Polyunsaturated</b> (fish oil, flaxseed, walnuts, sunflower oil): the omega-6 kind lowers LDL, and the omega-3 kind lowers triglycerides. Both are beneficial.</li><li><b>Trans</b> (partially hydrogenated oils, processed snacks): raises LDL and lowers HDL. It raises heart disease and death from all causes, and no amount is safe. The trans double bond keeps the chain straight, so a trans fat behaves like a saturated fat.</li></ul><p><b>How to read a fatty acid name.</b> In \"18:2, omega-6,\" 18 is the number of carbons and 2 is the number of double bonds. The omega number is the position of the first double bond, counted from the methyl end of the chain (the omega end).</p><p><b>The essential fatty acids.</b> Humans cannot place double bonds near the omega end, because they lack the needed enzymes (the Δ12 and Δ15 desaturases). So two fatty acids must come from food: <b>linoleic acid</b> (18:2, omega-6) and <b>α-linolenic acid</b> (18:3, omega-3). They are used to build membranes and to make eicosanoids, which are signaling lipids such as prostaglandins.</p><p><b>Omega-6 and omega-3.</b> Omega-6 (soybean, corn and sunflower oils) lowers LDL when it replaces saturated fat. In excess it is turned into arachidonic acid, the parent of eicosanoids that promote inflammation. Omega-3 comes from plants as α-linolenic acid (flaxseed, chia, walnuts) and from fatty fish as EPA and DHA (eicosapentaenoic acid and docosahexaenoic acid). EPA and DHA lower triglycerides, clotting, blood pressure and heart risk, with little effect on LDL or HDL. The goal is an omega-6 to omega-3 ratio of about 4 to 1 or lower. Modern diets are above 15 to 1.</p><p><b>What fat does in the diet.</b> Fat is dense energy (9 kilocalories per gram), slows stomach emptying so a meal is filling, and carries vitamins A, D, E and K, which can only be absorbed along with fat.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)","L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":22,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":28,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Dr. Pap macronutrients interactive","sl":17,"f":"F0114"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"2025 IS macronutrients","sl":3,"f":"F0118"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"post-discussion carb digestion & macronutrients IS: macronutrient part only","sl":9,"f":"F0125"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 16 IS","sl":23,"f":"F0172"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":19,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 16 IA session","sl":21,"f":"F0140"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session 16 Fall 2024","sl":29,"f":"F2100"}],"tier":1,"n":"12"}]},{"l":"BIO-C2","u":7,"label":"Mediterranean diet: more monounsaturated fat","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G028-43.jpg","cap":"Bars: same 38% fat; Mediterranean mostly monounsaturated, Western mostly saturated","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G028-44.jpg","cap":"Resveratrol and ω-3 fatty acids as protective Mediterranean components","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F1010-15-1","s":"A typical Mediterranean diet has the same distribution of calories among carbohydrates, protein and fat as a typical Western diet. Which of the following best explains why the Mediterranean diet is considered healthier than the Western diet?","o":["A. It contains more monounsaturated fat","B. It contains lots of red wine","C. It contains lot of bread and butter","D. It contains more polyunsaturated fat","E. It contains more protein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It contains more monounsaturated fat","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. It contains more monounsaturated fat.</b> The question itself tells you the two diets split their calories the same way: about 42% from carbohydrate, 20% from protein and 38% from fat. So the amount of fat cannot be the answer. The difference is the <b>type</b> of fat. Fat in the Western diet is mostly saturated animal fat. Fat in the Mediterranean diet is mostly <b>olive oil, which is monounsaturated</b>.</p><p><b>Why that matters.</b> Monounsaturated fat lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein, the cholesterol carrier that raises heart risk) and raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein, the carrier that returns cholesterol to the liver). Saturated fat raises LDL. So the same 38% of calories from fat can protect the heart or harm it, depending on which fat it is.</p><p><b>The other helpful parts of the Mediterranean diet.</b> Olive oil is the main reason, but the diet also has:</p><ul><li><b>Omega-3 fats from fish</b>, which lower triglycerides and also help protect nerve cells and the retina.</li><li><b>Nuts</b>, which supply omega-6 fats.</li><li><b>Red wine polyphenols such as resveratrol</b>, which are antioxidant and anti-inflammatory.</li></ul><p>The whole pattern is linked to less cardiovascular disease, better insulin sensitivity and longer life.</p><p><b>Why \"just eat less fat\" did not work.</b> Fat supplies 30 to 40% of calories in the United States, and the body stores fat easily and burns it slowly. Campaigns to push fat below 25% of calories failed. People replaced the fat with carbohydrate that is digested fast. That raised insulin and fat synthesis, and it promoted insulin resistance and abnormal blood lipids. The lesson is that fat quality matters more than fat quantity.</p><p><b>Current dietary guideline limits.</b></p><ul><li>Added sugars: less than 10% of calories.</li><li>Saturated fat: less than 10% of calories.</li><li>Sodium: less than 2,300 mg a day.</li><li>Alcohol: no more than 1 drink a day for women and 2 for men.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)","L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":4,"f":"G119"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 16 quiz","sl":15,"f":"F1010"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":34,"f":"D006"}],"tier":1,"n":"13"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":8,"label":"Fed state: blood glucose comes from the diet","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G088-40.jpg","cap":"High insulin: glucose is used and stored. Low insulin: the liver makes glucose.","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 40"},{"src":"../sl/G088-25.jpg","cap":"Three sources of blood glucose: diet, liver glycogen breakdown, liver gluconeogenesis","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"G088-25-1","s":"Insulin is high and glucagon is low. Where does the blood glucose come from in this state?","o":["A. Liver glycogen breakdown","B. The diet","C. Liver gluconeogenesis","D. Muscle glycogen breakdown"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The diet","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. The diet.</b> High insulin with low glucagon describes the <b>fed state</b>, the hours just after a meal. Glucose is pouring in from the gut, and that is where the blood glucose comes from. Insulin was released because of that incoming glucose. Insulin then tells the tissues to take glucose up and store it, and it switches off the liver's own glucose production.</p><p><b>The two hormones and what they signal.</b> The pancreas senses blood glucose directly. When glucose is high, the β (beta) cells release <b>insulin</b>, and tissues take glucose up. When glucose is low, the α (alpha) cells release <b>glucagon</b>, and the liver releases glucose. So the pair of hormone levels tells you which state the body is in. High insulin and low glucagon means fed. Low insulin and high glucagon means fasting.</p><p><b>The three sources of blood glucose, and when each one is used.</b></p><ul><li><b>The diet:</b> in the fed state.</li><li><b>Liver glycogen breakdown (glycogenolysis):</b> between meals and early in a fast. It covers about the first 10 to 18 hours.</li><li><b>Liver gluconeogenesis</b> (making new glucose from non-sugar molecules such as amino acids, lactate and glycerol): in longer fasts, once liver glycogen is running out.</li></ul><p>The two liver sources only switch on when insulin is low and glucagon is high.</p><p><b>Muscle glycogen is never a source of blood glucose.</b> Muscle lacks glucose 6-phosphatase, the enzyme that removes the phosphate so that free glucose can leave the cell. Muscle glycogen can only fuel that muscle's own work. Only the liver can release glucose into the blood.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":25,"f":"G088"}],"tier":1,"n":"14"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":8,"label":"Fed state: glucose oxidation makes the ATP","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G088-28.jpg","cap":"High insulin: glucose is oxidized by glycolysis and the TCA cycle for ATP","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Fatty acid oxidation and ketone production are glucagon effects, seen when insulin is low","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G088-25-2","s":"Insulin is high and glucagon is low. Which fuel is the main source of ATP in this state?","o":["A. Ketone bodies","B. Lipids (fatty acid oxidation)","C. Carbohydrate (glucose oxidation)","D. Protein (amino acid oxidation)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Carbohydrate (glucose oxidation)","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Carbohydrate (glucose oxidation).</b> High insulin with low glucagon is the <b>fed state</b>. Glucose is plentiful, and insulin moves it into cells. The cells burn it through glycolysis and then the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle in the mitochondria, and that is what makes the ATP (adenosine triphosphate, the cell's energy currency). Carbohydrate is the preferred fuel whenever it is available.</p><p><b>Which fuel is burned in each state.</b></p><ul><li><b>Fed (high insulin):</b> carbohydrate. Extra glucose is stored as glycogen and fat.</li><li><b>Fasting (low insulin, high glucagon):</b> fat. Fat is the concentrated fuel. Fat cells release fatty acids, and tissues burn them by β-oxidation.</li><li><b>Long fast or starvation:</b> ketone bodies join in. The liver makes them from fatty acids, and the brain uses them as an alternate fuel, which saves glucose and muscle protein.</li><li><b>Protein:</b> the fuel of last resort, because every protein already has a job.</li></ul><p><b>Why the fed state cannot be burning fat or ketones.</b> Insulin is an anabolic hormone, which means it builds and stores. It raises glucose uptake and the making of glycogen, fat and protein. At the same time it blocks the four fuel-releasing processes: glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis (making new glucose), lipolysis (fat breakdown) and ketogenesis (making ketone bodies). So when insulin is high, fat stays in storage and no ketone bodies are made.</p><p><b>The opposite side.</b> Glucagon and epinephrine are catabolic hormones, which means they break stores down. They raise those same four processes and lower glycogen and fatty acid synthesis. Insulin and glucagon are called counter-regulatory because each one opposes the other.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":25,"f":"G088"}],"tier":1,"n":"15"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":8,"label":"Insulin is the only hormone that lowers blood glucose","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-4.jpg","cap":"Insulin lowers blood glucose; glucagon, epinephrine and cortisol raise it","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-6-1","s":"Which of the following hormones can decrease blood glucose levels?","o":["A. Cortisol","B. Epinephrine","C. Glucagon","D. Insulin","E. Norepinephrine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Insulin","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Insulin.</b> Insulin is the <b>only hormone that lowers blood glucose</b>. It moves glucose out of the blood into muscle and fat, and it stores it as glycogen and fat. Every other hormone on the list raises blood glucose.</p><p><b>The hormones that raise blood glucose.</b></p><ul><li><b>Glucagon:</b> makes the liver break down glycogen and make new glucose.</li><li><b>Epinephrine</b> (from the adrenal medulla) and <b>norepinephrine</b> (from sympathetic nerves): drive glycogen breakdown and also block insulin release.</li><li><b>Cortisol:</b> raises gluconeogenesis, the making of new glucose.</li><li><b>Growth hormone:</b> also opposes insulin.</li></ul><p>It makes sense that there are many of these and only one insulin. Low blood glucose becomes dangerous within minutes, because the brain depends on glucose, so the body has several backup defenses against it.</p><p><b>Where insulin and glucagon come from.</b> The pancreas has two parts. The exocrine part sends digestive enzymes (proteases, lipase, amylase) and bicarbonate to the duodenum. The endocrine part is the islets of Langerhans, which are only 1 to 2% of the gland. The islet cells are:</p><ul><li><b>β (beta) cells, 60 to 80%:</b> insulin.</li><li><b>α (alpha) cells, 10 to 20%:</b> glucagon.</li><li><b>δ (delta) cells, about 5%:</b> somatostatin, a local brake on both insulin and glucagon.</li><li><b>PP (or F) cells, under 1%:</b> pancreatic polypeptide.</li></ul><p><b>What insulin does overall.</b> Insulin is anabolic. It raises glucose uptake and the synthesis of glycogen, fat and protein. It lowers glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis, fat breakdown and ketone body production.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 4 (Regulation of Blood)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 5 (Insulin and Glucagon are)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":6,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"16"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":9,"label":"Insulin and C-peptide released together","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-11.jpg","cap":"Steps 5–7: Golgi cuts proinsulin; insulin and C-peptide share granules, exocytosed together","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Ca2+ entry triggers insulin granule exocytosis from the beta cell","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-24-1","s":"How would one be able to distinguish between endogenous insulin secreted by the pancreas and exogenous human recombinant regular insulin?","o":["A. Endogenous insulin has shorter half-life","B. Exogenous insulin is less susceptible to insulinase","C. Endogenous insulin is secreted with C-peptide","D. Exogenous insulin has a faster onset of action","E. Exogenous insulin is more immunogenic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Endogenous insulin is secreted with C-peptide","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Endogenous insulin is secreted with C-peptide.</b> Endogenous means made inside the body, and exogenous means given from outside. The β cell makes insulin by cutting a larger protein, proinsulin, into two pieces: insulin and a leftover piece called the <b>C-peptide</b> (connecting peptide). Both pieces sit in the same granule and are released together, in equal amounts. A vial of recombinant insulin contains only finished insulin and <b>no C-peptide</b>. So C-peptide in the blood is the marker of insulin that the patient's own pancreas made.</p><p><b>How insulin is built.</b> Insulin has 51 amino acids in two chains: the A chain (21) and the B chain (30). Two disulfide bonds (sulfur-to-sulfur links between cysteines) join the chains, and a third sits inside the A chain.</p><ol><li>The messenger RNA is translated as <b>preproinsulin</b>: a signal sequence, then the B chain, the C-peptide and the A chain, all as one strand.</li><li>The signal sequence leads the strand into the rough endoplasmic reticulum and is cut off there. What remains is <b>proinsulin</b>, which folds and forms its disulfide bonds. The C-peptide holds the strand in the right shape so that the correct cysteines pair up. That is its only job. It has no hormone action.</li><li>In the Golgi and the secretory granules, proteases cut out the C-peptide. One proinsulin gives <b>one insulin and one C-peptide</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Why nothing else tells the two apart.</b> Recombinant human regular insulin has the exact human amino acid sequence, so it is the same molecule as the body's own insulin. It has the same half-life (4 to 6 minutes), it is broken down by the same enzyme (insulinase, mostly in the liver), and the immune system does not react to it more. (Older pig and cow insulins did cause more immune reactions.) It is not faster either: injected regular insulin takes about 30 minutes to start working.</p><p><b>How this is used.</b> In a patient with low glucose and high insulin, measure the C-peptide. High C-peptide means the patient's own β cells are over-secreting, as with an insulin-secreting tumor or a sulfonylurea drug. Low C-peptide means the insulin was injected. C-peptide is also cleared from the blood more slowly than insulin, so it is the better measure of how much insulin the pancreas is making.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 11 (Synthesis of Insulin)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 22 (Recombinant Insulin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":24,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"17"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":9,"label":"Low C-peptide = injected (exogenous) insulin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-23.jpg","cap":"Red line: insulin preparations contain only mature insulin, no C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 23"},{"src":"../sl/G029-11.jpg","cap":"Own beta cells release insulin and C-peptide together from the same granules","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0088-17-1","s":"A medical examiner was called to investigate the death of a 75-year-old type 1 diabetic Caucasian male who was a retired physician. His caretaker discovered his body in the bedroom with an empty syringe and a small bottle of lispro lying on the nightstand. She explains that his wife of 50 years passed away six months ago and that he had no children or family. He had become extremely depressed and did not want to live anymore. Which of the following values would be most consistent with his blood chemistries if a blood sample was taken?","o":["A. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and high C-peptide levels","B. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and normal C-peptide levels","C. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and absent C-peptide levels","D. Glucose: 95 mg/dL, low insulin and low C-peptide levels","E. Glucose: 95 mg/dL, high insulin and C-peptide levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and absent C-peptide levels","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and absent C-peptide levels.</b> He injected a large dose of lispro, which is a rapid-acting insulin made in a laboratory. Work out the three values one at a time.</p><ul><li><b>Glucose is very low (25 mg/dL):</b> the insulin overdose drove glucose out of the blood into the tissues. Glucose this low stops the brain from working, and that is the cause of death.</li><li><b>Insulin is high:</b> it is the injected insulin.</li><li><b>C-peptide is absent:</b> there are two reasons. Insulin from a vial never contains C-peptide. And he has type 1 diabetes, in which the β cells have been destroyed, so his own pancreas makes no insulin and no C-peptide at all.</li></ul><p><b>Where C-peptide comes from.</b> The β cell makes insulin as a single strand called proinsulin. Proteases cut the middle piece, the C-peptide (connecting peptide), out of the strand. Each proinsulin gives one insulin and one C-peptide, and the two are stored and released together in equal amounts. So C-peptide appears in the blood only when the body's own β cells secrete.</p><p><b>The rule for any patient with low glucose and high insulin.</b></p><ul><li><b>High C-peptide:</b> the patient's own β cells are secreting too much. The causes are an insulinoma (an insulin-secreting tumor) or a sulfonylurea drug, which forces β cells to release insulin.</li><li><b>Low or absent C-peptide:</b> the insulin came from a syringe. Even in a person with a healthy pancreas, injected insulin lowers the C-peptide, because the low glucose it causes shuts off the person's own β cells.</li></ul><p><b>Two more uses of C-peptide.</b> Insulin is removed from the blood within minutes (half-life 4 to 6 minutes, mostly by the liver enzyme insulinase), while C-peptide is cleared more slowly. That makes C-peptide the better measure of how much insulin the pancreas is producing. It is also used to check how much β-cell function a person with type 1 diabetes has left.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":34,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":17,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":11,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":15,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":20,"f":"F2030"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":3,"f":"F0997"}],"tier":1,"n":"18"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":9,"label":"Insulinoma: high insulin and high C-peptide","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-15.jpg","cap":"Insulinoma: unregulated insulin secretion, hypoglycemia, elevated C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 15"},{"src":"../sl/G029-34.jpg","cap":"Fasting hypoglycemia causes include insulinoma (increased glucose use)","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-16-1","s":"Which of the following would be a most likely observed in a patient recently diagnosed with insulinoma?","o":["A. Decreased body weight","B. Decreased insulin in the blood","C. Decreased C-peptide in the blood","D. Hypoglycemia","E. Hyperglycemia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypoglycemia","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Hypoglycemia.</b> An insulinoma is a tumor of the insulin-making β cells of the pancreas (a neuroendocrine tumor). Normal β cells stop secreting when blood glucose falls. The tumor does not obey that signal. It keeps releasing insulin, so blood glucose keeps falling. The result is <b>hypoglycemia</b>, which is typically worst during fasting, when no food is coming in to make up for it.</p><p><b>The full lab picture.</b></p><ul><li><b>Glucose:</b> low.</li><li><b>Insulin:</b> high.</li><li><b>C-peptide:</b> high. The tumor makes insulin the normal way, by cutting proinsulin into insulin plus C-peptide, so both rise together.</li></ul><p><b>Body weight tends to rise.</b> Insulin is the storage hormone. It moves glucose into cells and builds fat, and patients also eat often to relieve their symptoms.</p><p><b>How it differs from other causes of high insulin with low glucose.</b> Injected insulin gives low glucose and high insulin but a <b>low</b> C-peptide, because insulin from a vial has no C-peptide. A sulfonylurea drug makes the patient's own β cells secrete, so it gives a high C-peptide just as an insulinoma does.</p><p><b>What hypoglycemia looks like.</b> There are two groups of symptoms. Adrenergic symptoms come from the epinephrine surge that tries to push glucose back up: anxiety, palpitations, tremor and sweating. Neuroglycopenic symptoms come from the brain running short of glucose: headache, confusion, slurred speech, seizures and coma. Fasting hypoglycemia, as in an insulinoma, is the rare and serious kind, and it mainly shows the brain symptoms.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 34 (Types of Hypoglycemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":16,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"19"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":10,"label":"Sulfonylureas close beta-cell K-ATP channels","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Sulfonylureas block the beta-cell K-ATP channel, causing depolarization and insulin release","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G029-13.jpg","cap":"Tolbutamide and glibenclamide (sulfonylureas) listed as initiators of insulin secretion","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0088-11-1","s":"A 57-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, presents to doctor's office for routine screening. His serum glucose is 260 mg/dL. His doctor decided to start the patient on a medication that triggers insulin release. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["A. Closes potassium channels in β-cells","B. Increases insulin sensitivity in myocytes","C. Inhibits intestinal brush border enzymes","D. Delays gastric emptying","E. Decreases ATP generation in pancreas"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Closes potassium channels in β-cells","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Closes potassium channels in β-cells.</b> A drug for type 2 diabetes that \"triggers insulin release\" is a <b>sulfonylurea</b> (for example tolbutamide or glyburide, also called glibenclamide). It binds the potassium channel on the β cell and closes it. Closing that channel is the same switch that glucose itself uses to make the β cell release insulin.</p><p><b>How a β cell normally turns high glucose into insulin release.</b></p><ol><li><b>Entry.</b> Glucose enters through GLUT2 (glucose transporter 2). GLUT2 has a high capacity, so the glucose level inside the cell follows the level in the blood.</li><li><b>Trapping.</b> The enzyme glucokinase adds a phosphate to make glucose 6-phosphate. Glucokinase is not slowed by its own product, so it works faster the higher blood glucose goes. This makes it the cell's glucose sensor.</li><li><b>Burning.</b> Glycolysis and the mitochondria burn the glucose. ATP (adenosine triphosphate) rises and ADP (adenosine diphosphate) falls.</li><li><b>The switch.</b> The membrane has ATP-sensitive potassium channels, called K-ATP channels. At rest they are open, potassium leaks out, and the inside of the cell stays negative. A high ATP-to-ADP ratio <b>closes</b> them.</li><li><b>Depolarization.</b> With potassium trapped inside, the inside of the cell becomes less negative.</li><li><b>Calcium entry.</b> Depolarization opens voltage-gated calcium channels, and calcium flows in.</li><li><b>Release.</b> Calcium makes the storage granules fuse with the membrane. Insulin and C-peptide are released together.</li></ol><p><b>Where the drug acts.</b> Each K-ATP channel is a pore (Kir6.2) surrounded by a protein called SUR1, the sulfonylurea receptor. The drug binds SUR1 and closes the channel directly. It skips steps 1 to 3, so insulin comes out even without a rise in glucose. Two things follow. Sulfonylureas need living β cells, so they work in type 2 diabetes and not in type 1. And they raise C-peptide along with insulin, because the β cell releases both. They can also cause hypoglycemia.</p><p><b>What else controls insulin release.</b></p><ul><li><b>Initiators</b> can start release on their own: glucose (the most important), other fuels that raise ATP such as the amino acid leucine, and the sulfonylureas.</li><li><b>Potentiators</b> only amplify a release that glucose has started: acetylcholine from the vagus nerve, the amino acid arginine, glucagon, and the gut hormones called incretins: GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide 1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide).</li><li><b>Inhibitors:</b> somatostatin, and epinephrine acting on α2-adrenergic receptors. This is why stress (infection, low oxygen, exercise) shuts insulin off and keeps fuel in the blood.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":31,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":11,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":10,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":10,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":13,"f":"F2030"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":1,"f":"F0997"}],"tier":1,"n":"20"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":11,"label":"Insulin raises glycogen, fat, protein synthesis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Insulin arrows up: glucose uptake, glycogen, protein and fat synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G029-19.jpg","cap":"Insulin effects list: glucose uptake, glycogen, fatty acid/TAG, protein synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-20-1","s":"Which of the following processes is expected to increase as a metabolic consequence of insulin?","o":["A. Lipolysis","B. Gluconeogenesis","C. Glycogenesis","D. Ketogenesis","E. Protein degradation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glycogenesis","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Glycogenesis.</b> Insulin is the hormone of the fed state, and it is <b>anabolic</b>: it builds and stores. Glycogenesis means making glycogen from glucose, and it is the only storage process on the list. The other four (lipolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis and protein degradation) all break stores down or release fuel, and insulin turns every one of them down.</p><p><b>A way to decode the names.</b> \"-genesis\" means making and \"-lysis\" means breaking down. Glycogenesis makes glycogen. Glycogenolysis breaks glycogen down. Gluconeogenesis makes new glucose from non-sugars. Lipolysis breaks down stored fat. Ketogenesis makes ketone bodies. Be careful: gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis are \"making\" words, but what they make is fuel released into the blood during fasting, so insulin lowers them.</p><p><b>Everything insulin does, by fuel.</b></p><ul><li><b>Carbohydrate:</b> more glucose uptake into muscle and fat, and more glycogen synthesis in liver and muscle. Less liver glycogen breakdown and less gluconeogenesis. In the liver, insulin raises a signal molecule called fructose 2,6-bisphosphate, which switches on phosphofructokinase-1, the key enzyme of glycolysis. So glucose is burned instead of made.</li><li><b>Fat:</b> more fatty acid and triacylglycerol (stored fat) synthesis. Insulin switches off hormone-sensitive lipase, the enzyme that releases fat from fat cells. So lipolysis falls, and because ketone bodies are made from released fat, ketogenesis falls too.</li><li><b>Protein:</b> more amino acid uptake and protein synthesis, and less protein breakdown.</li></ul><p><b>What the liver does with extra glucose after a meal.</b> Once glycogen is full, the liver turns surplus glucose into fat. Glucose becomes pyruvate and then acetyl CoA. Acetyl CoA is carried out of the mitochondria as citrate. In the cytosol, the enzyme acetyl CoA carboxylase makes malonyl CoA, the first building block of a fatty acid. Malonyl CoA also blocks fatty acids from entering the mitochondria, so fat is not burned while it is being made.</p><p><b>When insulin is missing.</b> Lack of insulin is diabetes mellitus. In type 1 the β cells are destroyed. In type 2 the tissues resist insulin. All of the processes above then run in reverse.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":20,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"21"},{"id":"G105-6-1","s":"A 9-year old girl, a known type 1 diabetic, was brought to the emergency room in a state of coma. Her breath has a fruity odor. Her blood glucose is 450 mg/dL. The attending physician administers insulin and potassium chloride. What is the most likely effect of insulin?","o":["A. Hepatic gluconeogenesis","B. Fatty acid release from adipocytes","C. Increased glucose transport into skeletal muscle","D. Brain ketone body utilization","E. Hepatic glyogenolysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased glucose transport into skeletal muscle","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Increased glucose transport into skeletal muscle.</b> A child with type 1 diabetes who is in a coma, with fruity breath and a blood glucose of 450 mg/dL, has diabetic ketoacidosis. Her body has <b>no insulin</b>. The insulin she is given makes muscle and fat cells move a glucose transporter called <b>GLUT4</b> to their surface, so glucose leaves the blood and enters the cells. This is insulin's fastest and most direct effect.</p><p><b>GLUT4 is the transporter that needs insulin.</b> It is found in skeletal muscle, heart and fat. Without insulin it sits inside the cell in storage vesicles. Insulin moves it to the cell surface within seconds. The liver, the red blood cells and the brain take up glucose without insulin, through other transporters.</p><p><b>How the signal travels from insulin to GLUT4.</b></p><ol><li>Insulin binds the <b>insulin receptor</b>, which is a receptor tyrosine kinase. The two outer (α) subunits bind insulin. The two (β) subunits that cross the membrane are enzymes that attach phosphate groups to tyrosines.</li><li>Binding switches the enzyme on, and the two β subunits phosphorylate each other. This is called autophosphorylation.</li><li>The receptor then phosphorylates docking proteins called <b>IRS</b> (insulin receptor substrate).</li><li>IRS activates <b>PI 3-kinase</b> (phosphoinositide 3-kinase), which converts the membrane lipid PIP2 into PIP3.</li><li>PIP3 brings in a kinase called PDK1, which activates <b>protein kinase B</b> (also called Akt).</li><li>Protein kinase B sends GLUT4 to the surface and changes the activity of metabolic enzymes.</li></ol><p>That is the fast, metabolic branch. A slower growth branch runs from IRS through Ras and MAP kinase (mitogen-activated protein kinase) and switches on genes.</p><p><b>Insulin's other effects all go the opposite way from the wrong choices.</b> Insulin turns <b>off</b> liver gluconeogenesis (making new glucose), liver glycogenolysis (glycogen breakdown) and the release of fatty acids from fat cells. With less fat released, the liver stops making ketone bodies, so the ketoacidosis clears.</p><p><b>Why potassium chloride is given with the insulin.</b> Insulin also drives potassium from the blood into cells. Giving insulin alone could make the blood potassium fall dangerously low, so potassium is given along with it.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"22"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":11,"label":"Ketoacidosis: acetone gives fruity breath","slides":[{"src":"../sl/F0917-37.jpg","cap":"Acetone from ketone bodies leaves through the lungs, giving breath a fruity odor","where":", slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"G119-1-1","s":"An 11-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because he was found to have severe abdominal pain and vomiting in school. On presentation, he is found to be lethargic and difficult to arouse. His parents noticed that he was eating and drinking more over the last month; however, they attributed the changes to entering a growth spurt. Physical exam reveals deep and rapid breathing as well as a fruity odor on his breath. His urine tested positive for a reducing sugar. Which of the following lab results would most likely be seen in this patient?","o":["A. Low vasopressin in blood","B. Elevated C-peptide levels","C. Normal anion gap","D. Elevated acetoacetate in urine","E. Low blood glucose"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Elevated acetoacetate in urine","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Elevated acetoacetate in urine.</b> This boy has new type 1 diabetes that has reached <b>diabetic ketoacidosis</b>. His β cells have been destroyed, so he has no insulin. Without insulin the liver makes large amounts of ketone bodies, and they spill into the urine. Acetoacetate is one of the ketone bodies.</p><p><b>How the lack of insulin produces ketone bodies.</b></p><ol><li>Insulin normally keeps hormone-sensitive lipase, the fat-releasing enzyme of fat cells, switched off. With no insulin the enzyme runs freely, and fat cells flood the blood with fatty acids.</li><li>The liver takes up the fatty acids and burns them by β-oxidation, which produces a great deal of acetyl CoA.</li><li>The liver turns the excess acetyl CoA into <b>ketone bodies</b>: acetoacetate, β-hydroxybutyrate and a little acetone.</li><li>Acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate are acids. As they build up, the blood becomes acidic. This is ketoacidosis.</li></ol><p><b>Each clue in the story explained.</b></p><ul><li><b>Eating and drinking more for a month:</b> blood glucose is high because, without insulin, muscle and fat cannot take glucose up and the liver keeps releasing it. The extra glucose spills into the urine and drags water with it, so he loses water and is thirsty. His cells are starved of glucose, so he is hungry.</li><li><b>Reducing sugar in the urine:</b> this is glucose.</li><li><b>Deep and rapid breathing</b> (Kussmaul breathing): the lungs blow off carbon dioxide to make up for the acid in the blood.</li><li><b>Fruity breath:</b> acetone, which leaves through the lungs.</li><li><b>Abdominal pain, vomiting and drowsiness:</b> typical of ketoacidosis.</li></ul><p><b>The other lab values you should expect.</b> Blood glucose is high. C-peptide is low, because C-peptide is released together with the body's own insulin and he makes none. The anion gap is high. (The anion gap is sodium minus chloride and bicarbonate. It widens when extra acids such as ketone bodies are in the blood.) Vasopressin, the water-saving hormone, is raised and not lowered, because he is dehydrated.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 34 (Types of Hypoglycemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":1,"f":"G119"}],"tier":1,"n":"23"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":12,"label":"Glucagon catabolic: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"Glucagon → cAMP → PKA in liver: glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis to glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Glucagon side: increased glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis, lipolysis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Glucagon effects list: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, ketogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-30-1","s":"As a catabolic hormone, which of the following is an expected metabolic consequence of glucagon?","o":["A. ↑ Lipogenesis","B. ↑ Glycolysis","C. ↑ Glycogenolysis","D. ↓ Ketogenesis","E. ↓ Gluconeogenesis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"↑ Glycogenolysis","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. ↑ Glycogenolysis.</b> Glucagon is the hormone of fasting. It is <b>catabolic</b>, which means it breaks stores down to put fuel into the blood. Glycogenolysis is the breakdown of liver glycogen to glucose, and it is glucagon's fastest way to raise blood glucose.</p><p><b>What glucagon is.</b> Glucagon is made by the α (alpha) cells of the pancreatic islets. It is a single chain of 29 amino acids, and it is cut from a larger precursor, just as insulin is. Its main target is the <b>liver</b>. Skeletal muscle has no glucagon receptors, so glucagon does nothing to muscle glycogen.</p><p><b>What turns glucagon release up and down.</b></p><ul><li><b>Up:</b> low blood glucose (the main signal), amino acids such as arginine, and epinephrine or norepinephrine.</li><li><b>Down:</b> glucose and insulin.</li></ul><p>Compare with insulin. Glucose raises insulin and lowers glucagon. Amino acids raise both: after a pure protein meal the insulin would pull glucose down, and the glucagon prevents that. Epinephrine lowers insulin and raises glucagon, so both changes push fuel into the blood.</p><p><b>Everything glucagon does, by fuel.</b></p><ul><li><b>Carbohydrate:</b> more liver glycogen breakdown and more gluconeogenesis (making new glucose). Less glycogen synthesis and less glycolysis.</li><li><b>Fat:</b> more lipolysis (fat release from fat cells), and more fatty acid burning and ketone body production in the liver. Fat synthesis (lipogenesis) falls.</li><li><b>Protein:</b> the liver takes up more amino acids to use for gluconeogenesis.</li></ul><p>So with glucagon, glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis all go up, and lipogenesis and glycolysis go down. It is the mirror image of insulin.</p><p><b>The fasting timeline.</b> Liver glycogen supplies blood glucose for about the first 10 to 18 hours of a fast. Gluconeogenesis then takes over. In prolonged starvation the brain burns ketone bodies for much of its fuel, which spares glucose and muscle protein. The liver makes ketone bodies but cannot use them itself. Red blood cells have no mitochondria, so they can only use glucose.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":30,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"24"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":13,"label":"Glucagon catabolic: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"Glucagon → cAMP → PKA in liver: glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis to glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Glucagon side: increased glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis, lipolysis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"../sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Glucagon effects list: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, ketogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F0088-3-1","s":"Which of the following is a consequence of glucagon binding to its receptor?","o":["A. Increase in muscle glycogenolysis","B. Activation of guanylate cyclase and protein kinase B","C. Activation of liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis","D. Inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue","E. Increase in glycolysis and ATP generation in pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Activation of liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.</b> Glucagon is released when blood glucose is low, and its receptor is on <b>liver</b> cells. Binding switches on the liver's two ways of putting glucose into the blood: glycogenolysis (breaking down glycogen) and gluconeogenesis (making new glucose).</p><p><b>The signal inside the liver cell.</b></p><ol><li>Glucagon binds a <b>G protein–coupled receptor</b>. Its G protein is the stimulatory kind, called Gs.</li><li>The α subunit of Gs lets go of GDP (guanosine diphosphate) and binds GTP (guanosine triphosphate). That switches it on, and it then switches on the enzyme <b>adenylyl cyclase</b> (also called adenylate cyclase).</li><li>Adenylyl cyclase turns ATP into <b>cAMP</b> (cyclic adenosine monophosphate), the second messenger.</li><li>cAMP activates <b>protein kinase A</b> (PKA), an enzyme that adds phosphate groups to other enzymes.</li></ol><p><b>What protein kinase A phosphorylates.</b></p><ul><li><b>Glycogen enzymes.</b> It activates phosphorylase kinase, which activates glycogen phosphorylase, the enzyme that breaks glycogen down. Phosphorylation also switches off glycogen synthase. So glycogen is broken down and not rebuilt.</li><li><b>The glycolysis and gluconeogenesis switch.</b> It phosphorylates a two-part enzyme, so the level of the signal molecule fructose 2,6-bisphosphate falls. That molecule normally speeds glycolysis and holds back gluconeogenesis. When it falls, glycolysis slows and gluconeogenesis speeds up.</li><li><b>Pyruvate kinase.</b> It switches off this last enzyme of glycolysis in the liver, so phosphoenolpyruvate is not wasted and flows up toward glucose.</li></ul><p>Both routes end at glucose 6-phosphate. The liver enzyme glucose 6-phosphatase removes the phosphate, and free glucose leaves for the blood.</p><p><b>Facts that sort out the look-alike statements.</b> Muscle has no glucagon receptors and no glucose 6-phosphatase. Muscle glycogen breakdown is driven by epinephrine, which uses the same cAMP cascade. Glucagon's enzyme is adenylyl cyclase, which makes cAMP. It is not guanylate cyclase. Protein kinase B belongs to insulin's pathway. In fat cells, glucagon's signal activates hormone-sensitive lipase, so fat is released. And a rise in glycolysis and ATP inside the pancreatic β cell is the signal for insulin release, which is the opposite situation.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 29 (Signaling by Glucagon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":44,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":3,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":8,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":2,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":3,"f":"F2030"}],"tier":1,"n":"25"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":14,"label":"Newborn of a diabetic mother → hypoglycemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-4.jpg","cap":"High blood glucose makes β cells release insulin, which pushes glucose into cells","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 4"},{"src":"../sl/G029-37.jpg","cap":"Low glucose in an infant: poor feeding, irritability, lethargy, low muscle tone","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"G105-9-1","s":"A newborn whose mother had uncontrolled diabetes mellitus during pregnancy is likely to have which of the following findings?","o":["A. Atrophy of pancreatic islets cells","B. Hypoglycemia","C. Hyperglycemia","D. Amyloid deposits in pancreatic islets","E. Ketoacidosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypoglycemia","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Hypoglycemia.</b> Follow the glucose and the insulin separately. The mother's high blood glucose crosses the placenta. Her insulin does not. So all through pregnancy the fetus is bathed in extra glucose, and its own β cells enlarge and release <b>extra insulin</b> to handle it. At birth the cord is cut and the flood of glucose stops at once, but the baby's insulin is still high. The high insulin keeps pulling glucose out of the blood, so the newborn becomes <b>hypoglycemic</b>.</p><p><b>What hypoglycemia is.</b> It is a plasma glucose low enough to cause symptoms, usually below about 50 mg/dL, with symptoms that go away when glucose is given.</p><p><b>How it looks in a baby and in an adult.</b></p><ul><li><b>Infants and children:</b> poor feeding, irritability, lethargy, floppiness (hypotonia), fast breathing, a blue color (cyanosis) and failure to thrive.</li><li><b>Adults:</b> anxiety, palpitations, sweating, shakiness, pallor, confusion, blurred vision and lethargy.</li></ul><p><b>The two groups of symptoms.</b> Adrenergic symptoms come from the epinephrine surge and are typical of a sudden fall: anxiety, palpitations, tremor and sweating. Neuroglycopenic symptoms come from too little glucose reaching the brain and are typical of a gradual fall: headache, confusion, slurred speech, seizures, coma and death.</p><p><b>How the body normally defends blood glucose.</b> As glucose falls, the defenses come in order. Insulin release stops first (around 80 to 85 mg/dL). Then epinephrine and glucagon rise (65 to 70). Then growth hormone and cortisol (about 60). Symptoms begin only below that (adrenergic at about 55, brain symptoms at about 50). Glucagon and epinephrine act within minutes. Cortisol and growth hormone act over hours. In this newborn the defenses are overpowered because insulin stays high.</p><p><b>The three types of hypoglycemia.</b></p><ul><li><b>Insulin-induced:</b> the most common. A person with diabetes takes insulin and then misses a meal, or takes too much. It is treated with oral carbohydrate. If the person is unconscious, give intravenous glucose, or injected glucagon when there is no intravenous line.</li><li><b>Postprandial (reactive):</b> an exaggerated insulin release after a meal. It is mild and is managed with frequent small meals.</li><li><b>Fasting:</b> rare and serious. Either the liver makes too little glucose (liver disease, alcohol) or the body uses too much (sepsis, an insulin-secreting tumor).</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 32 (Plasma glucose level low enough to cause signs and symptoms of altered mental st)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Macronutrients Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G105"}],"tier":1,"n":"26"}]},{"l":"BIO-C3","u":14,"label":"Alcohol raises NADH, blocks gluconeogenesis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G029-35.jpg","cap":"Ethanol's NADH diverts pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate; less glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"G029-36-1","s":"An increase in which of the following ratios best explains the hypoglycemia that is observed after excessive ethanol consumption?","o":["A. ATP/AMP","B. FADH2/FAD","C. Glucagon/Insulin","D. NADH/NAD+","E. Glucose/ Glucose 6-P"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"NADH/NAD+","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. NADH/NAD+.</b> The liver breaks down ethanol in two steps, and each step turns one NAD+ into one NADH. (NAD+ is nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, a carrier that picks up electrons. NADH is the same carrier loaded with electrons.) Heavy drinking floods the liver with NADH, so the <b>NADH-to-NAD+ ratio</b> rises sharply. That high ratio shuts down gluconeogenesis, the liver's pathway for making new glucose, and blood glucose falls.</p><p><b>The two steps of ethanol breakdown.</b></p><ol><li><b>Alcohol dehydrogenase</b> turns ethanol into acetaldehyde, and makes one NADH.</li><li><b>Aldehyde dehydrogenase</b> turns acetaldehyde into acetate, and makes one more NADH.</li></ol><p><b>How a high NADH ratio blocks gluconeogenesis.</b> Gluconeogenesis begins with pyruvate, which is turned into oxaloacetate, then phosphoenolpyruvate, and on up to glucose. A large excess of NADH pushes two reversible reactions the wrong way:</p><ul><li>Pyruvate is reduced to <b>lactate</b>.</li><li>Oxaloacetate is reduced to <b>malate</b>.</li></ul><p>Pyruvate and oxaloacetate are the two starting materials of gluconeogenesis. When they are drained away, the pathway has nothing to work with. Other starting materials are blocked as well: lactate and glycerol cannot enter the pathway, because converting them needs free NAD+, and the NAD+ has been used up.</p><p><b>Why it shows up in a person who drinks without eating.</b> While liver glycogen lasts, glycogen breakdown still supplies glucose. After hours without food, glycogen is gone. Now the person depends on gluconeogenesis, and alcohol has blocked it. With neither source, glucose drops. This is a form of fasting hypoglycemia, the kind caused by too little glucose production.</p><p><b>Why it is not the other ratios.</b> Neither enzyme uses FAD, so FADH2 is not involved. A high glucagon-to-insulin ratio is the body's normal defense and would raise glucose. ATP to AMP reflects the cell's energy level, not its electron load.</p><p><b>A related drug.</b> Disulfiram blocks aldehyde dehydrogenase, the second step. Acetaldehyde builds up and causes flushing and nausea, which discourages drinking.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Insulin and glucagon lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Henshaw): question slides","sl":36,"f":"G029"}],"tier":1,"n":"27"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":15,"label":"Blocked bile → fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (night blindness)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-6.jpg","cap":"Vitamins A, D, E, K enter with fat and need bile salt micelles","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"},{"src":"../sl/G030-15.jpg","cap":"Vitamin A deficiency: night blindness, dry eye, Bitot spots, keratomalacia","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-4-1","s":"A person with persistent post-hepatic jaundice is at a greater risk of suffering from which of the following conditions as well?","o":["A. Scurvy","B. Nightblindness","C. Pellagra","D. Megaloblastic anemia","E. Wilson's disease"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Nightblindness","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Nightblindness.</b> Post-hepatic jaundice means the blockage is after the liver, in the bile duct. Bile cannot reach the intestine. Bile salts are needed to absorb fat and the four <b>fat-soluble vitamins: A, D, E and K</b>. So a long-lasting blockage leads to a lack of these vitamins. Night blindness is the earliest sign of <b>vitamin A</b> deficiency, and it is the only condition on the list that comes from a fat-soluble vitamin.</p><p><b>How fat-soluble vitamins are absorbed.</b> They travel with dietary fat, and every step is needed.</p><ol><li>Bile salts break the fat into tiny droplets (they emulsify it).</li><li>Pancreatic lipase digests the fat.</li><li>Bile salts form <b>micelles</b>, small carriers that ferry the fat products and the vitamins to the intestinal cell.</li><li>The intestinal cell packs them into chylomicrons, which leave through the lymph.</li></ol><p>Break any step and all four vitamins are lost in the stool. The three classic causes are blocked bile flow (no micelles), pancreatic insufficiency (too little lipase) and inflammatory bowel disease (a damaged lining).</p><p><b>Why low vitamin A causes night blindness.</b> The rod cells of the retina see in dim light using a pigment called rhodopsin. Rhodopsin is the protein opsin holding a form of vitamin A called 11-cis retinal. Light straightens the retinal, it leaves the opsin, and that fires the visual signal. The retinal must then be rebuilt. With too little vitamin A there is too little rhodopsin, and dim light is no longer enough to see by.</p><p><b>What the other three missing vitamins would cause.</b> Low vitamin D gives soft bones. Low vitamin K gives bleeding, because clotting factors are not activated. Low vitamin E gives fragile red blood cells and nerve problems.</p><p><b>How the two vitamin families differ.</b></p><ul><li><b>Absorption:</b> fat-soluble vitamins need fat and bile. Water-soluble vitamins (vitamin C and the B vitamins) do not.</li><li><b>Storage:</b> fat-soluble vitamins are not easily excreted. They build up in the liver and in fat, so A and D can reach toxic levels. Water-soluble vitamins are passed in the urine and are rarely toxic.</li><li><b>Action:</b> water-soluble vitamins mostly work as coenzymes, the small helpers that enzymes need. Among the fat-soluble ones only K is a coenzyme. A and D act as hormones, and E is an antioxidant.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"28"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":16,"label":"APL: ATRA restores promyelocyte differentiation","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-14.jpg","cap":"PML-RARα blocks differentiation; ATRA swaps corepressors for coactivators, cells mature","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"G030-17-1","s":"Several clinical trials have suggested that retinoic acid can induce remission in patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia. Such remission is related to the ability of retinoic acid to promote which of the following?","o":["A. Differentiation of promyelocytes","B. Proliferation of promyelocytes","C. Programed cell death","D. Production of interferon","E. Repair of DNA damage"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Differentiation of promyelocytes","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Differentiation of promyelocytes.</b> Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) is a blood cancer in which immature white blood cells called promyelocytes pile up because they <b>cannot mature</b>. The disease is a block in differentiation. Retinoic acid lifts the block. The promyelocytes then mature into normal white cells, live out a normal life span and die off, and the leukemia goes into remission.</p><p><b>Why the cells are stuck.</b> In APL, pieces of chromosomes 15 and 17 have swapped places. This joins two genes and creates an abnormal fusion protein, PML-RARα. Part of it is the retinoic acid receptor. The fusion protein sits on the genes that a promyelocyte needs in order to mature and keeps them switched off. A high dose of all-trans retinoic acid binds the fusion protein and makes it let go, so the genes switch on.</p><p><b>The three forms of vitamin A and their jobs.</b> Vitamin A is a family of molecules that differ only at the end of the chain.</p><ul><li><b>Retinal</b> (an aldehyde): vision. It is the light-catching part of the visual pigment.</li><li><b>Retinoic acid</b> (an acid): growth and the differentiation of epithelial cells. It works as a hormone.</li><li><b>Retinol</b> (an alcohol), with retinal: reproduction.</li></ul><p>Retinol and retinal can be converted back and forth. Retinal to retinoic acid is one-way.</p><p><b>How retinoic acid switches genes on.</b> It enters the nucleus and binds the retinoic acid receptor (RAR). RAR pairs with a partner receptor called RXR. The pair sits on specific DNA sequences called retinoic acid response elements and turns on the genes next to them. Steroid hormones, thyroid hormone and vitamin D work the same way. A classic target is the keratin gene, which retinoic acid holds in check. Too little vitamin A lets keratin build up, which gives a dry, horny eye surface. Too much suppresses it, which gives dry, itchy skin.</p><p><b>Retinoic acid drugs.</b> All-trans retinoic acid (tretinoin) is put on the skin for acne and psoriasis and taken by mouth for APL. 13-cis retinoic acid (isotretinoin) is taken by mouth for severe cystic acne. Isotretinoin causes birth defects, so it must never be used in pregnancy.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 12 (Retinoic Acid Is A Nuclear Hormone)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Fat-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":17,"f":"G030"}],"tier":1,"n":"29"},{"id":"G090-8-1","s":"A 28-year-old woman presents with fatigue, gingival bleeding, and easy bruising. Laboratory studies show anemia, thrombocytopenia, and prolonged coagulation times. A peripheral blood smear demonstrates abnormal promyelocytes containing multiple Auer rods. Genetic testing identifies a PML–RARα fusion protein. Treatment with all-trans retinoic acid promotes myeloid maturation through which molecular mechanism?","o":["A. Increased recruitment of corepressors to RAREs","B. Release of corepressors and recruitment of coactivators by PML–RARα","C. Activation of a membrane G protein-coupled receptor","D. Inhibition of DNA polymerase in promyelocytes","E. Activation of a receptor tyrosine kinase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Release of corepressors and recruitment of coactivators by PML–RARα","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Release of corepressors and recruitment of coactivators by PML–RARα.</b> She has acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). The fusion protein PML-RARα holds <b>corepressors</b> on the DNA, and these keep the maturation genes switched off. A high dose of all-trans retinoic acid binds the RARα part of the fusion protein. The corepressors fall off, <b>coactivators</b> bind in their place, and the genes switch on. The promyelocytes can then mature.</p><p><b>The normal system first.</b> Retinoic acid, a form of vitamin A, works inside the nucleus. It binds the retinoic acid receptor (RAR). RAR pairs with a partner receptor, RXR, and the pair sits on DNA sequences called retinoic acid response elements (RAREs). When retinoic acid is bound, the receptor recruits coactivators and the gene is read. So this is a nuclear receptor that acts directly on genes. It is not a receptor on the cell membrane.</p><p><b>What goes wrong in APL.</b></p><ol><li>Chromosomes 15 and 17 exchange pieces. This is the translocation t(15;17).</li><li>The exchange joins the PML gene (promyelocytic leukemia gene) to the RARα gene (retinoic acid receptor alpha). The cell makes the fusion protein <b>PML-RARα</b>.</li><li>The fusion protein still binds the RAREs, but it grips corepressors very tightly. The normal, low level of retinoic acid in the body cannot make it let go.</li><li>The genes needed for maturation stay off. White cell development stops at the promyelocyte stage, and promyelocytes pile up in the marrow and blood.</li></ol><p><b>How the drug fixes it.</b> A high dose of all-trans retinoic acid is enough to bind the fusion protein and change its shape. Corepressors leave and coactivators arrive. The cells differentiate into mature white cells. The drug does not kill the cells directly, and it does not repair the translocation. It was one of the first cancer drugs aimed at the abnormal protein itself.</p><p><b>Reading the case.</b> Promyelocytes packed with Auer rods (needle-shaped granules) are typical of APL. The marrow is crowded out, so she has anemia (fatigue) and a low platelet count. APL also sets off widespread clotting that uses up the clotting factors, so her clotting times are long. Both problems explain the bleeding gums and easy bruising.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 12 (Retinoic Acid Is A Nuclear Hormone)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":8,"f":"G090"}],"tier":1,"n":"30"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":17,"label":"Bitot spots, night blindness → vitamin A deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-15.jpg","cap":"Xerophthalmia photos: xerosis, night blindness, Bitot spots, keratomalacia","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-21-1","s":"A 45-year old male patient complains of dry sensation in his eyes. PE indicated grey patches on the sclerae. During questioning, he was bragging about his 60-lb. body weight loss following a very strict fat-free diet. Which of the following vitamins could be deficient in this patient leading to the symptoms described above?","o":["A. Tocopherol","B. Calcitriol","C. Ascorbic acid","D. Retinol","E. Phylloquinone"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Retinol","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Retinol.</b> Retinol is <b>vitamin A</b>. A dry feeling in the eyes plus gray patches on the white of the eye is the eye disease of vitamin A deficiency. The cause is his diet. Vitamin A is fat-soluble, so it is absorbed only together with dietary fat. On a strict fat-free diet he eats almost no vitamin A, and he cannot absorb the little he does eat.</p><p><b>Know the chemical names.</b> Retinol is vitamin A. Calcitriol is active vitamin D. Tocopherol is vitamin E. Phylloquinone is vitamin K. Ascorbic acid is vitamin C. The first four are the fat-soluble vitamins.</p><p><b>Why the eye dries out.</b> One form of vitamin A, retinoic acid, keeps the keratin gene held down in moist surface tissues. Without it, the moist lining of the eye starts to make keratin and becomes dry and horny, like skin. The stages, in order:</p><ol><li><b>Night blindness.</b> This is the earliest sign. Too little retinal means too little of the visual pigment rhodopsin, so dim light is not enough to see by. It is reversible at first.</li><li><b>Xerosis.</b> The conjunctiva (the lining over the white of the eye) loses its shine, dries and wrinkles. The whole dry-eye condition is called xerophthalmia.</li><li><b>Bitot's spots.</b> Foamy gray patches of keratin debris, roughly triangular, on the conjunctiva. These are his gray patches.</li><li><b>Keratomalacia.</b> The cornea softens and clouds over.</li><li><b>Ulcer, scar and blindness.</b></li></ol><p><b>From food to tissues.</b> Animal foods (liver, egg yolk, butter, fish liver oil) supply retinol. Yellow and dark green vegetables supply β-carotene, a plant precursor that the intestine splits into two molecules of retinal. The intestinal cell packs vitamin A into chylomicrons along with fat. The liver stores it, and releases it into the blood on a carrier called retinol-binding protein. That protein is made by the liver, so a malnourished child with low protein can be deficient in vitamin A even while the liver still holds a store.</p><p><b>Too much is also harmful.</b> Because vitamin A is stored and not excreted, chronic excess causes dry itchy skin, inflamed lips, hair loss, headache, nausea and an enlarged liver. It can raise the pressure inside the skull enough to mimic a brain tumor. Most important, excess vitamin A in pregnancy causes birth defects.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":1,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":21,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":1,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":37,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":2,"f":"F2435"}],"tier":1,"n":"31"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":18,"label":"Active vitamin D = calcitriol","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-21.jpg","cap":"Active vitamin D is 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol); D2 plants, D3 animals/skin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Pathway: cholecalciferol → liver 25-hydroxylase → kidney 1-α-hydroxylase → calcitriol","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-1-1","s":"Calcium is one the more abundant elements in the human body and is essential for the mineralization of bone. Which of the following is directly associated with calcium absorption from the small intestine?","o":["A. Ergocalciferol","B. 25-Hydroxycholecalciferol","C. 7-Dehydrocholesterol","D. 1, 25 -Dihydroxycholecalciferol","E. cholecalciferol"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"1, 25 -Dihydroxycholecalciferol","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. 1, 25 -Dihydroxycholecalciferol.</b> This is <b>calcitriol</b>, the active form of vitamin D. It is the only compound on the list that acts on tissues. It works on the cells of the small intestine and makes them produce calbindin, a protein that carries calcium. So more calcium, and more phosphate, is absorbed from food. Every other compound listed is an inactive precursor on the way to calcitriol.</p><p><b>The pathway, with every name in the question.</b></p><ol><li><b>7-Dehydrocholesterol</b> sits in the skin. It is an intermediate of cholesterol synthesis. Ultraviolet light from the sun turns it into cholecalciferol.</li><li><b>Cholecalciferol</b> (vitamin D3) comes from sunlit skin and from animal foods. <b>Ergocalciferol</b> (vitamin D2) comes from plants. Both are inactive.</li><li>The <b>liver</b> adds a hydroxyl group (an –OH) at carbon 25. This makes <b>25-hydroxycholecalciferol</b> (calcidiol). It is still inactive, but it is the main form that circulates in the blood and the storage form.</li><li>The <b>kidney</b> adds a second hydroxyl at carbon 1, using the enzyme 1α-hydroxylase. This makes <b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> (calcitriol), the active hormone.</li></ol><p>The name tells you the number of hydroxyl groups. \"Dihydroxy\" means two, and only the form with two is active.</p><p><b>What calcitriol does.</b> Its job is to keep blood calcium and phosphate high enough.</p><ul><li><b>Intestine:</b> more uptake of calcium and phosphate. This is its most important action.</li><li><b>Kidney:</b> more calcium is reabsorbed, so less is lost in the urine.</li><li><b>Bone:</b> when blood calcium is low, it works with parathyroid hormone to release calcium from bone.</li></ul><p>Releasing calcium from bone sounds harmful. But by keeping calcium and phosphate in the blood high enough, calcitriol is what allows new bone to be mineralized, so overall it protects bone.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"32"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":18,"label":"Kidney 1-α-hydroxylase makes active vitamin D","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Skin, liver, then kidney 1-α-hydroxylase makes calcitriol; PTH stimulates it","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D039-16.jpg","cap":"Chronic kidney disease lowers renal 1α-hydroxylase, so calcitriol and calcium absorption fall","where":"2026 spring interactive, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-17-1","s":"A 43-year-old man with chronic renal failure, who requires hemodialysis complains of generalized bone pain. Physical examination shows bowing of legs and a sallow complexion. A radiograph of man shows generalized osteopenia. The patient most likely has a deficiency of which of the following vitamin?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin K","C. Vitamin D","D. Vitamin C","E. Vitamin E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Vitamin D.</b> The last step that activates vitamin D happens in the <b>kidney</b>. In chronic renal failure too little working kidney is left, so the active hormone is not made. This is a vitamin D deficiency even if his diet and sunlight are normal. Without active vitamin D the gut absorbs too little calcium, and bone loses its mineral. That explains his bone pain, bowed legs and thin-looking bones on the radiograph (osteopenia).</p><p><b>Why the kidney is essential.</b> Vitamin D from skin or food is inactive. It needs two hydroxyl groups added.</p><ol><li>The <b>liver</b> enzyme 25-hydroxylase makes 25-hydroxycholecalciferol. This is still inactive. It is the main circulating and storage form.</li><li>The <b>kidney</b> enzyme <b>1α-hydroxylase</b> makes 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, called calcitriol. This is the active hormone.</li></ol><p><b>Only the kidney step is controlled.</b> When blood calcium falls, the parathyroid glands release parathyroid hormone (PTH). PTH switches 1α-hydroxylase on. Low phosphate does the same. Calcitriol then raises blood calcium, switches off its own enzyme and lowers PTH. Calcitriol lasts only 2 to 4 hours in the blood, so its level follows the body's current need. The rule to remember: without PTH or without a working kidney, calcitriol stays low no matter how much vitamin D is eaten.</p><p><b>What follows from low calcitriol.</b> The intestine absorbs less calcium and phosphate. Blood calcium tends to fall, so PTH rises and pulls calcium out of bone. New bone cannot be mineralized. In an adult this is osteomalacia, which means soft bones, with bone pain and fractures from minor force. In a child the same defect is rickets.</p><p><b>Every cause of vitamin D deficiency breaks one link.</b> Poor diet or too little sunlight (no starting material). Fat malabsorption (vitamin D is fat-soluble). Liver disease (first hydroxylation lost). Kidney disease (second hydroxylation lost). A patient like this man needs the already-active form, calcitriol, because he cannot activate ordinary vitamin D.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Synthesis of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":15,"f":"G090"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":12,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":17,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":9,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":23,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":6,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"33"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":18,"label":"Sunlight turns skin 7-dehydrocholesterol into vitamin D3","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-23.jpg","cap":"Ultraviolet light turns 7-dehydrocholesterol into D3 in the skin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G030-27-1","s":"Individuals who spend inadequate time in the sunlight, dietary sources of vitamin D are necessary. Which of the following requires sunlight for Vitamin D formation?","o":["A. 7-Dehydrocholestrol","B. 25-Hydroxycholecalciferol","C. Cholecalciferol","D. 1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol","E. Ergocalciferol"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"7-Dehydrocholestrol","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. 7-Dehydrocholestrol.</b> Sunlight acts on one molecule only: <b>7-dehydrocholesterol</b> in the skin. Ultraviolet light breaks open one of its rings and turns it into cholecalciferol (vitamin D3). That is the only step in the whole vitamin D pathway that needs light. A person who gets too little sun cannot do this step, so they must eat their vitamin D.</p><p><b>The trap.</b> Cholecalciferol is the <b>product</b> of the sunlight step. It does not need sunlight itself, and it can also come straight from food. The question asks which compound needs sunlight in order to become vitamin D, and that is the starting material, 7-dehydrocholesterol.</p><p><b>Where each compound comes from.</b></p><ul><li><b>7-Dehydrocholesterol:</b> made in the skin as an intermediate of cholesterol synthesis. Needs ultraviolet light.</li><li><b>Cholecalciferol (D3):</b> from sunlit skin, and from animal foods. It is the most common form in the diet and in supplements.</li><li><b>Ergocalciferol (D2):</b> from plants in the diet.</li><li><b>25-Hydroxycholecalciferol:</b> made in the liver. No light is needed. It is the main form in the blood.</li><li><b>1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol):</b> made in the kidney. No light is needed. It is the active hormone.</li></ul><p><b>Four tissues work together.</b> Skin and intestine supply vitamin D3. The liver adds the first hydroxyl group. The kidney adds the second, and only that kidney step is regulated (parathyroid hormone switches it on when blood calcium is low).</p><p><b>Who becomes deficient for lack of sun.</b> People in northern latitudes in winter, people who stay indoors and people who use sunscreen heavily. The recommended daily intake is 5 micrograms of cholecalciferol, which equals 200 international units (1 microgram = 40 units).</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 23 (Metabolism And Actions of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 28 (Review of Objective 3)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Fat-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":27,"f":"G030"}],"tier":1,"n":"34"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":19,"label":"Vitamin D raises gut calcium and phosphate uptake","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-24.jpg","cap":"Calcitriol raises intestinal calcium uptake, kidney reabsorption and bone mobilization","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/G030-23.jpg","cap":"Calcitriol induces calbindin in intestinal cells to absorb calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-23-1","s":"A 3-year old girl was brought to the clinic for consultation. She is well below the 5th percentile for both height and weight with multiple rachitic deformities of the skeleton to include severe bowing of the tibias bilaterally and pigeon chest. Which of the following can be the reason for her clinical symptoms?","o":["A. Increased reabsorption of calcium from kidney","B. Decreased intestinal uptake of calcium and phosphate","C. Increased intestinal uptake of calcium","D. Increased reabsorption of phosphate from kidney","E. Decreased resorption of calcium by bone"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased intestinal uptake of calcium and phosphate","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Decreased intestinal uptake of calcium and phosphate.</b> Bowed legs, a pigeon chest and poor growth in a young child are <b>rickets</b>, the childhood disease of vitamin D deficiency. The most important job of active vitamin D (calcitriol) is to make the intestine absorb calcium and phosphate. Without it, <b>less calcium and phosphate is taken up from food</b>, and growing bone cannot be mineralized. The soft bones bend under the child's weight.</p><p><b>What else happens in the body.</b></p><ol><li>Gut calcium absorption falls, so blood calcium tends to drop.</li><li>The parathyroid glands respond by releasing more parathyroid hormone (PTH).</li><li>PTH pulls calcium out of bone, so bone breakdown goes <b>up</b>.</li><li>PTH makes the kidney hold on to calcium but <b>lose phosphate</b> in the urine.</li></ol><p>The net result is that calcium and phosphate are both too low to build bone mineral. Notice the directions. In rickets, intestinal uptake is down, bone resorption is up and kidney phosphate reabsorption is down.</p><p><b>Same defect at two ages.</b></p><ul><li><b>Children: rickets.</b> The bones are still growing. They stay soft, growth is poor and the skeleton deforms (bowed legs, pigeon chest).</li><li><b>Adults: osteomalacia</b> (\"soft bones\"). Bone that is already formed loses mineral. The result is bone pain and fractures from minor force.</li></ul><p><b>Causes.</b> Each one breaks a link in the vitamin D pathway: poor diet, fat malabsorption, lack of sunlight, liver disease or kidney disease. Lack of parathyroid hormone is another, because without PTH the kidney never activates vitamin D. That gives low calcium with high phosphate, since PTH is what normally makes the kidney excrete phosphate.</p><p><b>The opposite problem: too much vitamin D.</b> Vitamin D is the most toxic of the fat-soluble vitamins in excess. Too much calcitriol pulls calcium in from gut and bone. Blood and urine calcium rise, which causes loss of appetite, nausea, thirst and stupor, and calcium deposits in the kidneys (stones) and arteries. Sarcoidosis gives the same picture. In sarcoidosis, immune cells in the granulomas have their own 1α-hydroxylase that PTH does not control, so they make calcitriol without limit.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Functions of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":2,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":23,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":3,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":39,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":4,"f":"F2435"}],"tier":1,"n":"35"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":20,"label":"Newborn bleeding: sterile gut, no vitamin K","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-33.jpg","cap":"Newborn deficiency from sterile intestines: hemorrhagic disease of the newborn","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K source: diet and gut bacteria","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"G030-34-1","s":"A newborn does well with breast feeding. Two days later, the mother calls frantically because baby is bleeding from umbilical cord and nostrils. Which of the following vitamins might be deficient?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin D","C. Vitamin E","D. Vitamin C","E. Vitamin K"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Vitamin K.</b> A healthy newborn who starts bleeding from the umbilical cord and the nose on day 2 has <b>vitamin K deficiency</b>. Vitamin K is needed to activate several clotting factors. Without it the blood does not clot properly.</p><p><b>Why newborns run short of vitamin K.</b></p><ul><li>A large share of a person's vitamin K is made by bacteria in the gut. A newborn's intestine is <b>sterile</b>, so nothing is being made yet.</li><li><b>Breast milk</b> contains very little vitamin K.</li><li>It is worse if the mother was deficient herself.</li></ul><p>This is called hemorrhagic disease of the newborn. It is prevented by giving every baby a vitamin K injection at birth.</p><p><b>What vitamin K does.</b> The liver makes four clotting factors as inactive precursors: prothrombin (factor II) and factors VII, IX and X. Vitamin K is the coenzyme for the reaction that finishes them. The reaction adds an extra carboxyl group to certain glutamate residues, which lets the factor grip calcium and attach to the surface of platelets, where clotting takes place. Without vitamin K the factors are made but cannot work. Active prothrombin is low (hypoprothrombinemia), clotting times are long and the baby bleeds.</p><p><b>Forms and sources.</b> Phylloquinone (vitamin K1) comes from plants such as cabbage, cauliflower and spinach. Menaquinones (vitamin K2) are made by gut bacteria. Egg yolk and liver also supply it. The daily need is tiny, 70 to 140 micrograms.</p><p><b>How it differs from other vitamin problems in babies.</b> Vitamin E deficiency also affects infants, but it breaks red blood cells (hemolysis). It does not cause bleeding. Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) causes bleeding from weak blood vessels, but it takes months of poor diet to develop, and the clotting times stay normal.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (The -carboxyglutamates of prothrombin are good chelators of calcium. The prothr)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Fat-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":34,"f":"G030"}],"tier":1,"n":"36"},{"id":"G096-15-1","s":"Twenty-year-old Mely gave birth to a 3.5-kilogram baby boy. To prevent hemorrhagic disease of the newborn, her baby must be given:","o":["A. Folic acid","B. Vitamin A","C. Vitamin C","D. Vitamin K","E. Vitamin D"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Vitamin K.</b> Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn is bleeding caused by <b>vitamin K deficiency</b> in the first days of life. One injection of vitamin K at birth prevents it, and it is given routinely to every baby.</p><p><b>Why every newborn is at risk.</b> The intestine is sterile at birth, so the gut bacteria that normally make vitamin K are not there yet. Breast milk holds little vitamin K. If the mother is deficient, the baby starts with even less.</p><p><b>Why low vitamin K means bleeding.</b> Vitamin K converts four clotting factors from inactive precursors into working factors: prothrombin (factor II) and factors VII, IX and X. Without it, they cannot bind calcium and cannot do their job. The typical baby bleeds from the umbilical cord, the nose or the gut within the first few days.</p><p><b>Vitamin K deficiency after the newborn period.</b> Adults rarely become deficient, because gut bacteria make the vitamin and it is recycled in the body. The causes to know are:</p><ul><li><b>Antibiotics,</b> which kill the gut bacteria that make vitamin K.</li><li><b>Certain cephalosporin antibiotics</b> (cefamandole, cefoperazone, moxalactam), which also block the recycling of vitamin K directly, the same way warfarin does.</li><li><b>Fat malabsorption,</b> because vitamin K is fat-soluble.</li></ul><p><b>The lab pattern.</b> Clotting times are long, and the platelet count is normal. This separates it from scurvy (vitamin C deficiency). In scurvy the bleeding comes from weak blood vessels, and the clotting times are normal.</p><p><b>Toxicity.</b> Natural vitamin K is safe. The synthetic form, menadione, given to infants for a long time causes hemolytic anemia and jaundice, because it damages the red cell membrane.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (A newborn does well with breast feeding. Two days later, the mother calls franti)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":15,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":9,"f":"G098"}],"tier":1,"n":"37"}]},{"l":"BIO-C4","u":20,"label":"Vitamin K makes γ-carboxyglutamate (Gla) on clotting factors","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G030-31.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K carboxylase turns glutamate into γ-carboxyglutamate (Gla), which binds calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K cycle: γ-carboxylase adds the Gla residue to factors II, VII, IX, X","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-7-1","s":"A newborn baby has multiple hemorrhages. Clotting studies demonstrate an elevated prothrombin time. An abnormality of which of the following biochemical reaction is likely to present in this patient?","o":["A. Conversion of homocysteine to methionine","B. Conversion of methylmalonyl CoA to succinyl CoA","C. Degradation of cystathionine","D. Formation of gamma-carboxyglutamate residues","E. Hydroxylation of proline"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Formation of gamma-carboxyglutamate residues","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Formation of gamma-carboxyglutamate residues.</b> A bleeding newborn with a long prothrombin time (the time blood takes to clot in a standard test) has <b>vitamin K deficiency</b>. Vitamin K is the coenzyme for one specific reaction: adding a carboxyl group to glutamate residues on clotting factors, which turns each glutamate into <b>gamma-carboxyglutamate</b>, shortened to Gla. That is the reaction failing in this baby.</p><p><b>The reaction step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The liver makes prothrombin (factor II) and factors VII, IX and X as inactive precursors.</li><li>The enzyme γ-glutamyl carboxylase adds a second carboxyl group to the gamma carbon of several glutamates on each factor. It uses carbon dioxide, oxygen and the active form of vitamin K, called vitamin K hydroquinone.</li><li>Each Gla now has two negative charges side by side. Together they grip a calcium ion.</li><li>The calcium links the factor to the phospholipids on the surface of platelets, which is where clotting happens.</li></ol><p>Without Gla, the factors are made but float free and do nothing. In one line: vitamin K converts precursor clotting factors into mature clotting factors.</p><p><b>Vitamin K is recycled, and that is where warfarin acts.</b> Each carboxylation uses up the active vitamin and leaves it as vitamin K epoxide, which is useless. Two enzymes regenerate it. Vitamin K epoxide reductase turns the epoxide into the quinone, and a second reductase turns the quinone back into the active hydroquinone. This recycling is why a few micrograms a day is enough. The drugs <b>warfarin</b> and dicoumarol look like vitamin K and block the reductases. They do not block the carboxylase. The vitamin is trapped as epoxide, the factors lack Gla and clotting slows. This is why warfarin is used to prevent clots in deep veins and in the lungs.</p><p><b>Why a newborn.</b> The gut is sterile at birth, so no bacteria make vitamin K yet, and breast milk has little.</p><p><b>The vitamin behind each classic reaction.</b> It is worth knowing these pairs together.</p><ul><li>Making gamma-carboxyglutamate: vitamin K.</li><li>Hydroxylating proline in collagen: vitamin C.</li><li>Converting homocysteine to methionine: vitamin B12 and folate.</li><li>Converting methylmalonyl CoA to succinyl CoA: vitamin B12.</li><li>Breaking down cystathionine: vitamin B6.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin K)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"38"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":21,"label":"Vitamin C hydroxylates proline, lysine in collagen","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Prolyl hydroxylase reaction: ascorbate needed to hydroxylate proline in pro-alpha collagen chains","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/F2296-193.jpg","cap":"Proline and lysyl hydroxylases cycle Fe2+ to Fe3+; ascorbic acid reduces it back","where":"2023 TA review, slide 193"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-5-1","s":"A 10-month old infant is brought to the hospital with petechiae and echymoses. Further examination reveals bleeding gums and bilateral tenderness of lower limbs. The deficiency of a water-soluble vitamin is suspected. Why does the deficiency cause the observed symptoms?","o":["A. The deficient vitamin is needed for the gamma-glutamyl carboxylase","B. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of lysyl oxidase","C. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of glycogen phosphorylase","D. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of prolyl hydroxylase","E. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of pyruvate dehydrogenase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of prolyl hydroxylase","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. The deficient vitamin is needed for the function of prolyl hydroxylase.</b> Pinpoint skin bleeds (petechiae), bruises (ecchymoses), bleeding gums and tender legs in an infant are <b>scurvy</b>, the disease of <b>vitamin C</b> deficiency. Vitamin C is water-soluble, as the question says. It is needed by prolyl hydroxylase, an enzyme that builds collagen. Without it, collagen is weak. Small blood vessels are held together by collagen, so they leak and the child bleeds.</p><p><b>What prolyl hydroxylase does.</b> Collagen is made of three protein chains wound into a triple helix. In the rough endoplasmic reticulum, two enzymes add hydroxyl groups (–OH) to the chains. Prolyl hydroxylase adds them to proline, and lysyl hydroxylase adds them to lysine. These hydroxyl groups let the three chains hydrogen-bond to each other, which makes the helix stable, and they are needed for the later cross-links that make collagen fibers strong.</p><p><b>Where vitamin C comes in.</b> Both hydroxylases hold an iron atom at their active site. The iron works only in its reduced form, ferrous iron (Fe<sup>2+</sup>). During the reaction the iron is sometimes oxidized to ferric iron (Fe<sup>3+</sup>), and the enzyme stalls. Vitamin C is a reducing agent, which means it donates electrons. It turns the iron back into Fe<sup>2+</sup>, so the enzyme keeps working. The enzymes also use oxygen and α-ketoglutarate.</p><p><b>Why each sign appears.</b> Weak collagen means weak vessel walls and poor repair. Gums become sore, spongy and bleed. The skin shows petechiae and bruises. Wounds heal badly. In infants, blood collects under the lining of the bones, which makes the legs very tender. The platelets and the clotting factors are normal in scurvy. The problem is the vessel wall.</p><p><b>Do not mix up the similar-sounding enzymes.</b></p><ul><li><b>Prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase:</b> need vitamin C. They hydroxylate collagen.</li><li><b>Lysyl oxidase:</b> needs copper, not a vitamin. It makes the cross-links between collagen fibers later on.</li><li><b>Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase:</b> needs vitamin K, a fat-soluble vitamin. It activates clotting factors.</li><li><b>Pyruvate dehydrogenase:</b> needs thiamine (vitamin B1), among others.</li><li><b>Glycogen phosphorylase:</b> needs vitamin B6.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Review of Objective 2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"39"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":21,"label":"Scurvy → collagen synthesis fails","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-11.jpg","cap":"Scurvy photos: petechiae on skin and swollen, bleeding gums","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Prolyl hydroxylase needs ascorbate to add OH to proline in collagen","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-25-1","s":"A 77-year-old male is brought to your clinic at the insistence of his daughter for a regular check up appointment. The patient lives alone and has not seen a physician in several years. According to the daughter, the patient has been reclusive ever since his wife passed away and has been caring for himself. On physical exam, the patient is very thin and disheveled. He has poor dentition with swollen gingivae. The rest of his exam is within normal limits. Which of the following is most likely affected in this patient?","o":["A. Coagulation factor synthesis","B. Bone metabolism","C. Amino acid and carbohydrate metabolism","D. Collagen synthesis","E. DNA synthesis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Collagen synthesis","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Collagen synthesis.</b> A thin, neglected elderly man who lives alone and has swollen gums has <b>scurvy</b>, which is <b>vitamin C</b> deficiency. Vitamin C is needed to make strong collagen, so collagen synthesis is the process affected.</p><p><b>Why he is the typical patient.</b> Vitamin C comes from fresh fruit and vegetables, and the body does not store much. A person who eats a poor, narrow diet for months runs out. The classic patients are isolated elderly people, people with alcoholism and infants fed poorly. His gums are the first place it shows.</p><p><b>How vitamin C builds collagen.</b> Two enzymes, prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase, add hydroxyl groups to the prolines and lysines of new collagen chains. The hydroxyl groups let the three chains lock into a stable triple helix. Both enzymes use an iron atom that must stay in the reduced ferrous form (Fe<sup>2+</sup>). Vitamin C keeps the iron reduced. Without vitamin C the collagen is under-hydroxylated and weak.</p><p><b>The signs of scurvy.</b> Weak collagen makes small vessels leak and tissue heal badly.</p><ul><li>Sore, spongy, swollen gums that bleed, and loose teeth.</li><li>Petechiae, which are pinpoint purple spots of bleeding in the skin.</li><li>Easy bruising.</li><li>Poor wound healing.</li></ul><p>Clotting times are normal in scurvy, because the clotting factors are fine. That separates it from vitamin K deficiency, where the bleeding comes from inactive clotting factors and clotting times are long.</p><p><b>Numbers and absorption.</b> The recommended intake is 60 mg a day for a nonsmoking adult. Smokers need 35 mg a day more. The intestine absorbs vitamin C with a specific active transporter called SVCT-1 (sodium-dependent vitamin C transporter 1).</p><p><b>The process tied to each vitamin.</b> Collagen synthesis goes with vitamin C. Clotting factor activation goes with vitamin K. Bone mineral goes with vitamin D. DNA synthesis goes with folate and vitamin B12. Amino acid and carbohydrate metabolism goes with the B vitamins such as B6 and B1.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":4,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":25,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":7,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":43,"f":"D006"}],"tier":1,"n":"40"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":21,"label":"Vitamin C: carnitine from trimethyllysine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-13.jpg","cap":"Vitamin C makes collagen, norepinephrine and carnitine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"G031-12-1","s":"Which of the following vitamins is required as a coenzyme for the synthesis of carnitine from trimethyllysine ?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin B","C. Vitamin C","D. Vitamin D","E. Vitamin E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin C","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Vitamin C.</b> The body makes carnitine from trimethyllysine. The pathway includes hydroxylation steps, and the hydroxylase enzymes use iron that must be kept in its reduced form. <b>Vitamin C</b> (ascorbic acid) does that, exactly as it does for the collagen hydroxylases.</p><p><b>What carnitine is for.</b> Long-chain fatty acids are burned inside the mitochondria, but they cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane on their own. Carnitine is the carrier. The fatty acid is attached to carnitine, crosses as acyl carnitine and is then burned by β-oxidation. So a shortage of carnitine means less fat burning and less energy. This is one reason why fatigue is an early complaint in vitamin C deficiency.</p><p><b>The one idea behind all of vitamin C's jobs.</b> Vitamin C is a <b>reducing agent</b>: it donates electrons. (After donating them it becomes dehydroascorbic acid.) Several enzymes hold a metal, iron or copper, that must stay reduced to work. Vitamin C keeps it reduced.</p><ul><li><b>Collagen:</b> hydroxylation of proline and lysine. This is the most important job. Its failure causes scurvy.</li><li><b>Carnitine:</b> made from trimethyllysine.</li><li><b>Norepinephrine:</b> the enzyme dopamine β-hydroxylase turns dopamine into norepinephrine. It contains copper, and vitamin C keeps the copper reduced.</li><li><b>Iron absorption:</b> in the gut, vitamin C reduces ferric iron (Fe<sup>3+</sup>) from food to ferrous iron (Fe<sup>2+</sup>), the form that can be absorbed.</li><li><b>Antioxidant:</b> it neutralizes reactive molecules and also regenerates vitamin E.</li></ul><p><b>About the option \"Vitamin B.\"</b> There is no single vitamin B. The B complex is a family of different vitamins, each with its own coenzyme, so \"vitamin B\" cannot be the coenzyme for one specific reaction.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Review of Objective 2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":12,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"41"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":22,"label":"Pantothenic acid (B5) → coenzyme A","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-30.jpg","cap":"Pantothenic acid structure built into coenzyme A; deficiency signs","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/G031-15.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase's five vitamin cofactors: coenzyme A comes from pantothenic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F2297-27-1","s":"7.Your mother sent you a bottle of multivitamins (Given Below). Looking at its label, you know that it is important source of coenzyme in your body for the enzyme:","o":["A. Pyruvate Carboxylase","B. Transketolase","C. Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","D. Pyruvate Kinase","E. Alanine Transaminase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Pyruvate Dehydrogenase.</b> The bottle in the picture is <b>pantothenic acid, vitamin B5</b>. The body uses pantothenic acid to build <b>coenzyme A</b> (CoA). Coenzyme A is one of the five cofactors of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex. It is the molecule that receives the 2-carbon acetyl group, so the product of the reaction is acetyl CoA.</p><p><b>What the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex does.</b> It sits in the mitochondria and links glycolysis to the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle. It turns pyruvate (3 carbons) into acetyl CoA (2 carbons), releases one carbon as carbon dioxide and hands a pair of electrons to NAD<sup>+</sup> to make NADH. It has three enzymes, called E1, E2 and E3, and five cofactors. Four of the five come from B vitamins.</p><p><b>The five cofactors, in the order they work.</b></p><ol><li><b>Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), from vitamin B1:</b> on E1. It grabs pyruvate and removes the carbon dioxide.</li><li><b>Lipoic acid:</b> on E2. It is a swinging arm that picks up the 2-carbon piece. The body makes it, so it is not a vitamin.</li><li><b>Coenzyme A, from vitamin B5:</b> takes the 2-carbon piece from lipoic acid. Acetyl CoA leaves.</li><li><b>FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide), from vitamin B2 (riboflavin):</b> on E3. It takes the electrons off lipoic acid, which resets the arm for the next turn.</li><li><b>NAD<sup>+</sup> (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide), from vitamin B3 (niacin):</b> takes the electrons from FAD and becomes NADH, which goes to the electron transport chain.</li></ol><p><b>Two sister enzymes use the same five cofactors.</b> They are α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase in the TCA cycle and branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase, which breaks down valine, isoleucine and leucine.</p><p><b>More about coenzyme A and vitamin B5.</b> Coenzyme A is the carrier of acetyl groups (acetyl CoA) and it also activates fatty acids (acyl CoA). Deficiency of pantothenic acid is rare. It causes fatigue, sleep problems, poor coordination and nausea.</p><p><b>The coenzymes of the other enzymes listed.</b> Pyruvate carboxylase uses biotin. Transketolase uses thiamine pyrophosphate. Alanine transaminase uses pyridoxal phosphate, from vitamin B6. Pyruvate kinase needs no vitamin coenzyme.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G096-28.jpg","in":"Photo of a supplement bottle labelled 'TWINLAB Pantothenic Acid (B-5) Caps' - the vitamin is named only in the picture.","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin B5 – Pantothenic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (PDH Complex (PDHC))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":28,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":10,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":27,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":14,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"42"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":22,"label":"Thiamine (TPP) → oxidative decarboxylation, PDH","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-17.jpg","cap":"TPP is cofactor for PDH, alpha-KG dehydrogenase, branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, transketolase","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 17"},{"src":"../sl/G031-18.jpg","cap":"Map of TPP steps: pyruvate→acetyl-CoA, alpha-KG→succinyl-CoA, branched-chain keto acids, pentose shunt","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G031-15.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex needs five cofactors: TPP, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, NAD+","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-20-1","s":"Deficiency of thiamine in alcoholics may lead to Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. This syndrome is associated with energy deficit in the brain because thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is:","o":["A. Necessary for oxidation of alcohol","B. An essential cofactor for Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","C. Necessary for utilizing amino acids for energy via transamination","D. A cofactor for Transketolase","E. A major cofactor for Reduction reactions"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"An essential cofactor for Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. An essential cofactor for Pyruvate Dehydrogenase.</b> The brain gets nearly all of its energy by burning glucose with oxygen. Glucose is first broken down to pyruvate. The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex must then turn pyruvate into acetyl CoA before it can enter the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle, where most of the ATP comes from. The first enzyme of that complex needs <b>thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)</b>, the working form of thiamine (vitamin B1). Without thiamine, this step stalls and the brain runs short of energy. The question asks why there is an <b>energy</b> deficit, and this is the reason.</p><p><b>What happens to the pyruvate.</b> It cannot move forward, so it is turned into lactate instead. ATP falls most in the tissues that depend on burning glucose: brain, nerves and heart. Neurons have no fuel reserve, so they fail early.</p><p><b>All the enzymes that use TPP.</b> TPP is the cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation, which means removing carbon dioxide from an α-keto acid.</p><ul><li><b>Pyruvate dehydrogenase:</b> pyruvate to acetyl CoA.</li><li><b>α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase:</b> a step of the TCA cycle.</li><li><b>Branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase:</b> breakdown of valine, isoleucine and leucine.</li><li><b>Transketolase:</b> moves 2-carbon pieces between sugars in the pentose phosphate pathway.</li></ul><p>Transketolase really is a TPP enzyme. But its pathway makes sugars and NADPH, not ATP, so it does not explain an energy deficit. The first two enzymes are the ones in the energy pathway.</p><p><b>Why people with alcoholism become thiamine deficient.</b> Alcohol impairs the absorption of thiamine in the intestine. Their diet is poor. Carbohydrate, including ethanol, raises the demand for thiamine. And they are often low in magnesium, which the TPP enzymes also need.</p><p><b>Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome.</b></p><ul><li><b>Wernicke's encephalopathy</b> is the acute phase: eye movement problems (nystagmus, a drooping eyelid), ataxia (unsteady, uncoordinated movement) and confusion.</li><li><b>Korsakoff's psychosis</b> is the chronic phase: memory loss, making up stories to fill the gaps (confabulation) and hallucinations.</li></ul><p>Treatment is thiamine, first into a vein and then by mouth. Give thiamine before or together with glucose, because glucose alone pushes more fuel toward the blocked enzymes. The Wernicke signs often improve. Full recovery from Korsakoff's is unlikely.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (PDH Complex (PDHC))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":34,"f":"G090"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":34,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":11,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":20,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":10,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":24,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":8,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"43"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":23,"label":"Thiamine helps mild maple syrup urine disease","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-18.jpg","cap":"TPP-dependent breakdown of valine, isoleucine and leucine keto acids","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G031-17.jpg","cap":"TPP is the cofactor for branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, the defective enzyme","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-43-1","s":"Thiamine supplementation may help treat milder form of defect:","o":["A. Phenylketonuria","B. Albinism","C. Maple syrup urine disease","D. Alkaptonuria","E. Severe combined Immunodeficiency syndrome"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Maple syrup urine disease","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Maple syrup urine disease.</b> Maple syrup urine disease is an inherited defect of an enzyme called branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase. This enzyme uses <b>thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)</b>, the working form of thiamine (vitamin B1), as a cofactor. In some milder forms the enzyme is weak but not absent, and a high dose of thiamine makes it work better.</p><p><b>What the enzyme does.</b> Three amino acids have branched carbon chains: <b>valine, isoleucine and leucine</b>. To break them down, the body first removes the amino group, which leaves a branched-chain keto acid. The dehydrogenase then removes a carbon dioxide from the keto acid and attaches the rest to coenzyme A. When the enzyme fails, the three amino acids and their keto acids build up in the blood and urine. The keto acids give the urine a sweet smell like maple syrup, and they are toxic to the brain.</p><p><b>Why thiamine can help.</b> This enzyme is built just like the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex. It has three parts and uses the same five cofactors: thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoic acid, coenzyme A, FAD and NAD<sup>+</sup>. Thiamine pyrophosphate works on the first part and removes the carbon dioxide. Extra thiamine helps a partly working enzyme hold on to its cofactor.</p><p><b>The thiamine enzymes as a group.</b> Thiamine pyrophosphate serves pyruvate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase and transketolase. The first three remove carbon dioxide from an α-keto acid. A lack of thiamine slows all of them at once.</p><p><b>Where the three amino acids end up.</b> Valine is glucogenic (it can be made into glucose). Leucine is ketogenic (it gives acetyl CoA). Isoleucine is both.</p><p><b>The enzyme behind each of the other diseases.</b> Phenylketonuria is a defect of phenylalanine hydroxylase. Albinism is a defect of tyrosinase, so no melanin is made. Alkaptonuria is a defect of homogentisate oxidase. None of these enzymes uses thiamine.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":12,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":43,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"44"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":23,"label":"Beriberi → thiamine deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-19.jpg","cap":"Wet beriberi: leg edema photo, tachycardia, cardiomegaly, heart failure; infantile beriberi","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 19"},{"src":"../sl/G031-20.jpg","cap":"Dry beriberi: symmetrical peripheral neuropathy of the legs; Wernicke–Korsakoff in alcoholics","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-6-1","s":"Freshwater mussels such as Velesunio ambiguous contain the enzyme, thiaminase, which destroys Vitamin B1 in the diet. Excessive consumption of such mussels may lead to which of the following diseases?","o":["A. Pernicious anemia","B. Beriberi","C. Pellagra","D. Scurvy","E. Osteomalacia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Beriberi","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Beriberi.</b> Thiaminase is an enzyme that destroys thiamine, which is vitamin B1. Eating a lot of food that contains thiaminase destroys the thiamine in the diet before it can be absorbed. The result is thiamine deficiency, and severe thiamine deficiency is called <b>beriberi</b>.</p><p><b>Why thiamine matters.</b> Its working form, thiamine pyrophosphate, is needed by pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, two enzymes the cell must use to burn glucose for energy. Without thiamine, pyruvate cannot enter the energy pathway and piles up as lactate. The tissues that burn the most glucose suffer first: nerves, brain and heart. That is why beriberi is a disease of the heart and the nerves.</p><p><b>The types of beriberi.</b></p><ul><li><b>Wet beriberi</b> affects the heart and circulation. There is swelling of the lower legs (this is why it is called wet), a fast heart rate, an enlarged and weak heart, and heart failure.</li><li><b>Dry beriberi</b> affects the nerves. There is a symmetrical peripheral neuropathy, mainly in the legs, because the longest nerves fail first. It causes tingling, pain and loss of feeling in the feet and legs.</li><li><b>Infantile beriberi</b> affects babies breastfed by mothers who lack thiamine. It starts quickly with a fast heart rate, vomiting and convulsions, and it is often fatal.</li></ul><p><b>The brain form.</b> In people with chronic alcoholism, thiamine deficiency shows up as Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome: confusion, unsteady movement and eye signs at first, and later memory loss with made-up stories.</p><p><b>Match each deficiency disease to its vitamin.</b></p><ul><li>Beriberi: thiamine (B1).</li><li>Pellagra: niacin (B3).</li><li>Scurvy: vitamin C.</li><li>Pernicious anemia: cobalamin (B12).</li><li>Osteomalacia in adults and rickets in children: vitamin D.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"45"},{"id":"G031-36-1","s":"A homeless woman presents with shortness of breath on exertion and pedal edema. Cardiac workup performed shows evidence of dilated cardiomyopathy and increased cardiac output. She also has decreased sensation over both extremities bilaterally. Which vitamin deficiency most likely caused these symptoms?","o":["A. Pantothenic acid","B. Niacin","C. Biotin","D. Thiamine","E. Riboflavin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thiamine","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Thiamine.</b> She has both forms of beriberi at once, and beriberi is severe <b>thiamine (vitamin B1)</b> deficiency. The shortness of breath, swollen feet, enlarged weak heart and high cardiac output are <b>wet beriberi</b>. The reduced sensation in both legs is <b>dry beriberi</b>. A homeless person with a poor diet is a typical patient.</p><p><b>Why thiamine deficiency hits the heart and nerves.</b> Thiamine pyrophosphate is the cofactor of pyruvate dehydrogenase, the enzyme that lets pyruvate enter the energy pathway. Without it, pyruvate is turned into lactate, and ATP falls in the tissues that depend on burning glucose.</p><p><b>How wet beriberi produces high-output heart failure.</b></p><ol><li>Pyruvate and lactate build up, and they make the small arteries widen.</li><li>The resistance to blood flow falls, so the heart must pump more blood each minute to keep the pressure up. This is the increased cardiac output.</li><li>At the same time the heart muscle is short of energy. It stretches and weakens. This is the dilated cardiomyopathy.</li><li>The overworked heart fails. Fluid backs up into the lungs, which causes breathlessness on exertion, and into the legs, which causes the edema.</li></ol><p><b>How dry beriberi produces numbness.</b> Nerves burn glucose and have no energy reserve. The longest nerves fail first, so the loss of feeling starts in the feet and legs on both sides. Tingling and pain are common.</p><p><b>Who gets it.</b> People with a poor diet, and above all people with chronic alcoholism, in whom alcohol also blocks thiamine absorption. In them the brain form, Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome, is the most common: confusion, unsteady movement and eye signs, followed by memory loss.</p><p><b>The energy-releasing B vitamins look alike in mild deficiency.</b> They share nerve symptoms, confusion and tiredness. What singles out thiamine is the heart. None of the others on the list causes heart failure. Niacin deficiency gives diarrhea, dermatitis and dementia. Riboflavin deficiency gives cracked lips and a sore tongue. Biotin deficiency gives dermatitis and hair loss. Pantothenic acid deficiency is rare and gives fatigue.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":36,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"46"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":24,"label":"Riboflavin (FMN, FAD) → energy-yielding pathways","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-22.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin converted to FMN then FAD, cofactors for oxidoreduction reactions","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G031-23.jpg","cap":"FAD and FMN carry electrons from fatty acyl-CoA, succinate and pyruvate to CoQ","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-7-1","s":"The dietary riboflavin molecule is converted into active forms in the body and is essential for:","o":["A. Hemoglobin synthesis.","B. Energy yielding pathways.","C. Prevent megaloblastic anemia.","D. Absorption of Fe+2","E. B12 absorption."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Energy yielding pathways.","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Energy yielding pathways.</b> Riboflavin is vitamin B2. The body turns it into two active forms, <b>FMN</b> (flavin mononucleotide) and <b>FAD</b> (flavin adenine dinucleotide). Both carry electrons, as two hydrogens at a time, in oxidation-reduction reactions. The pathways that release energy from food are chains of exactly these reactions, so riboflavin is essential for them.</p><p><b>Where FAD and FMN work.</b></p><ul><li><b>Fat burning:</b> acyl CoA dehydrogenase, the first step of β-oxidation, uses FAD.</li><li><b>The tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle:</b> succinate dehydrogenase, which turns succinate into fumarate, uses FAD.</li><li><b>Pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase:</b> their third enzyme (E3) uses FAD to pass electrons on to NAD<sup>+</sup>.</li><li><b>The electron transport chain:</b> FMN is part of complex I, where it accepts the electrons from NADH and passes them to coenzyme Q.</li><li><b>Glycerol 3-phosphate dehydrogenase</b> in the mitochondria also uses FAD.</li></ul><p>In each case the electrons end up in the electron transport chain, flow to oxygen and drive the making of ATP.</p><p><b>How flavins differ from NAD<sup>+</sup>.</b> NAD<sup>+</sup>, which comes from niacin, picks up electrons at one enzyme and floats off to deliver them somewhere else. FAD and FMN stay tightly bound to their own enzyme. A cofactor that stays attached like this is called a prosthetic group, and the enzymes are called flavoproteins.</p><p><b>Other facts.</b> Riboflavin is destroyed by light. The daily need is about 1 to 1.3 mg.</p><p><b>What deficiency looks like.</b> It shows in the mouth and the eye: cracks at the corners of the mouth, cracked and swollen lips, a smooth magenta tongue, and blood vessels growing into the cornea.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":37,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":13,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":7,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":4,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":21,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":2,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"47"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":24,"label":"Riboflavin deficiency: mouth, tongue, cornea signs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-24.jpg","cap":"Four riboflavin deficiency signs with photos; 2 C's: cheilosis and corneal vascularization","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F1022-11-1","s":"29-year-old man presents to the free clinic with multiple complaints. He has a sore throat, fatigue, rash, and sensitivity to light. He states he has never seen a doctor and his past medical history is not known. Physical examination reveals cracks at the corners of the mouth, glossitis, stomatitis, and a dry rash diffusely. Which of the following vitamins is most likely deficient in this patient?","o":["A. Vitamin C","B. Vitamin A","C. Vitamin B6","D. Vitamin B2","E. Vitamin B9"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B2","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Vitamin B2.</b> Vitamin B2 is riboflavin. Cracks at the corners of the mouth, an inflamed tongue (glossitis), an inflamed mouth (stomatitis), a sore throat, a rash and sensitivity to light together make the picture of <b>riboflavin deficiency</b>. It shows in the mouth and in the eye.</p><p><b>The four classic signs.</b></p><ul><li><b>Angular stomatitis:</b> cracks (fissures) at the corners of the mouth.</li><li><b>Cheilosis:</b> vertical cracks in the lips, which later become red and swollen.</li><li><b>Glossitis:</b> a smooth, swollen tongue with a purplish-magenta color.</li><li><b>Corneal vascularization:</b> blood vessels grow into the cornea, which normally has none. This is what makes the eyes sensitive to light.</li></ul><p>A memory hook: B<b>2</b> has <b>2</b> C's, <b>c</b>heilosis and <b>c</b>orneal vascularization.</p><p><b>Why these tissues.</b> Riboflavin is the building block of FAD and FMN (flavin adenine dinucleotide and flavin mononucleotide), which carry electrons in the pathways that produce energy. Tissues that replace their cells quickly, such as the lining of the mouth, the lips, the tongue and the skin, need a steady supply. They are the first to break down when the vitamin runs short.</p><p><b>How to tell it from the look-alikes.</b> Several vitamin deficiencies give a sore tongue, so look for the sign that is special to each.</p><ul><li><b>Riboflavin (B2):</b> mouth-corner cracks plus an eye sign, light sensitivity from corneal vessels.</li><li><b>Niacin (B3):</b> pellagra, with diarrhea, dermatitis on sun-exposed skin and dementia.</li><li><b>Vitamin B6:</b> nerve damage and a type of anemia.</li><li><b>Folate (B9):</b> megaloblastic anemia, with large red cells.</li><li><b>Vitamin C:</b> spongy, bleeding gums and pinpoint skin bleeds.</li><li><b>Vitamin A:</b> night blindness and dry eyes.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G120-6.jpg","in":"Open mouth: cracks and redness at both corners of the mouth (angular cheilitis) and a red, smooth tongue (glossitis).","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":6,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":11,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":47,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":27,"f":"F0928"}],"tier":1,"n":"48"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":24,"label":"Brown syndrome: riboflavin restores FMN/FAD electron transfer","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-26.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin transporter deficiency: motor, sensory and cranial neuronopathy that improves with riboflavin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G031-22.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin is the precursor of FMN and FAD, the cofactors for oxidation-reduction","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"G090-22-1","s":"A 10-year-old boy develops progressive sensorineural hearing loss, gait ataxia, distal muscle weakness, and respiratory difficulty. Genetic testing identifies biallelic pathogenic variants in SLC52A2, which encodes a riboflavin transporter. Treatment with high-dose riboflavin improves his symptoms. Which cellular process is most directly enhanced by this treatment?","o":["A. Electron transfer by FMN- and FAD-dependent flavoproteins","B. Oxidative decarboxylation mediated by thiamine pyrophosphate","C. Electron transfer mediated by NAD⁺-dependent enzymes","D. Amino acid transamination mediated by pyridoxal phosphate","E. One-carbon transfer reactions mediated by tetrahydrofolate"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Electron transfer by FMN- and FAD-dependent flavoproteins","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Electron transfer by FMN- and FAD-dependent flavoproteins.</b> The gene SLC52A2 makes a transporter that carries <b>riboflavin (vitamin B2)</b> into tissues. With both copies of the gene faulty, his nerve cells do not get enough riboflavin. Riboflavin is the building block of <b>FMN and FAD</b> (flavin mononucleotide and flavin adenine dinucleotide). These are the electron carriers of the flavoproteins, enzymes found in the energy pathways. A very high dose of riboflavin pushes enough vitamin into the cells to rebuild FMN and FAD, so electron transfer by these enzymes recovers.</p><p><b>The two riboflavin transporters.</b></p><ul><li><b>RFVT3</b> (gene SLC52A3) takes riboflavin from food into the cells of the gut.</li><li><b>RFVT2</b> (gene SLC52A2) moves it from the blood into tissues such as the brain.</li></ul><p>A defect in either one starves the neurons of riboflavin.</p><p><b>The disease.</b> It is called Brown–Vialetto–Van Laere syndrome, or Brown syndrome for short. Nerve cells degenerate, both motor and sensory. It is often misdiagnosed as an autoimmune disease.</p><ul><li><b>Motor:</b> weakness of the limbs, wasting of the hands and feet, and breathing trouble because the diaphragm is paralyzed.</li><li><b>Sensory:</b> an unsteady walk (gait ataxia).</li><li><b>Cranial nerves:</b> hearing loss of the nerve type (sensorineural deafness), damage to the optic nerve, and weak speech and swallowing.</li></ul><p>High-dose riboflavin (10 to 50 mg per kg each day) improves the signs and symptoms. This makes it a treatable inherited nerve disease, which is why it must not be missed.</p><p><b>Why neurons suffer.</b> FAD and FMN work in fat burning, in the tricarboxylic acid cycle and in complex I of the electron transport chain. Neurons need a great deal of energy and have no reserve.</p><p><b>Each B vitamin and its working form.</b></p><ul><li>Thiamine (B1): thiamine pyrophosphate, for oxidative decarboxylation.</li><li>Riboflavin (B2): FMN and FAD, for electron transfer.</li><li>Niacin (B3): NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADP<sup>+</sup>, for electron transfer.</li><li>Pyridoxine (B6): pyridoxal phosphate, for transamination and other amino acid reactions.</li><li>Folic acid (B9): tetrahydrofolate, for one-carbon transfers.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (RFVT2 (SLC52A2) and RFVT3 (SLC52A3))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (A motor/sensory neuronopathy that is usually misdiagnosed as an autoimmune disea)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":22,"f":"G090"}],"tier":1,"n":"49"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":25,"label":"NADPH drives reductive synthesis (cholesterol)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-28.jpg","cap":"NADH and NADPH act as reducing agents; NADPH protects against reactive oxygen species","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-42-1","s":"NADPH dependent enzyme are primarily essential for body  :","o":["A. heme synthesis.","B. energy yielding reactions.","C. synthesis methionine .","D. synthesis of histamine.","E. cholesterol synthesis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"cholesterol synthesis","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. cholesterol synthesis.</b> NADPH is the cell's reducing agent for <b>building molecules</b>. Building cholesterol, fatty acids and steroid hormones means adding hydrogens and electrons to carbon chains, and NADPH supplies them. In cholesterol synthesis the key, rate-limiting enzyme is HMG-CoA reductase (hydroxymethylglutaryl-CoA reductase), and it uses NADPH.</p><p><b>Two similar carriers with opposite jobs.</b> Both come from niacin, vitamin B3.</p><ul><li><b>NAD<sup>+</sup></b> (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) works in <b>breakdown</b> pathways. It collects electrons from dehydrogenases as fuel is burned and becomes NADH. NADH hands the electrons to the electron transport chain, and they flow through coenzyme Q and cytochrome c to oxygen. This is how energy is released. So energy-yielding reactions use NAD<sup>+</sup> (and FAD), not NADPH.</li><li><b>NADPH</b> (the reduced form of NADP<sup>+</sup>, which is NAD<sup>+</sup> with an extra phosphate) works in <b>building</b> pathways. It donates electrons for the synthesis of fatty acids, cholesterol and steroids.</li></ul><p><b>The second job of NADPH: protection.</b> NADPH is used by the enzyme glutathione reductase to keep glutathione in its reduced form. Reduced glutathione destroys reactive oxygen species, which are damaging forms of oxygen. So NADPH also protects cells, especially red blood cells, from oxidative damage.</p><p><b>Where NAD<sup>+</sup> picks up its electrons.</b> In the mitochondria: the dehydrogenases for pyruvate, isocitrate, α-ketoglutarate and malate, and a step of fat burning. In the cytosol: glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase in glycolysis, and alcohol dehydrogenase.</p><p><b>The vitamins behind the other processes listed.</b> Heme synthesis starts with an enzyme that needs vitamin B6. Methionine synthesis needs vitamin B12 and folate. Histamine is made from histidine by an enzyme that needs vitamin B6.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 28 (Pyruvate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":40,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":15,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":42,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"50"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":25,"label":"Maize (corn) diet → pellagra","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-27.jpg","cap":"Niacin is synthesized from tryptophan, so low tryptophan intake limits niacin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G031-29.jpg","cap":"Pellagra signs: the 3 D's and photos of sun-exposed dermatitis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-39-1","s":"Which among the following may predispose to Pellagra?","o":["A. methotrexate intake.","B. maize supplemented diet occasionally with a little vegetable oil.","C. Glucose -6-phosphatase deficiency.","D. an inherited defect where painful skin excoriations (pruritis) provoked by porphyrin-mediated generation of superoxide .","E. Cystic fibrosis."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"maize supplemented diet occasionally with a little vegetable oil.","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. maize supplemented diet occasionally with a little vegetable oil.</b> Pellagra is the disease of <b>niacin (vitamin B3)</b> deficiency. The body has two sources of niacin: niacin in food, and niacin that it makes itself from the amino acid <b>tryptophan</b>. A diet based on maize (corn) fails on both counts. Corn is low in tryptophan, and the niacin in corn is tightly bound and poorly absorbed. Vegetable oil adds neither one. So a person living mostly on corn develops pellagra.</p><p><b>Making niacin from tryptophan.</b> Tryptophan is an essential amino acid. The body can convert it step by step into NAD<sup>+</sup> (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide), the working form of niacin. The route is inefficient: it takes about 60 mg of tryptophan to make 1 mg of niacin. Some of its steps need vitamin B2 (as FAD) and vitamin B6.</p><p><b>Deficiency needs both sources to fail.</b> That is why the conditions that lead to pellagra are the ones that also take away tryptophan.</p><ul><li><b>A corn-based diet:</b> low in niacin that can be absorbed and low in tryptophan.</li><li><b>Hartnup disease:</b> the transporter for neutral amino acids in the gut and kidney is defective. Tryptophan is not absorbed and is lost in the urine.</li><li><b>Carcinoid syndrome:</b> a tumor that makes serotonin uses up tryptophan, because serotonin is made from tryptophan.</li><li><b>Deficiency of vitamin B2 or B6:</b> slows the conversion of tryptophan to niacin.</li></ul><p><b>What pellagra looks like.</b> The 3 D's: <b>diarrhea, dermatitis and dementia</b>. Untreated, it ends in death. The tongue is also swollen. Each D is a tissue with a high need for NAD<sup>+</sup>: the fast-renewing gut and skin, and the energy-hungry brain. The rash appears on skin exposed to the sun.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":39,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":14,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":39,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"51"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":25,"label":"Pellagra (3 D's) → niacin deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-29.jpg","cap":"Pellagra: diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia, later death; scaly rash on sun-exposed skin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29"},{"src":"../sl/F2296-201.jpg","cap":"Four D's of niacin deficiency beside photos of sun-exposed leg dermatitis","where":"2023 TA review, slide 201"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-3-1","s":"Deficiency of which of the following vitamins results in pellagra?","o":["A. Ascorbic acid","B. Niacin","C. Thiamine","D. Cobalamin","E. Folic acid"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Niacin","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Niacin.</b> Pellagra is the deficiency disease of <b>niacin, vitamin B3</b>. Remember it by the 3 D's: <b>diarrhea, dermatitis and dementia</b>. If it is not treated, the fourth D is death. The tongue is also swollen and sore (glossitis).</p><p><b>Why these three tissues.</b> Niacin is the building block of NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADP<sup>+</sup> (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide and its phosphate), the main electron carriers of the cell. Every cell needs them, but some tissues need the most.</p><ul><li><b>Gut lining:</b> it renews itself very quickly. It fails, and diarrhea follows.</li><li><b>Skin:</b> it also renews quickly. The dermatitis appears on <b>sun-exposed skin</b>, which becomes thick and scaly. Sunlight damages DNA, and repairing DNA uses up NAD<sup>+</sup>, so exposed skin runs out first.</li><li><b>Brain:</b> it needs a great deal of energy. It fails, and dementia follows.</li></ul><p><b>What NAD<sup>+</sup> and NADPH do.</b> NAD<sup>+</sup> collects electrons as fuel is broken down and delivers them, as NADH, to the electron transport chain to make ATP. NADPH donates electrons to build fatty acids, cholesterol and steroids, and it protects cells from oxidative damage.</p><p><b>Two sources of niacin.</b> The diet, and the body's own synthesis from the amino acid tryptophan (60 mg of tryptophan gives 1 mg of niacin). Pellagra appears when both fail, as on a corn-based diet. The daily need is 14 to 16 mg.</p><p><b>Niacin as a drug.</b> At very high doses, 1 to 3 grams a day, niacin is used to treat abnormal blood lipids. It blocks the release of fat from fat tissue, so the liver receives fewer fatty acids and makes less VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein). LDL (low-density lipoprotein, the harmful cholesterol carrier) and triglycerides fall, and HDL (high-density lipoprotein, the protective carrier) rises. These doses cause flushing and itching, and they can damage the liver. Taking aspirin beforehand reduces the flushing. Niacin is one of the few water-soluble vitamins that is toxic in excess.</p><p><b>The deficiency disease of each vitamin listed.</b> Ascorbic acid (vitamin C): scurvy. Thiamine (B1): beriberi. Cobalamin (B12) and folic acid: megaloblastic anemia.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 39 (Review of Objective 3)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"52"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":26,"label":"Biotin → pyruvate carboxylase → hypoglycemia, lactate","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-31.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate carboxylase needs biotin to make oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"},{"src":"../sl/G031-32.jpg","cap":"Biotin bound to carboxylases; needed for liver gluconeogenesis and lipid synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 32"},{"src":"../sl/F0319-41.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate carboxylase blocked: pyruvate becomes lactate, which competes with urate excretion","where":"2022 faculty review, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-26-1","s":"A deficiency of biotin may be associated with:","o":["A. Coarse facial features","B. Hypoglycemia","C. Methylmalonic acidosis","D. Bleeding gums","E. Hyperammonemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypoglycemia","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Hypoglycemia.</b> Biotin is vitamin B7. It is the cofactor of the enzyme <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b>, which carries out the first step of gluconeogenesis, the liver's pathway for making new glucose. During a fast the liver depends on gluconeogenesis to keep blood glucose up. Without biotin, that first step fails, and blood glucose falls.</p><p><b>What biotin does.</b> Biotin is a carrier of carbon dioxide. It is attached to a lysine on its enzyme and acts like a swinging arm. First, ATP is spent to load a carbon dioxide (taken from bicarbonate) onto the biotin. Then the arm swings over and adds the carbon dioxide to the substrate. Adding a carbon dioxide this way is called carboxylation, and the enzymes are called carboxylases. Compare thiamine, whose enzymes remove carbon dioxide.</p><p><b>The pyruvate carboxylase reaction.</b> Pyruvate (3 carbons) plus carbon dioxide gives oxaloacetate (4 carbons), and ATP is used. The enzyme is in the mitochondria. It has two purposes:</p><ul><li><b>Gluconeogenesis:</b> oxaloacetate is the first intermediate on the way from pyruvate to glucose.</li><li><b>Refilling the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle:</b> the cycle needs oxaloacetate to keep turning. A reaction that refills a cycle is called anaplerotic.</li></ul><p>Acetyl CoA switches the enzyme on. A lot of acetyl CoA is a sign that the cell needs more oxaloacetate.</p><p><b>The four biotin enzymes.</b></p><ul><li><b>Pyruvate carboxylase:</b> pyruvate to oxaloacetate (gluconeogenesis).</li><li><b>Acetyl CoA carboxylase:</b> acetyl CoA to malonyl CoA (the first step of fatty acid synthesis).</li><li><b>Propionyl CoA carboxylase:</b> propionyl CoA to methylmalonyl CoA. The next step after it needs vitamin B12.</li><li><b>Methylcrotonyl CoA carboxylase:</b> breakdown of the amino acid leucine.</li></ul><p><b>Signs of biotin deficiency.</b> Dermatitis, inflammation of the gut, hair loss, depression and hallucinations. When pyruvate carboxylase is blocked, pyruvate also backs up and is turned into lactate.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (Pyruvate Carboxylase: in mitochondriaFormation of OAA from Pyruvate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":47,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":16,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":26,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":12,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":26,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":12,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"53"},{"id":"F0319-40-1","s":"Biotin defect may be associated with:","o":["A. rusty brown ring around cornea","B. hyperglycemia","C. hyperuricemia","D. darkening of helix","E. sweet sickly urine smell"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"hyperuricemia","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. hyperuricemia.</b> Hyperuricemia means a high level of uric acid in the blood. The link from biotin to uric acid has three steps.</p><ol><li>Biotin is the cofactor of <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b>, the enzyme that turns pyruvate into oxaloacetate.</li><li>When biotin is lacking, pyruvate cannot go forward. It backs up and is turned into <b>lactate</b>, so lactate rises in the blood.</li><li>The kidney uses the same transport system to excrete lactate and uric acid. Lactate competes with uric acid, so less uric acid is excreted and its blood level rises.</li></ol><p><b>The other effect of the same block.</b> Pyruvate carboxylase is the first step of gluconeogenesis, the making of new glucose. So a biotin defect lowers blood glucose during fasting. It gives hypoglycemia, never hyperglycemia. Low glucose and high lactate come from the same blocked step.</p><p><b>What biotin does in general.</b> It carries carbon dioxide for four carboxylase enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase (gluconeogenesis), acetyl CoA carboxylase (fatty acid synthesis), propionyl CoA carboxylase and methylcrotonyl CoA carboxylase (breakdown of certain amino acids and odd-chain fats).</p><p><b>How a person becomes biotin deficient.</b> Biotin is plentiful in food and is also made by gut bacteria, so a poor diet rarely causes deficiency.</p><ul><li><b>Raw eggs.</b> Raw egg white contains avidin, a protein that binds biotin very tightly and blocks its absorption. It takes many raw eggs (about 20 a day, or 2 a day for months). Cooking destroys avidin.</li><li><b>Inherited defects</b> of the enzymes that attach biotin to its carboxylases or recycle it.</li></ul><p><b>Signs of any biotin deficiency.</b> Dermatitis, hair loss, gut inflammation, depression and hallucinations.</p><p><b>The disease behind each of the other findings.</b> A rusty brown ring around the cornea is Wilson disease (copper overload). A darkened ear cartilage is alkaptonuria. A sweet-smelling urine is maple syrup urine disease.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (Pyruvate Carboxylase: in mitochondriaFormation of OAA from Pyruvate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":48,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":17,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":47,"f":"G099"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":40,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"54"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":26,"label":"Late-onset multiple carboxylase deficiency = biotinidase (frees biotin from biocytin)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-33.jpg","cap":"Biotin cycle: biotinidase cleaves biocytin to free biotin; holocarboxylase synthetase attaches it","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G031-34.jpg","cap":"Early onset = holocarboxylase synthetase; late onset = biotinidase, which responds to biotin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G090-28-1","s":"A 9-month-old infant develops seizures, hypotonia, alopecia, and scaly dermatitis. Laboratory studies show metabolic acidosis and reduced activity of several biotin-dependent carboxylases. The symptoms improve with biotin supplementation. Which reaction is most likely impaired?","o":["A. Cleavage of biocytin to release free biotin","B. Attachment of biotin to apocarboxylases","C. Transfer of CO₂ by pyruvate carboxylase","D. Conversion of riboflavin to FAD","E. Absorption of the vitamin B₁₂–intrinsic factor complex"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cleavage of biocytin to release free biotin","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Cleavage of biocytin to release free biotin.</b> This is the job of the enzyme <b>biotinidase</b>. The infant has low activity of several biotin-dependent carboxylases at once, which is called multiple carboxylase deficiency. He became ill at 9 months, which is the late-onset form, and he improved with biotin. Late onset plus a response to biotin points to biotinidase deficiency.</p><p><b>The biotin cycle.</b> Biotin is used over and over.</p><ol><li><b>Loading.</b> The enzyme holocarboxylase synthetase attaches free biotin to a carboxylase that has none (an apocarboxylase). This makes the active enzyme (a holocarboxylase). All four biotin enzymes are loaded this way: pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl CoA carboxylase, propionyl CoA carboxylase and methylcrotonyl CoA carboxylase.</li><li><b>Breakdown.</b> When a carboxylase wears out, it is digested. What is left is <b>biocytin</b>, which is biotin still attached to a lysine.</li><li><b>Recycling.</b> <b>Biotinidase</b> cuts biocytin and releases free biotin, which can be used again. Biotinidase also frees the biotin that is bound to protein in food.</li></ol><p><b>The two inherited defects of the cycle.</b></p><ul><li><b>Holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency: early onset.</b> No carboxylase can be loaded from the start, so symptoms begin within days of birth.</li><li><b>Biotinidase deficiency: late onset.</b> The carboxylases are loaded normally at first. But biotin cannot be recycled or freed from food, so the supply drains away slowly. Symptoms first appear after one month of age.</li></ul><p><b>Why biotin treats it.</b> Supplemental biotin is already free, so it bypasses the missing recycling step. Biotinidase deficiency always responds to it.</p><p><b>Why the symptoms.</b> With all four carboxylases slow, pyruvate and other organic acids build up, which causes the metabolic acidosis. The nervous system is affected (seizures, low muscle tone), and so are the skin and hair (scaly dermatitis, hair loss, called alopecia). Notice that a defect in pyruvate carboxylase alone would not lower the other carboxylases. Only a problem with biotin itself lowers them all.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (Early-onset)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Free biotin is formed from dietary sources or from the cleavage of biocytin by t)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":28,"f":"G090"}],"tier":1,"n":"55"}]},{"l":"BIO-C5","u":26,"label":"Inherited biotin recycling defect → dermatitis, alopecia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-35.jpg","cap":"Biotin deficiency: dermatitis, enteritis, alopecia, depression, hallucinations, with photos","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/G031-34.jpg","cap":"Biotinidase and holocarboxylase synthetase defects cause multiple carboxylase deficiency","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-26-1","s":"A 2-year old boy born to parents who are second cousins is referred to the Dermatology clinic for severe rashes all over the body of one year duration. The parents also observed increased restlessness and mental deterioration. Relevant findings on PE include alopecia and diffused eczematous rash all over the body especially in the neck, trunk and genital areas. Neurologic exam marked irritability and ataxia (wide-based gait). A genetic defect leading to the deficiency of which of the following vitamins could best explain the patient’s symptoms described above?","o":["A. Riboflavin","B. Folic acid","C. Biotin","D. Cobalamin","E. Thiamine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Biotin","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Biotin.</b> Hair loss (alopecia) and a widespread eczema-like rash, together with nervous system signs such as irritability and an unsteady, wide-based walk (ataxia), are the signs of <b>biotin deficiency</b>. In a young child of related parents the cause is an inherited defect. The usual one is <b>biotinidase deficiency</b>, in which the body cannot recycle its biotin.</p><p><b>Reading the clues.</b></p><ul><li><b>Parents who are second cousins:</b> related parents can both carry the same rare faulty gene. This points to an autosomal recessive disease.</li><li><b>Symptoms that began at about 1 year of age:</b> this is the late-onset pattern. The child is born with enough biotin, and the supply runs down slowly.</li><li><b>Rash plus hair loss plus neurologic signs:</b> the typical trio of biotin deficiency.</li></ul><p><b>Why a recycling defect causes deficiency.</b> Biotin is attached to four carboxylase enzymes. When those enzymes wear out and are digested, biotin is left stuck to a lysine, as a fragment called biocytin. The enzyme biotinidase cuts the biotin free so that it can be used again. Biotinidase also frees biotin from the proteins in food. Without the enzyme, biotin is steadily lost, and the child becomes deficient even on a normal diet. All four biotin enzymes slow down together, which is called multiple carboxylase deficiency.</p><p><b>Treatment.</b> Biotin supplements. Free biotin does not need to be recycled, so it works every time.</p><p><b>Biotin's normal job.</b> It carries carbon dioxide for the carboxylases that start gluconeogenesis (pyruvate carboxylase) and fatty acid synthesis (acetyl CoA carboxylase), and for two enzymes that break down amino acids.</p><p><b>The non-inherited cause.</b> Eating many raw eggs. Raw egg white contains avidin, which binds biotin tightly and blocks its absorption.</p><p><b>The sign that marks each vitamin on the list.</b> Biotin: rash and hair loss. Riboflavin: cracks at the corners of the mouth and a sore tongue. Folic acid and cobalamin: anemia with large red cells. Thiamine: heart failure and nerve damage (beriberi).</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (Early-onset)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 35 (Biotin deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":5,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":26,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":9,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":45,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":10,"f":"F2435"}],"tier":1,"n":"56"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":27,"label":"AIP = porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-20.jpg","cap":"Acute intermittent porphyria: porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency; aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen accumulate","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G026-18.jpg","cap":"Chart pairing each heme-pathway enzyme with its porphyria","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0179-8-1","s":"A 25-year-old female who is an outdoors enthusiast presents to the emergency department with vague abdominal pain. Physical exam is remarkable only for diffuse abdominal pain with no rebound or localizing signs. Her urine is dark red-brown (port-wine colored) and tests positive for aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen. A deficiency in which of the following enzymes is most likely responsible for this patient's symptoms?","o":["A. ALA Dehydratase","B. Porphobilinogen deaminase","C. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","D. Ferrochelatase","E. Hemoglobin beta chain"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphobilinogen deaminase","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Porphobilinogen deaminase.</b> Her urine contains two early building blocks of heme: aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen (PBG). When both pile up, the block must be at the very next step, the enzyme that uses up PBG. That enzyme is <b>porphobilinogen deaminase</b>. Its deficiency is called <b>acute intermittent porphyria</b>.</p><p><b>The rule for every porphyria.</b> A porphyria is an inherited defect in one enzyme of heme synthesis. The molecules made before the blocked step pile up, and they cause the symptoms. So to find the enzyme, ask what is piling up and look one step further.</p><p><b>The first steps of heme synthesis.</b></p><ol><li><b>ALA synthase</b> joins glycine and succinyl CoA to make ALA. This is the rate-limiting step. It takes place in the mitochondria and needs vitamin B6.</li><li><b>ALA dehydratase</b> joins two ALA molecules to make PBG.</li><li><b>PBG deaminase</b> joins four PBG molecules into a chain called hydroxymethylbilane. <b>This is her block.</b> ALA and PBG back up behind it.</li><li>Later enzymes close the chain into a ring and trim it. They include uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase. The last enzyme, ferrochelatase, inserts iron to make heme.</li></ol><p><b>Why she has abdominal pain and no skin problem.</b> ALA and PBG are toxic to nerves, both the nerves of the abdomen and the brain. So an attack brings severe abdominal pain with a nearly normal exam, and often anxiety, confusion or seizures. ALA and PBG are small and colorless, so they do not absorb light. That is why there is <b>no sensitivity to sunlight</b>. The detail that she is an outdoors enthusiast tells you that sunlight does not bother her.</p><p><b>The urine.</b> Fresh urine may look normal. On standing in light and air, the PBG in it darkens to a red-brown, port-wine color.</p><p><b>Blocks at other steps look different.</b> A block at ALA dehydratase raises ALA only, because PBG is never made. A block at uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase raises uroporphyrin and causes sun-blistered skin (porphyria cutanea tarda). A block at ferrochelatase raises protoporphyrin and also causes sun-sensitive skin.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 6 picture (not needed to answer): two tubes held side by side, clear water on the left and dark red-brown (port-wine) urine on the right","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))","L31 Heme slide 11 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":6,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":8,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":8,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":5,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":5,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":6,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":8,"f":"F2421"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":2,"f":"F0293"}],"tier":1,"n":"57"},{"id":"G107-3-1","s":"A young man who enjoys outdoors and the open sky has a history of seizures is seen in the ER with complaints of confusion and stomach pain. ER doctor examines his urine sample and notices a reddish brown color. Which of the following enzymes likely to be deficient in this individual?","o":["A. Homogentisate oxidase","B. Tyrosinase","C. Ferrochelatase","D. Hydroxymethylbilane synthase","E. Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hydroxymethylbilane synthase","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Hydroxymethylbilane synthase.</b> This is a second name for <b>porphobilinogen deaminase</b>. The enzyme joins four molecules of porphobilinogen (PBG) into a chain called hydroxymethylbilane, which is how it gets this name. Its deficiency is <b>acute intermittent porphyria</b>. The picture fits: abdominal pain, confusion, seizures and reddish-brown urine in a man who is comfortable in the sun.</p><p><b>Why these symptoms.</b> The enzyme is the third step of heme synthesis. When it is weak, the two molecules made before it pile up: aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and PBG. Both are toxic to nerves. In the abdomen that causes pain. In the brain it causes anxiety, confusion, seizures and even delirium. ALA and PBG are colorless and do not absorb light, so the skin is spared. A porphyria with skin blisters in sunlight is a block later in the pathway, where colored ring molecules (porphyrins) pile up.</p><p><b>Why the disease is \"intermittent.\"</b> The weak enzyme keeps up most of the time. An attack comes when something pushes the pathway hard.</p><ol><li>The classic triggers are <b>barbiturates and other seizure drugs, anesthetics and alcohol</b>. The liver breaks these down with cytochrome P450 enzymes, which contain heme.</li><li>The liver makes more P450, and that uses up its heme.</li><li>Heme normally holds back ALA synthase, the first and rate-limiting enzyme of the pathway. When heme falls, ALA synthase is released from this control and makes a flood of ALA.</li><li>The flood jams at the weak enzyme. ALA and PBG surge, and the attack begins.</li></ol><p>His history of seizures matters for this reason. Seizure drugs are common triggers, and seizures can also be part of an attack.</p><p><b>Treatment.</b> The aim is to switch ALA synthase back off.</p><ul><li><b>Hemin</b> given into a vein supplies heme, which represses ALA synthase.</li><li><b>Glucose</b> in large amounts also represses it. Fasting does the opposite and can bring on an attack.</li><li>Pain relief, and avoiding the trigger drugs and alcohol.</li></ul><p><b>The urine.</b> It can look normal when fresh and turns red-brown on standing in light and air.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 19 (Porphyrias)","L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 18 Inborn and Heme Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit): the 2 heme questions","sl":1,"f":"G107"}],"tier":1,"n":"58"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":28,"label":"Photosensitivity + uroporphyrins → PCT","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-21.jpg","cap":"Porphyria cutanea tarda: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency, blistered sun-exposed skin, red urine","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G026-23.jpg","cap":"Table: porphyria cutanea tarda cutaneous with high uroporphyrins; acute intermittent porphyria neurological","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-13-1","s":"A 40-year old single, Caucasian female presents in the emergency room in an agitated state, complaining of severe abdominal pain and a rash. The patient’s history reveals similar past episodes with the severe skin rashes when exposed to sunlight. Her physical examination and laboratory tests appear largely normal, with the exception of high levels of uroporphyrin III in the urine and a photosensitive skin. What is the likely diagnosis of this patient based on her clinical presentation?","o":["A. Porphyria Cutanea Tarda","B. Acute Intermittent Porphyria","C. Phenylketonuria","D. Acute lead poisoning","E. Alkaptonuria"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphyria Cutanea Tarda","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Porphyria Cutanea Tarda.</b> Two findings decide this: her skin reacts to <b>sunlight</b>, and her urine has a high level of <b>uroporphyrin</b>. Porphyria cutanea tarda is a deficiency of the enzyme uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, the fifth step of heme synthesis. The molecule just before that step, uroporphyrinogen, piles up and turns into uroporphyrin.</p><p><b>Why a late block damages the skin.</b> The later intermediates of heme synthesis are ring-shaped molecules. When they pile up they are oxidized to porphyrins, which are colored and absorb light. A porphyrin in the skin absorbs light, passes the energy to oxygen and creates oxygen radicals. The radicals damage the skin cells. The result is blisters and scars on sun-exposed skin, especially the hands and face.</p><p><b>Early blocks are the opposite.</b> In acute intermittent porphyria the block is early, at porphobilinogen deaminase. Aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen pile up. They are colorless and toxic to nerves. That disease causes attacks of abdominal pain and mental symptoms, with <b>no</b> photosensitivity and no rise in uroporphyrin. So: early block, nerves. Late block, skin.</p><p><b>Key facts about porphyria cutanea tarda.</b></p><ul><li>It is the <b>most common</b> porphyria.</li><li>It is chronic, and it starts in mid-life, in the 40s and 50s. \"Tarda\" means late.</li><li>The enzyme deficiency alone often causes no disease. It is brought out by iron overload, alcohol, estrogen, sunlight, hepatitis B or C, or HIV.</li><li>The urine is red-brown in natural light and pink-red under fluorescent light.</li><li>Treatment: avoid sunlight and use sunscreens that block visible light, remove blood (phlebotomy) to lower iron, and take β-carotene, which mops up radicals.</li></ul><p><b>The porphyrias in pathway order.</b></p><ul><li><b>Porphobilinogen deaminase</b> (acute intermittent porphyria): ALA and porphobilinogen pile up. Acute attacks, no skin signs.</li><li><b>Uroporphyrinogen III synthase</b> (congenital erythropoietic porphyria): skin disease from childhood.</li><li><b>Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase</b> (porphyria cutanea tarda): uroporphyrin piles up. Chronic skin disease in mid-life.</li><li><b>Ferrochelatase</b> (erythropoietic protoporphyria): protoporphyrin piles up. Skin disease from childhood.</li></ul><p>Lead poisoning is an acquired block. Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, so aminolevulinic acid and protoporphyrin rise, not uroporphyrin.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )","L31 Heme slide 23 (| AIP | PCT |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 18 AA Inborn and Heme Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded): the 3 heme questions","sl":6,"f":"G121"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":13,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2019 clicker","n":"old Exam 3 clicker questions: heme part only","sl":22,"f":"F0034"}],"tier":1,"n":"59"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":29,"label":"Free bilirubin enters the brain: kernicterus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-30.jpg","cap":"Sulfonamides and salicylates displace bilirubin from albumin; hydrophobic bilirubin enters brain: kernicterus","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 30"},{"src":"../sl/G026-43.jpg","cap":"Loosely bound bilirubin crosses the blood-brain barrier, causing kernicterus in newborns","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-17-1","s":"A 26-year old woman in her third trimester of pregnancy self medicated herself with sulfamethoxazole for urinary tract infection. Sulfamethoxazole is contraindicated in late pregnancy because it binds to albumin and can lead to kernicterus in the fetus. The plasma concentration of which of the following unbound intermediates of heme degradation would be affected by sulfamethoxazole administration?","o":["A. Biliverdin","B. Bilirubin","C. Bilirubin diglucuronide","D. Urobilin","E. Urobilinogen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bilirubin","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Bilirubin.</b> Bilirubin here means unconjugated bilirubin, the form that travels in the blood attached to albumin. Sulfamethoxazole is a sulfonamide drug. It binds the same site on albumin and <b>pushes bilirubin off</b>. The bilirubin that is now unbound (free) is fat-soluble, so it can cross into the baby's brain and damage it. That brain damage is called <b>kernicterus</b>.</p><p><b>Where bilirubin comes from.</b> Red blood cells live about 120 days. Old ones are broken down by macrophages in the spleen and liver.</p><ol><li>The enzyme heme oxygenase opens the heme ring. This gives green <b>biliverdin</b>, and releases iron and carbon monoxide.</li><li>Biliverdin reductase turns biliverdin into orange-yellow <b>bilirubin</b>. This is unconjugated bilirubin. It does not dissolve in water.</li><li>Bilirubin leaves the macrophage and travels in the blood bound to <b>albumin</b>.</li><li>The liver takes it up and attaches two glucuronic acid molecules. This makes <b>bilirubin diglucuronide</b> (conjugated bilirubin), which is water-soluble and goes into the bile.</li><li>In the gut, bacteria turn it into <b>urobilinogen</b>. Most becomes stercobilin, the brown color of stool. A little is reabsorbed and leaves in the urine as <b>urobilin</b>, the yellow color of urine.</li></ol><p>Of all the molecules on this list, only unconjugated bilirubin depends on albumin for transport. That is why it is the one affected.</p><p><b>Albumin's limit.</b> Albumin has a high-affinity site that can hold about 25 mg of bilirubin per 100 mL of plasma. Above that, bilirubin sits on a weak second site and comes off easily. Drugs that carry a negative charge, such as <b>sulfonamides and salicylates</b> (aspirin), compete for albumin and displace bilirubin even at lower levels.</p><p><b>Why the newborn is at risk.</b> A newborn's liver is slow to conjugate bilirubin, because the conjugating enzyme is low for about the first month. So a newborn already has extra unconjugated bilirubin. Free bilirubin settles in the brain, especially in the basal ganglia, and causes lasting damage. For this reason sulfonamides and salicylates are not given late in pregnancy or to newborns.</p><p><b>One more consequence of albumin binding.</b> Unconjugated bilirubin on albumin cannot be filtered by the kidney. It never appears in the urine.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 18 AA Inborn and Heme Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded): the 3 heme questions","sl":8,"f":"G121"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":17,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2019 clicker","n":"old Exam 3 clicker questions: heme part only","sl":24,"f":"F0034"}],"tier":1,"n":"60"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":29,"label":"Gut bacteria: urobilinogen → stercobilin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-34.jpg","cap":"β-glucuronidases free bilirubin; bacteria make urobilinogen, oxidized to stercobilin (brown stool)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/G026-35.jpg","cap":"Overview: urobilinogen to urine urobilin or fecal stercobilin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0382-6-1","s":"Selena is a 19-year-old female who consulted for episodes of headache, weakness, and easy fatigability. As the attending physician, you examined her and noticed that she looked pale. You ordered a serum hemoglobin level which turned out to be low. In your mind, you know that the prosthetic group of hemoglobin is heme. Which of the following is true regarding heme metabolism?","o":["A. ALA Dehydratase requires B6 for its activity.","B. PCT is heme synthesis defect leading to accumulation of ALA and PBG.","C. Massive hemolysis may lead to an increase conjugated bilirubin.","D. Bilirubin Glucuronidase is essential for urobilinogen synthesis in the intestine.","E. Lead decreases the synthesis of heme by binding with aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthetase."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Bilirubin Glucuronidase is essential for urobilinogen synthesis in the intestine.","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Bilirubin Glucuronidase is essential for urobilinogen synthesis in the intestine.</b> The liver sends bilirubin into the bile with two glucuronic acid molecules attached (bilirubin diglucuronide). In the intestine, bacteria make an enzyme, <b>β-glucuronidase</b>, that removes the glucuronic acid. Only then can the bacteria reduce the freed bilirubin to <b>urobilinogen</b>. So the glucuronidase is needed to make urobilinogen. The option calls the enzyme bilirubin glucuronidase.</p><p><b>The whole path of bilirubin, in order.</b></p><ol><li><b>Formation.</b> Macrophages break down old red cells. Heme is opened to biliverdin and then reduced to bilirubin. This is unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin.</li><li><b>Transport.</b> It does not dissolve in water, so it travels on albumin.</li><li><b>Uptake.</b> The liver cell takes it up from the blood.</li><li><b>Conjugation.</b> The enzyme bilirubin glucuronyl transferase (also called UGT) adds glucuronic acid twice. The product, bilirubin diglucuronide, is water-soluble. This is conjugated (direct) bilirubin.</li><li><b>Secretion into bile.</b> This is active transport, and it is the slowest, rate-limiting step of the liver's handling of bilirubin. Only conjugated bilirubin is secreted.</li><li><b>In the gut.</b> Bacterial β-glucuronidase removes the glucuronic acid, and bacteria reduce bilirubin to colorless urobilinogen.</li></ol><p><b>What happens to urobilinogen.</b></p><ul><li>Most is oxidized to <b>stercobilin</b> and leaves in the feces. Stercobilin gives stool its brown color.</li><li>Some is reabsorbed into the portal blood. Part of that goes back to the liver and into bile again. Part reaches the kidney and leaves in the urine as <b>urobilin</b>, which gives urine its yellow color.</li></ul><p><b>Facts about heme to have straight.</b></p><ul><li>Vitamin B6 is needed by <b>ALA synthase</b>, the first enzyme of heme synthesis. ALA dehydratase, the second enzyme, needs zinc.</li><li><b>Lead</b> blocks ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase. It does not block ALA synthase.</li><li>ALA and porphobilinogen pile up in <b>acute intermittent porphyria</b>. In porphyria cutanea tarda it is uroporphyrin that piles up.</li><li>Massive hemolysis raises <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin, because more bilirubin is made than the liver can conjugate.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 34 (Bilirubin diglucuronide)","L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)","L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":11,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":16,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh recap 2 heme & iron","sl":6,"f":"F0382"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":18,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"61"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":30,"label":"Pre-hepatic jaundice: no urine bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-39.jpg","cap":"Hemolytic jaundice: high unconjugated bilirubin, high urine urobilinogen, no urine bilirubin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 39"},{"src":"../sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Jaundice table: pre-hepatic, hepatic, post-hepatic bilirubin, urine, stool and enzyme findings","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0179-11-1","s":"Fifteen-month-old Myko, known to have glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, was brought to your clinic for severe progressive pallor and jaundice. Prior to the onset of jaundice, Myko was seen playing with several naphthalene balls, with some seen inside his mouth. Suspecting that his jaundice is due to massive hemolysis, which of the following will be observed on urine analysis?","o":["A. Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen 0","B. Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen +2","C. Urine bilirubin +2; urine urobilinogen 0","D. Urine bilirubin +2; urine urobilinogen +2","E. Urine bilirubin +4; urine urobilinogen +4"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen +2","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen +2.</b> Massive hemolysis (the bursting of red blood cells) releases a large amount of heme, and macrophages turn it into bilirubin. This bilirubin is the <b>unconjugated</b> form. Take the two urine tests one at a time.</p><ul><li><b>Urine bilirubin is zero.</b> Unconjugated bilirubin does not dissolve in water and travels tightly bound to albumin. The kidney cannot filter it. Only conjugated bilirubin can ever appear in urine.</li><li><b>Urine urobilinogen is raised.</b> His liver is healthy and works at full speed. It conjugates much more bilirubin than usual and sends it into the gut. Gut bacteria turn it into much more urobilinogen. More urobilinogen is reabsorbed into the blood, and more reaches the urine.</li></ul><p><b>Why he hemolyzed.</b> He has glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency. G6PD makes NADPH, an electron carrier that red cells need to defend themselves against oxidizing chemicals. Naphthalene, the chemical in mothballs, is a strong oxidant. Without enough NADPH his red cells are damaged and burst.</p><p><b>This is pre-hepatic jaundice.</b> The problem lies before the liver: too much bilirubin is being made. The liver can handle about 3,000 mg of bilirubin a day and normally receives only about 300 mg, so it takes massive hemolysis to overwhelm it. Other causes are malaria, sickle cell anemia, pyruvate kinase deficiency and hereditary spherocytosis.</p><p><b>The full pattern of pre-hepatic jaundice.</b></p><ul><li>Blood: unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is high.</li><li>Urine: no bilirubin. Urobilinogen is high.</li><li>Stool: normal or dark, because plenty of pigment reaches the gut.</li><li>Liver enzymes: normal.</li></ul><p><b>How to read any urine pattern.</b> Bilirubin in the urine means conjugated bilirubin is backing up into the blood, which happens with liver cell damage or a blocked bile duct. No urobilinogen in the urine means no bile is reaching the gut, which is a complete blockage. Both raised together is the pattern of liver cell damage.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 13 shows the options as a table picture: A = urine bilirubin 0, urobilinogen 0; B = 0, +2; C = +2, 0; D = +2, +2; E = +4, +4 (already written out in the options)","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/10/26: heme, insulin & macronutrients (Dr. Bagh), post-discussion deck","sl":13,"f":"G088"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 1 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":18,"f":"G095"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":11,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":3,"f":"F0293"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":12,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":12,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":16,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":19,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"62"}]},{"l":"BIO-C6","u":31,"label":"Obstructive jaundice: direct bilirubin, clay stools","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G026-41.jpg","cap":"Obstruction: conjugated bilirubin up, bilirubin in urine, no urobilinogen, clay stools","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Post-hepatic column: conjugated bilirubin, absent urobilinogen and stercobilin, high alkaline phosphatase","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-15-1","s":"A 40-year old overweight female presents at the clinic with yellow sclerae, intolerance to fatty foods, pain in the upper right quadrant of the abdomen and passage of clay colored stools. Serum total bilirubin was 20 mg/dL and direct bilirubin was 16 mg/dL. LFT showed elevated ALP. Her urine was dark in color. Which of the following diagnoses is consistent with the above findings? Normal values: Total = < 1.0 mg/dL; Direct = 0.1 - 0.2 mg/dL; Indirect = 0.2 - 0.6 mg/dL","o":["A. Crigler-Najjar syndrome, Type 2","B. Posthepatic jaundice","C. Intrahepatic jaundice","D. Gilbert’s syndrome","E. Prehepatic jaundice"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Posthepatic jaundice","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Posthepatic jaundice.</b> Post-hepatic means the problem is after the liver: the bile duct is blocked. Here the likely cause is a gallstone. Every finding fits a blocked duct.</p><ul><li><b>Most of her bilirubin is direct.</b> Direct means conjugated. She has 16 mg/dL direct out of 20 total. Her liver takes up bilirubin and conjugates it normally. But the conjugated bilirubin cannot drain into the gut, so it leaks back into the blood.</li><li><b>Dark urine.</b> Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble, so the kidney filters it into the urine.</li><li><b>Clay-colored stools.</b> No bilirubin reaches the gut, so no stercobilin, the brown stool pigment, is made.</li><li><b>High ALP.</b> Alkaline phosphatase is an enzyme of the bile ducts. It rises when they are blocked.</li><li><b>Intolerance to fatty foods and pain in the upper right abdomen.</b> Bile is needed to digest fat, and this is where the gallbladder sits. An overweight woman of 40 is a typical gallstone patient.</li></ul><p><b>The three kinds of jaundice.</b> Jaundice is yellow skin and eyes from bilirubin. It shows when total bilirubin is above about 2 mg/dL (normal is below 1).</p><ul><li><b>Pre-hepatic (hemolysis).</b> Too much bilirubin is made. Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is high. No bilirubin in urine. Urine urobilinogen is high. Stool color is normal. Liver enzymes are normal.</li><li><b>Hepatic (damaged liver cells, as in hepatitis or cirrhosis).</b> The liver conjugates poorly and also secretes poorly, so both forms are high. Bilirubin is in the urine, and urobilinogen is still present. The liver cell enzymes ALT and AST (alanine and aspartate transaminase) are high.</li><li><b>Post-hepatic (obstruction by a stone, a narrowing, or a cancer of the head of the pancreas).</b> Conjugated (direct) bilirubin is high. Bilirubin is in the urine. There is no urobilinogen in the urine and the stools are pale. ALP is high.</li></ul><p><b>Inherited causes look different.</b> Gilbert syndrome and Crigler–Najjar syndrome are inherited weaknesses of the conjugating enzyme. They raise <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin, and the stools keep their normal color.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 18 AA Inborn and Heme Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded): the 3 heme questions","sl":7,"f":"G121"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":15,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2019 clicker","n":"old Exam 3 clicker questions: heme part only","sl":23,"f":"F0034"}],"tier":1,"n":"63"},{"id":"G107-4-1","s":"Test results from a jaundiced adult male revealed modest elevation of indirect bilirubin, marked elevation of direct bilirubin, chalky colored stools, and no urobilinogen in the urine. A possible cause of these finding might be:","o":["A. Failure of the liver bilirubin conjugation pathway","B. Failure of the liver to clear urobilinogen from the portal circulation","C. Blockage of the bile duct with cholesterol “stones”","D. Excessive production of bilirubin caused by hemolysis","E. Excessive activity of heme oxygenase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Blockage of the bile duct with cholesterol “stones”","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Blockage of the bile duct with cholesterol “stones”.</b> Follow the bilirubin. His liver is conjugating it, because the direct (conjugated) bilirubin is very high. But nothing is reaching the intestine, because the stools are chalky and there is no urobilinogen in the urine. Conjugated bilirubin that cannot get out must be stuck in a <b>blocked bile duct</b>. This is post-hepatic, or obstructive, jaundice.</p><p><b>Each finding explained.</b></p><ul><li><b>Marked rise in direct bilirubin.</b> The liver keeps conjugating. With the duct blocked, conjugated bilirubin flows back into the blood.</li><li><b>Chalky stools.</b> Stool gets its brown color from stercobilin, which gut bacteria make from bilirubin. No bilirubin in the gut means no stercobilin.</li><li><b>No urobilinogen in the urine.</b> Urobilinogen is made only in the gut, by bacteria acting on bilirubin. Some is normally reabsorbed and passed in the urine. If no bilirubin reaches the gut, none is made at all. This finding means the blockage is complete.</li><li><b>Modest rise in indirect bilirubin.</b> A blockage that lasts damages the liver cells, so they begin to fall behind on conjugation, and some unconjugated bilirubin builds up too.</li></ul><p>His urine would also be dark, because conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and is filtered by the kidney. The bile duct enzyme alkaline phosphatase (ALP) would be high.</p><p><b>What \"direct\" and \"indirect\" mean.</b> The names come from the lab test. Conjugated bilirubin dissolves in water and reacts with the test chemical straight away, so it is called direct. Unconjugated bilirubin reacts only after methanol is added. The lab measures total and direct, and then calculates: indirect = total − direct.</p><p><b>How the other mechanisms would look.</b> The pattern tells them apart.</p><ul><li><b>Too much bilirubin made</b> (hemolysis, or more heme oxygenase activity): indirect bilirubin is high, urine urobilinogen is high and the stools are dark.</li><li><b>Failed conjugation in the liver:</b> indirect bilirubin is high and direct is not.</li><li><b>Blocked duct:</b> direct bilirubin is high, with pale stools and no urine urobilinogen.</li></ul><p><b>Causes of a blocked duct.</b> Gallstones, a narrowing of the duct, a liver tumor, or a cancer of the head of the pancreas pressing on the duct.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 28 (Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 18 Inborn and Heme Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit): the 2 heme questions","sl":1,"f":"G107"}],"tier":1,"n":"64"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":32,"label":"Pyridoxine (B6) → ALA synthase → heme","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-42.jpg","cap":"Pyridoxal phosphate reactions, incl. glycine + succinyl-CoA → delta-aminolevulinic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/G031-43.jpg","cap":"B6 deficiency: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia; ringed sideroblast images","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F2297-25-1","s":"5. Hemoglobin synthesis in the body essentially requires vitamin:","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Niacin","C. Pyridoxine","D. Vitamin E","E. Vitamin K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxine","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Pyridoxine.</b> Pyridoxine is vitamin B6. Hemoglobin is the protein globin plus heme, and heme cannot be made without vitamin B6. The very first step of heme synthesis is carried out by the enzyme <b>ALA synthase</b>, and that enzyme needs the working form of B6, called <b>pyridoxal phosphate</b>, as its coenzyme.</p><p><b>The reaction.</b> ALA synthase joins the amino acid glycine to succinyl CoA and makes δ-aminolevulinic acid (ALA). It happens in the mitochondria, and it is the rate-limiting step of the whole heme pathway. Seven more steps turn ALA into heme. If the first step is slow, little heme is made, so little hemoglobin is made. The red cells come out small and pale. This is a microcytic anemia.</p><p><b>The forms of vitamin B6.</b> Pyridoxine comes from plants. Pyridoxal and pyridoxamine come from animal foods. All three are turned into the one coenzyme, pyridoxal phosphate. The daily need is about 2 mg.</p><p><b>What pyridoxal phosphate does in general.</b> It is the coenzyme for reactions of amino acids. There are four types to know, each with an example.</p><ul><li><b>Transamination:</b> moving an amino group from an amino acid to a keto acid. The transaminases ALT and AST (alanine transaminase and aspartate transaminase) do this.</li><li><b>Deamination:</b> removing an amino group as ammonia, for example serine to pyruvate.</li><li><b>Decarboxylation:</b> removing carbon dioxide, for example histidine to histamine.</li><li><b>Condensation:</b> joining two molecules, as in glycine plus succinyl CoA to ALA.</li></ul><p>It is also the coenzyme of glycogen phosphorylase, the enzyme that breaks down glycogen. So the four B6 enzymes to remember are the transaminases, the decarboxylases, glycogen phosphorylase and ALA synthase.</p><p><b>The main jobs of the other vitamins listed.</b> Vitamin D controls calcium. Niacin makes the electron carrier NAD<sup>+</sup>. Vitamin E is an antioxidant. Vitamin K activates clotting factors.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 42 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":52,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":18,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":25,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":10,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"65"},{"id":"G031-57-1","s":"Sideroblastic anemia is observed in the deficiency of which of the following?","o":["A. Thiamine","B. Pantothenic acid","C. Folic acid","D. Cobalamine","E. Pyridoxine"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxine","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Pyridoxine.</b> Pyridoxine is vitamin B6. Its working form, pyridoxal phosphate, is the coenzyme of ALA synthase, the first enzyme of heme synthesis. Without B6, heme cannot be made, and the iron that was meant for heme piles up inside the red cell precursor. That iron-loaded cell is a <b>sideroblast</b>, and the anemia is called sideroblastic anemia.</p><p><b>How the iron ring forms.</b></p><ol><li>ALA synthase makes δ-aminolevulinic acid (ALA), the first building block of heme. With no B6, little ALA is made.</li><li>So little protoporphyrin, the ring that iron is inserted into, is made further down the pathway.</li><li>Meanwhile the cell keeps bringing iron into its mitochondria, where the last step, inserting iron into the ring, takes place.</li><li>With too few rings to receive it, the iron stays in the mitochondria.</li><li>In a red cell precursor the mitochondria sit in a circle around the nucleus. With an iron stain, the iron granules show up as a ring around the nucleus. These cells are called <b>ringed sideroblasts</b>.</li></ol><p><b>The anemia.</b> Little heme means little hemoglobin, so the red cells are small. It is a microcytic anemia.</p><p><b>Compare iron deficiency.</b> Both give small red cells. In iron deficiency the iron is missing. In sideroblastic anemia iron is plentiful but stuck, because heme cannot be finished.</p><p><b>Other causes of sideroblasts.</b> An inherited loss of the red cell form of ALA synthase gives X-linked sideroblastic anemia. Lead also blocks heme synthesis, but at later steps. So ALA is high in lead poisoning and low in B6 deficiency.</p><p><b>Who becomes B6 deficient.</b> A poor diet alone rarely does it. The classic cause is <b>isoniazid</b>, a tuberculosis drug that binds pyridoxal phosphate and inactivates it. This is why patients on isoniazid are also given B6. Others at risk are people with alcoholism, women on oral contraceptives and newborns on formula low in B6.</p><p><b>Other signs, and toxicity.</b> A greasy, scaly rash beside the nose, a sore tongue and peripheral neuropathy (nerve damage in the hands and feet). B6 is one of the few water-soluble vitamins that is toxic: more than about 500 mg a day causes a sensory neuropathy.</p><p><b>The anemia of the other vitamins.</b> Folic acid and cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency also cause anemia, but with large red cells (megaloblastic anemia).</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 56 (Summary of Disorders Associated)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":57,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"66"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":33,"label":"Tetrahydrofolate → de novo purine ring","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"N10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate feeds purine synthesis; methylene form makes thymidylate","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"G096-71-1","s":"Vegetarian consume green leafy vegetables, a source rich in vitamin folic acid. Folic acid in the form of Tetrahydrofolate (THF) is essential for the synthesis of:","o":["A. Purine","B. Homocysteine","C. Oxaloacetate","D. Acetyl CoA","E. Heme"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Purine","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Purine.</b> Purines are the bases adenine and guanine of DNA and RNA. The purine ring is built piece by piece, and two of its carbons are delivered by folate. The working form of folate is <b>tetrahydrofolate (THF)</b>, and its job is to carry single carbon atoms from one molecule to another.</p><p><b>The one-carbon pool.</b> THF holds one carbon on its nitrogens, numbered N5 and N10. The carbon can be carried in several forms, and each form has its own use.</p><ul><li><b>N10-formyl-THF:</b> gives carbons to the <b>purine</b> ring.</li><li><b>N5,N10-methylene-THF:</b> gives a carbon to make <b>thymidylate</b>, the thymine building block that only DNA uses.</li><li><b>N5-methyl-THF:</b> gives its methyl group to homocysteine to make <b>methionine</b>. This step also needs vitamin B12.</li></ul><p>The carbons come mainly from the amino acid serine, and also from glycine and histidine.</p><p><b>Why this matters.</b> Purines and thymidylate are both needed to copy DNA. Without folate, a cell cannot copy its DNA and cannot divide. The cells that divide fastest suffer first, especially the blood-forming cells of the bone marrow. The result is megaloblastic anemia, with large red cells.</p><p><b>Notice the direction for homocysteine.</b> Folate helps <b>use up</b> homocysteine by turning it into methionine. It does not make homocysteine. In folate deficiency homocysteine rises.</p><p><b>Folate basics.</b> Leafy green vegetables are the main source. The daily need is 400 micrograms. Body stores are small, so deficiency can appear within weeks. It is probably the most common vitamin deficiency in the United States.</p><p><b>The vitamin needed for the other products listed.</b> Oxaloacetate is made by pyruvate carboxylase, which needs biotin. Acetyl CoA is made by pyruvate dehydrogenase, which needs thiamine and four other cofactors. Heme synthesis starts with ALA synthase, which needs vitamin B6.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":71,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":20,"f":"G098"}],"tier":1,"n":"67"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":33,"label":"Folic acid → tetrahydrofolate, the one-carbon carrier","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"Tetrahydrofolate forms carry formyl, methylene and methyl groups to purines, thymidylate and methionine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-2-1","s":"The coenzymes involved in the transfer of formyl, methyl, methylene or hydroxymethyl groups from one molecule to another are derivative of:","o":["A. Biotin","B. Pyridoxine","C. Methionine","D. Folic acid","E. Thiamine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Folic acid","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Folic acid.</b> Formyl, methyl, methylene and hydroxymethyl groups are all groups with <b>one carbon</b>. The coenzyme that carries one-carbon groups is <b>tetrahydrofolate (THF)</b>, which the body makes from folic acid.</p><p><b>Activating folic acid.</b> Folic acid from food is not active. The enzyme dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) reduces it in two steps: folic acid to dihydrofolate, then dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate. Each step uses one NADPH as the hydrogen donor.</p><p><b>The forms of THF and what each carbon is used for.</b></p><ul><li><b>N10-formyl-THF</b> (carries a formyl group): makes purines.</li><li><b>N5,N10-methylene-THF</b> (carries a methylene group): makes thymidylate for DNA.</li><li><b>N5-methyl-THF</b> (carries a methyl group): gives the methyl to homocysteine to make methionine, with the help of vitamin B12.</li></ul><p>The one-carbon groups come from serine, glycine, histidine, formate and formaldehyde. Most forms of THF can be converted into one another.</p><p><b>Two drugs that act on this pathway.</b></p><ul><li><b>Sulfonamides.</b> Bacteria cannot take up folate. They make their own from a small molecule called PABA (para-aminobenzoic acid). Sulfonamides look like PABA and block the bacterial enzyme. Humans do not make folate, so human cells are spared.</li><li><b>Methotrexate.</b> It blocks dihydrofolate reductase in human cells. THF cannot be regenerated, DNA synthesis stops and dividing cells die. It is used to treat cancers such as childhood leukemia, and it can cause folate deficiency.</li></ul><p><b>Each carrier coenzyme carries its own kind of group.</b></p><ul><li>Tetrahydrofolate, from folic acid: one-carbon groups.</li><li>Biotin: carbon dioxide, for carboxylation.</li><li>Pyridoxal phosphate, from pyridoxine (B6): amino groups.</li><li>Thiamine pyrophosphate, from thiamine (B1): removes carbon dioxide from keto acids and moves two-carbon pieces.</li><li>S-adenosylmethionine, made from the amino acid methionine: methyl groups only. Methionine is an amino acid, not a vitamin.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"68"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":33,"label":"Folate deficiency → megaloblastic anemia (slow DNA)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-45.jpg","cap":"Megaloblastic anemia: too few purines and thymidine, so cells can't make DNA","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"Folate forms feeding purine and thymidylate synthesis; deficiency gives megaloblastic anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-57-1","s":"Megaloblastic anemia is caused by abnormally slow synthesis of :","o":["A. S-adenosylmethionine.","B. dUMP.","C. folic acid.","D. hemoglobin.","E. DNA."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"DNA.","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. DNA.</b> Megaloblastic anemia is an anemia with large red cells and large, immature-looking precursors in the bone marrow, called megaloblasts. The cause is always the same: the cells <b>cannot make DNA fast enough</b>. The usual reasons are a lack of folate or a lack of vitamin B12.</p><p><b>Why slow DNA synthesis makes cells large.</b> A cell that is preparing to divide does two things at once. It grows, by making RNA and protein. And it copies its DNA. When DNA synthesis is slow, the cell keeps growing but cannot divide on time. The cytoplasm gets ahead of the nucleus. The marrow releases fewer cells, and the ones it releases are too big. The mean corpuscular volume (MCV), which is the average size of a red cell, rises above 100 femtoliters.</p><p><b>Why folate is needed for DNA.</b> DNA uses the base thymine where RNA uses uracil. The cell makes the thymine building block, called thymidylate or dTMP, by adding one carbon to dUMP, the matching uracil building block. The enzyme is thymidylate synthase. The carbon comes from a form of folate, N5,N10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate. Folate also supplies carbons for the purine bases. Short of folate, the cell lacks both and cannot copy its DNA. In this state dUMP builds up. It is the product made from it, dTMP, that is too slow.</p><p><b>Where vitamin B12 comes in.</b> Without B12, folate becomes trapped in a form that cannot be used for DNA. So B12 deficiency causes the same anemia.</p><p><b>Other cells show it too.</b> All fast-dividing cells are affected. The tongue becomes smooth and sore. Neutrophils become hypersegmented, which means their nucleus has more than 5 lobes.</p><p><b>Compare the small-cell anemias.</b> When the slow step is <b>hemoglobin</b> synthesis, as in iron deficiency or vitamin B6 deficiency, the cells are small (microcytic). Slow DNA gives big cells. Slow hemoglobin gives small cells.</p><p><b>Folate and pregnancy.</b> The neural tube, the future brain and spinal cord, closes 17 to 30 days after conception. This needs rapid cell division. Too little folate at that time causes neural tube defects such as spina bifida and anencephaly. Every woman who could become pregnant should take 0.4 mg of folic acid a day.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":73,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":22,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":57,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"69"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":34,"label":"B12 made only by microbes; two active forms","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-47.jpg","cap":"Cobalamin structure; made only by microorganisms, absent in plants, vegans need supplements","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 47"},{"src":"../sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"Methylcobalamin and deoxyadenosylcobalamin reactions","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-8-1","s":"An examination of a four-year-old boy raised as a strict vegetarian (no animal products at all) revealed a child whose weight and height were at the 20th percentile of his age group. He was anemic with a significant number of megaloblasts in his peripheral circulation. It is most likely that this boy is deficient in which of the following vitamins?","o":["A. Biotin","B. Pyridoxine","C. Riboflavin","D. Thiamine","E. Cobalamin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cobalamin","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Cobalamin.</b> Cobalamin is vitamin B12. It is made only by microorganisms, and it is found in <b>animal foods only</b>: liver, meat, fish, eggs and dairy. Plants contain none. A child raised with no animal products at all gets no B12. The megaloblasts in his blood are large, immature red cell precursors, and they are the mark of megaloblastic anemia, which B12 deficiency causes.</p><p><b>Why B12 deficiency gives megaloblasts.</b> Megaloblasts appear when cells cannot make DNA fast enough. DNA synthesis needs folate. Vitamin B12 is needed by the enzyme methionine synthase, which is the only enzyme that can release folate from its methyl form. Without B12, the body's folate becomes stuck in that one form and cannot be used to make DNA. This is called the folate trap. So B12 deficiency causes the same large-cell anemia as folate deficiency.</p><p><b>Why it is B12 and not folate in a vegetarian.</b> Folate deficiency also gives megaloblasts. But folate is plentiful in green leafy vegetables, so a vegetarian diet is rich in it. B12 is the vitamin that a plant-only diet lacks.</p><p><b>Facts about B12.</b></p><ul><li>It has a cobalt atom at the center of a ring, which is where the name cobalamin comes from.</li><li>The pill form is cyanocobalamin. The two working forms in the body are methylcobalamin and deoxyadenosylcobalamin.</li><li>The daily need is tiny, 2 to 4 micrograms.</li><li>The liver stores 2 to 5 mg. That is enough for years, so an adult who stops eating animal foods takes years to become deficient. A young child has had little time to build a store, so a child raised this way is affected sooner.</li><li>People who eat no animal foods need supplements.</li></ul><p><b>What else B12 deficiency does.</b> Unlike folate deficiency, it damages nerves: tingling of the hands and feet, poor coordination and memory loss. Poor growth, as in this child, reflects slow cell division throughout the body.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"70"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":34,"label":"Large liver B12 stores: deficiency takes years to appear","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-47.jpg","cap":"Daily need is only 2–4 micrograms, so milligram liver stores last for years","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 47"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-4","s":"In cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency from a strict vegetarian diet, megaloblastic anemia usually develops slowly and takes 2–5 years to develop because:","o":["A. It involves peripheral nerves.","B. Slow absorption of cobalamin","C. Relatively large body cobalamin stores","D. Absence of clinical indicators to assess deficiency.","E. Excessive Intrinsic Factor Secretion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Relatively large body cobalamin stores","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Relatively large body cobalamin stores.</b> Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is unusual among the water-soluble vitamins because the body <b>stores</b> it. The liver holds 2 to 5 milligrams. The body uses only 2 to 4 micrograms a day. One milligram is 1,000 micrograms, so the store is about a thousand times the daily need. When intake stops, the store lasts for years before the deficiency shows.</p><p><b>Compare folate.</b> Folate stores are small. Folate deficiency appears within weeks to a few months of a poor diet. This difference in timing is a common exam point: B12 deficiency from diet is slow, folate deficiency is fast.</p><p><b>Diet versus failed absorption.</b> B12 deficiency from a strict vegetarian diet takes years. Deficiency from failed absorption comes on faster. The reason is that the body recycles B12. B12 is passed into the bile and then reabsorbed in the gut. When absorption fails, this recycled B12 is lost as well as the B12 from food, so the store drains more quickly.</p><p><b>Where B12 comes from.</b> Only microorganisms make it. It is found in animal foods (liver, meat, fish, eggs, dairy) and not in plants. So people who eat no animal foods need supplements.</p><p><b>How B12 is absorbed.</b> Stomach acid frees B12 from food protein. It then binds intrinsic factor, a protein made by the parietal cells of the stomach. The complex of B12 and intrinsic factor is absorbed in the ileum, the last part of the small intestine. With intrinsic factor, absorption works well. Most B12 deficiency in practice comes from a failure of one of these steps, not from diet.</p><p><b>What the deficiency finally causes.</b> Megaloblastic anemia with large red cells, and nerve damage (tingling, poor coordination, memory loss). The nerve damage is a result of the deficiency. It has nothing to do with how long the deficiency takes to appear.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"71"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":34,"label":"Lost acid or intrinsic factor → B12 deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-48.jpg","cap":"Stomach acid frees B12 from food; B12 binds haptocorrin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 48"},{"src":"../sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells; cubilin uptake in ileum; transcobalamin in blood","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"B12 deficiency causes: achlorhydria, proton pump inhibitors, autoimmune parietal-cell loss","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-2","s":"A diet with no animal foods (strict vegetarian) can cause cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency. Which of the following may also be associated with cobalamin deficiency?","o":["A. 15-year female with partial Gastrectomy","B. A pregnant female on low green diet","C. A 25-year man with Chronic renal failure","D. 10-year-old with bleeding gums and falling out of teeth","E. 5-year-old boy with bowlegs"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"15-year female with partial Gastrectomy","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. 15-year female with partial Gastrectomy.</b> Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency has two kinds of cause: not eating it, and not absorbing it. Absorption depends on the <b>stomach</b>. The parietal cells of the stomach make two things that B12 absorption needs: acid and <b>intrinsic factor</b>. A gastrectomy removes part of the stomach, so both are reduced, and B12 cannot be absorbed properly.</p><p><b>How B12 is absorbed, step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In food, B12 is bound to proteins. <b>Stomach acid and pepsin</b> free it.</li><li>The free B12 binds <b>R-protein</b> (haptocorrin), a protein from saliva that protects it from the acid.</li><li>In the duodenum, <b>pancreatic enzymes</b> digest the R-protein and release the B12.</li><li>B12 now binds <b>intrinsic factor</b>, which the parietal cells of the stomach made.</li><li>The B12–intrinsic factor complex travels to the <b>ileum</b>. There it binds a receptor called cubilin and is taken into the cell.</li><li>In the blood, B12 travels on a carrier called transcobalamin to the liver, where it is stored.</li></ol><p><b>Where the chain breaks.</b></p><ul><li><b>No acid</b> (achlorhydria): B12 stays locked to food protein. This is the usual cause in the elderly. Long-term use of acid-blocking drugs called proton pump inhibitors, such as omeprazole, does the same.</li><li><b>No intrinsic factor:</b> autoimmune destruction of the parietal cells, or gastrectomy.</li><li><b>Pancreatic insufficiency:</b> the R-protein is not digested, so B12 is not released.</li><li><b>Diseased or removed ileum,</b> as in Crohn disease: no site of uptake.</li></ul><p><b>Pernicious anemia.</b> Megaloblastic anemia caused by failed B12 absorption is called pernicious anemia. Its most common cause is autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor. Treatment is lifelong B12 injections into muscle, which bypass the gut. Very high oral doses also work, because about 1% crosses the gut wall without intrinsic factor.</p><p><b>Match each of the other patients to a vitamin.</b> Pregnancy with few green vegetables: folate. Chronic kidney failure: vitamin D, because the kidney activates it. Bleeding gums and loose teeth: vitamin C. Bowed legs in a child: vitamin D.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"72"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":34,"label":"Ileal disease (Crohn) → no B12 uptake → megaloblastic anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"The B12–intrinsic factor complex is absorbed only in the ileum, through cubilin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-10-1","s":"Crohn’s disease causes malabsorption in the ileum. Which one of the following diseases may also be associated with someone who has Crohn’s disease?","o":["A. Megaloblastic anemia","B. Iron deficiency anemia","C. Hemochromatosis","D. Hemosiderosis","E. Gout"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Megaloblastic anemia","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Megaloblastic anemia.</b> The <b>ileum</b>, the last part of the small intestine, is the only place where vitamin B12 is absorbed. Crohn disease damages the ileum, so B12 is not absorbed. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia, an anemia with large red cells.</p><p><b>How B12 gets to the ileum.</b> In the stomach, acid frees B12 from food. B12 is first protected by a carrier from saliva, and in the duodenum it is handed over to <b>intrinsic factor</b>, a protein made by the parietal cells of the stomach. The B12–intrinsic factor complex resists digestion and travels all the way down to the ileum. There it binds a specific receptor, cubilin, and is taken up. If the ileum is diseased or has been removed, nothing can replace this step, and the patient needs B12 injections for life.</p><p><b>Why B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia.</b> Cells need folate to make DNA. Vitamin B12 is needed to release folate from its methyl form, so without B12 the folate is trapped and useless. The bone marrow cannot copy DNA fast enough. Red cell precursors keep growing but cannot divide, so they become large (megaloblasts). B12 deficiency also damages nerves.</p><p><b>Different nutrients are absorbed in different places.</b></p><ul><li><b>Iron:</b> in the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine. So ileal disease is not the classic cause of iron deficiency.</li><li><b>Vitamin B12:</b> in the ileum, with intrinsic factor.</li></ul><p><b>The two iron-overload words in the choices.</b> Hemochromatosis is an inherited disease in which too much iron is absorbed. Hemosiderosis is iron overload from another cause, such as many blood transfusions. Both are about too much iron. They are the opposite of a malabsorption problem.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"73"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":35,"label":"Homocysteine needs B6, folate, B12","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Homocysteine to methionine (B12, folate) and to cysteine via transsulfuration (B6)","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"},{"src":"../sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"Methionine synthase: homocysteine + N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, with methylcobalamin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-12-1","s":"The vitamins essential for Homocysteine metabolism in the body are:","o":["A. Vitamin A, Vitamin D and Vitamin E","B. Folate, Vitamin B12 and Vitamin B6","C. Vitamin C and Vitamin K","D. Vitamin B12 and Vitamin K","E. Vitamin E and Vitamin C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate, Vitamin B12 and Vitamin B6","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Folate, Vitamin B12 and Vitamin B6.</b> Homocysteine is an amino acid that the body must keep clearing away. It has <b>two exits</b>, and between them they need three vitamins.</p><ul><li><b>Exit 1, back to methionine.</b> The enzyme methionine synthase adds a methyl group to homocysteine. The methyl group is supplied by <b>folate</b> (as N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate) and is passed along by <b>vitamin B12</b> (as methylcobalamin). This one reaction needs both vitamins.</li><li><b>Exit 2, on to cysteine.</b> The enzyme cystathionine synthase joins homocysteine to serine and makes cystathionine, which is then cut to give the amino acid cysteine. This route needs <b>vitamin B6</b> (as pyridoxal phosphate).</li></ul><p><b>Where homocysteine comes from.</b> It comes from methionine. Methionine is first turned into S-adenosylmethionine (SAM), the body's main methyl donor. After SAM gives away its methyl group, what remains is broken down to homocysteine. So there is a cycle: methionine to SAM to homocysteine and back to methionine.</p><p><b>What follows.</b> A deficiency of <b>any</b> of the three vitamins lets homocysteine build up in the blood. A high homocysteine level is a risk factor for cardiovascular disease.</p><p><b>How the lab tells the three apart.</b> A second marker, methylmalonic acid, depends on vitamin B12 alone.</p><ul><li><b>Folate deficiency:</b> high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid, large red cells.</li><li><b>B12 deficiency:</b> high homocysteine <b>and</b> high methylmalonic acid, large red cells, nerve damage.</li><li><b>B6 deficiency:</b> high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid, small red cells.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":59,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":19,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":12,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":6,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":22,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":4,"f":"F0078"}],"tier":1,"n":"74"},{"id":"G096-57-2","s":"Homocysteine is methylated back to methionine. Which enzyme and coenzymes carry out this step?","o":["A. Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","B. Methionine synthase (methyl transferase) with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin","C. Methionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","D. Methylmalonyl CoA mutase with adenosyl cobalamin","E. Methionine synthase with methylene folate and adenosyl cobalamin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Methionine synthase (methyl transferase) with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Methionine synthase (methyl transferase) with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin.</b> Turning homocysteine back into methionine means adding one methyl group to it. The enzyme is <b>methionine synthase</b>. It uses two coenzymes that work as a relay.</p><ol><li><b>Methyl folate</b> (N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate) arrives carrying the methyl group.</li><li>The methyl group is passed to vitamin B12 on the enzyme, which makes <b>methylcobalamin</b>.</li><li>Methylcobalamin hands the methyl group to homocysteine. Homocysteine becomes methionine.</li><li>The folate is left as plain tetrahydrofolate, free to be used again.</li></ol><p>So this single reaction needs both folate and vitamin B12. A lack of either one raises homocysteine.</p><p><b>Why step 4 matters so much: the folate trap.</b> Folate can be changed from most of its forms into the others. The methyl form is the exception. Once folate has become N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, the only way out is this reaction. Without vitamin B12 the reaction stops, and folate piles up in the methyl form. The forms needed to make DNA run out. This is why B12 deficiency causes the same large-cell (megaloblastic) anemia as folate deficiency.</p><p><b>Vitamin B12 has two coenzyme forms, one for each of its two enzymes.</b></p><ul><li><b>Methylcobalamin</b> works with methionine synthase (homocysteine to methionine). Uses folate.</li><li><b>Adenosylcobalamin</b> (deoxyadenosylcobalamin) works with methylmalonyl CoA mutase (methylmalonyl CoA to succinyl CoA). Uses no folate.</li></ul><p>Match the right form to the right enzyme. Also match the right folate: methyl folate makes methionine, while methylene folate makes thymidylate for DNA.</p><p><b>Homocysteine's other exit.</b> Cystathionine synthase, with vitamin B6, turns homocysteine into cystathionine, which goes on to cysteine. Methionine synthase itself does not use vitamin B6.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 52 (http://lpi.oregonstate.edu/mic/vitamins/folate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":57,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":56,"f":"G099"}],"tier":1,"n":"75"},{"id":"G096-57-1","s":"Homocysteine is converted to cystathionine, which then gives cysteine. Which enzyme and coenzyme carry out the homocysteine → cystathionine step?","o":["A. Methionine synthase with methyl folate and methyl cobalamin","B. Methylmalonyl CoA mutase with adenosyl cobalamin","C. Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","D. Propionyl CoA carboxylase with biotin","E. Cystathionine synthase with folic acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Cystathionine synthase with pyridoxine (vitamin B6).</b> This step is the start of a route called transsulfuration, in which the sulfur of homocysteine is passed on to make cysteine. The enzyme <b>cystathionine synthase</b> joins homocysteine to the amino acid serine and makes cystathionine. A second enzyme then cuts cystathionine to release <b>cysteine</b>. Both enzymes need <b>pyridoxal phosphate</b>, the working form of vitamin B6.</p><p><b>The two exits of homocysteine.</b></p><ul><li><b>Back to methionine:</b> methionine synthase, with methyl folate and methylcobalamin (vitamin B12). This is called remethylation.</li><li><b>On to cysteine:</b> cystathionine synthase, with vitamin B6. This is transsulfuration.</li></ul><p>Homocysteine itself comes from methionine, after methionine has been used as a methyl donor in the form of S-adenosylmethionine.</p><p><b>What this means for deficiencies.</b> A lack of folate, B12 or B6 each closes one of the exits, so each raises homocysteine. B6 deficiency raises homocysteine but leaves methylmalonic acid normal, because that marker depends on vitamin B12 only.</p><p><b>Why vitamin B6 fits here.</b> Pyridoxal phosphate is the coenzyme for reactions of amino acids: moving amino groups (transamination), removing carbon dioxide (decarboxylation), and joining or splitting amino acids, as in this pathway. Folic acid does a different job. It carries one-carbon groups.</p><p><b>The neighboring reactions and their vitamins.</b></p><ul><li>Propionyl CoA to methylmalonyl CoA: propionyl CoA carboxylase, with <b>biotin</b>.</li><li>Methylmalonyl CoA to succinyl CoA: methylmalonyl CoA mutase, with <b>adenosylcobalamin</b> (vitamin B12).</li><li>Homocysteine to methionine: methionine synthase, with <b>methyl folate and methylcobalamin</b>.</li><li>Homocysteine to cystathionine: cystathionine synthase, with <b>vitamin B6</b>.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 52 (http://lpi.oregonstate.edu/mic/vitamins/folate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":57,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":56,"f":"G099"}],"tier":1,"n":"76"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":35,"label":"B6 deficiency: microcytic anemia, high homocysteine, normal methylmalonyl CoA","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-43.jpg","cap":"Vitamin B6 deficiency causes microcytic, sideroblastic anemia with ringed sideroblasts","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Vitamin B6 is needed to send homocysteine down transsulfuration to cysteine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-11-1","s":"A 5-year old boy brought to his primary care provider complaining of fatigue. His mother complains about his food habits and says he is a picky eater. On examination it is observed that his skin is pale, his hight is in the 50th percentile, and weight in the 20th percentile for his age. His Hemoglobin level is 10.9 g/dl, the mean corpuscular volume is 63 fL and a normal iron panel. Additional laboratory studies in this patient would most likely reveal which of the following?","o":["A. Elevated homocysteine and decreased succinyl-CoA","B. Elevated homocysteine and elevated methylmalonyl-CoA","C. Elevated homocysteine and normal methylmalonyl-CoA","D. Elevated propionyl-CoA and decreased methylmalonyl-CoA","E. Normal homocysteine and normal methylmalonic acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Elevated homocysteine and normal methylmalonyl-CoA","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Elevated homocysteine and normal methylmalonyl-CoA.</b> Work through it in two stages: first find the vitamin, then predict the labs.</p><p><b>Stage 1: which deficiency?</b> His hemoglobin is low, so he is anemic. His mean corpuscular volume (MCV), the average size of his red cells, is 63 femtoliters. Normal is about 80 to 100, so his cells are small. This is a microcytic anemia. The most common cause of small cells is iron deficiency, but his iron panel is normal. The other vitamin-related cause of small red cells is <b>vitamin B6 deficiency</b>. B6 is the coenzyme of ALA synthase, the first enzyme of heme synthesis. Without it, too little heme and hemoglobin are made, and the cells come out small. A picky eater can run short of it.</p><p><b>Stage 2: what else does vitamin B6 do?</b></p><ul><li><b>Homocysteine.</b> One of the two ways the body clears homocysteine is to turn it into cystathionine and then cysteine. The enzyme, cystathionine synthase, needs vitamin B6. Without B6 this exit closes, and <b>homocysteine rises</b>.</li><li><b>Methylmalonyl CoA.</b> It is turned into succinyl CoA by methylmalonyl CoA mutase. That enzyme needs vitamin B12 and nothing else. His B12 is fine, so <b>methylmalonyl CoA stays normal</b>, and succinyl CoA is made normally.</li></ul><p><b>The three blood-building vitamins compared.</b></p><ul><li><b>Vitamin B6:</b> small red cells (microcytic, sideroblastic). Homocysteine high, methylmalonyl CoA normal.</li><li><b>Folate:</b> large red cells (megaloblastic, MCV above 100). Homocysteine high, methylmalonyl CoA normal. Nerves spared.</li><li><b>Vitamin B12:</b> large red cells. Homocysteine high <b>and</b> methylmalonyl CoA high. Nerve damage.</li></ul><p>So red cell size separates B6 from the other two, and methylmalonyl CoA (measured as methylmalonic acid) separates B12 from folate.</p><p><b>The step just before.</b> Propionyl CoA is turned into methylmalonyl CoA by an enzyme that needs biotin. Only a block there would raise propionyl CoA and lower methylmalonyl CoA.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"77"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":36,"label":"High methylmalonic acid + homocysteine → B12","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"B12's two reactions: methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"},{"src":"../sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"B12 versus folate table: B12 raises homocysteine and methylmalonic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-24-1","s":"A 29-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with worsening fatigue and lightheadedness over the past several months. Laboratory studies are ordered as seen below. Hemoglobin: 9.0 g/dL (normal range 12.0 to 15.5 g/dL); Hematocrit: 29% (normal range 35%-45%); Methylmalonic acid: 1.0 mmol/L (normal < 0.40 mmol/L); Homocysteine: 20 mmol/L (normal 5-15 mmol/L). Which of the following is the most likely etiology of this patient's symptoms?","o":["A. Pyridoxine deficiency","B. Iron deficiency","C. Vitamin B12 deficiency","D. Ascorbate deficiency","E. Folate deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. Vitamin B12 deficiency.</b> She is anemic (low hemoglobin and hematocrit), and two markers are high: <b>methylmalonic acid</b> and <b>homocysteine</b>. Vitamin B12 is the coenzyme for two reactions, and each marker builds up when one of them stops. Only B12 deficiency raises both.</p><p><b>The two reactions of vitamin B12.</b></p><ul><li><b>Methionine synthase</b> turns homocysteine into methionine. It uses B12 in the form methylcobalamin, and it also needs folate. When it stops, <b>homocysteine</b> rises.</li><li><b>Methylmalonyl CoA mutase</b> turns methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA, which enters the tricarboxylic acid cycle. It uses B12 in the form adenosylcobalamin, and it needs <b>no folate</b>. When it stops, methylmalonyl CoA builds up and appears in the blood as <b>methylmalonic acid</b>.</li></ul><p><b>How this separates B12 deficiency from folate deficiency.</b> The blood count cannot tell them apart, because both cause megaloblastic anemia with large red cells. The two markers can.</p><ul><li><b>B12 deficiency:</b> both reactions stop. Homocysteine and methylmalonic acid are both high.</li><li><b>Folate deficiency:</b> only methionine synthase stops. Homocysteine is high. The mutase does not use folate and keeps working, so methylmalonic acid is normal.</li></ul><p>Methylmalonic acid is therefore the marker that points to B12 alone.</p><p><b>The clinical difference.</b> B12 deficiency also damages nerves: tingling in the hands and feet, poor coordination and memory loss. Folate deficiency gives the anemia only.</p><p><b>The other deficiencies listed.</b> Iron deficiency and pyridoxine (B6) deficiency cause anemia with small red cells, and neither raises methylmalonic acid. Ascorbate (vitamin C) deficiency is scurvy.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":3,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":24,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":5,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":41,"f":"D006"}],"tier":1,"n":"78"},{"id":"G031-58-1","s":"A 30 year-old female presenting with worsening fatigue and lightheadedness poor memory over the past several months. She also complained about tingling sensation in her hands and feets. Relevant findings are as follows: Hb 8.2, Hct 20%, Retic 0.2%, MCV 115 fL. The homocysteine and methylmalonic acid levels are increased. What is the likely deficiency in patient? (Normal values for an adult female: Hb 12–16, Hct 37–47%, Retic 1.0–2.0%, MCV 78–98 fL.)","o":["A. Vitamin B6","B. Vitamin B12","C. Iron","D. Folate","E. Riboflavin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Vitamin B12.</b> Three findings together point to vitamin B12: <b>large red cells</b>, <b>nerve symptoms</b>, and a rise in <b>both</b> homocysteine and methylmalonic acid.</p><p><b>Reading her labs.</b></p><ul><li><b>Hemoglobin 8.2 and hematocrit 20%:</b> both low. She is anemic, which explains the fatigue and lightheadedness.</li><li><b>MCV 115 femtoliters:</b> MCV is the mean corpuscular volume, the average size of a red cell. Above 100 is macrocytic, meaning large cells. Large cells mean DNA synthesis is slow, which points to folate or B12.</li><li><b>Reticulocytes 0.2%:</b> reticulocytes are newly released red cells. A low count means the marrow is failing to produce cells. She is not losing blood or destroying cells.</li><li><b>Homocysteine and methylmalonic acid both high:</b> B12 is needed to clear both. Folate deficiency raises homocysteine only.</li></ul><p><b>Why the nerves are affected in B12 deficiency.</b> Tingling in the hands and feet and poor memory are nerve signs. Folate deficiency does not cause them. They come from B12's own reactions.</p><ul><li>When methylmalonyl CoA mutase stops, methylmalonyl CoA and propionyl CoA build up. They get built into abnormal fatty acids, and these go into the myelin that insulates nerves. The myelin is abnormal.</li><li>When methionine synthase stops, less methionine is made, so there is less S-adenosylmethionine, the methyl donor that nerve tissue needs.</li></ul><p>The anemia usually appears first. The nerve damage can become permanent if treatment is delayed.</p><p><b>A warning about treatment.</b> Giving a large dose of folic acid alone to a person with B12 deficiency improves the anemia, because fresh folate lets the marrow make DNA again. But it does nothing for the nerves, which keep getting worse while the warning sign, the anemia, has gone. This is called masking. The rule: treat megaloblastic anemia with both B12 and folic acid until the cause is known.</p><p><b>The other deficiencies.</b> Vitamin B6 and iron deficiency give small red cells. Riboflavin deficiency gives cracked lips and a sore tongue.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":58,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"79"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":36,"label":"Folate deficiency: homocysteine only, no neuropathy","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"B12 versus folate table: folate gives anemia only, homocysteine only","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-1","s":"A folate-deficient individual with normal cobalamin (vitamin B12) is expected to have:","o":["A. High homocysteine (HCY) and low methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","B. Low homocysteine (HCY) and low methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","C. High homocysteine (HCY) and normal methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","D. Low homocysteine (HCY) and high methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","E. High homocysteine (HCY) and high methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"High homocysteine (HCY) and normal methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels","a":"C","why":"<p><b>C. High homocysteine (HCY) and normal methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels.</b> Take the two markers one at a time and ask whether each one needs folate.</p><ul><li><b>Homocysteine</b> is turned back into methionine by the enzyme methionine synthase. That enzyme gets the methyl group from <b>folate</b> (methyl-tetrahydrofolate), with vitamin B12 as the go-between. No folate means no methyl group to give. The reaction stops and <b>homocysteine rises</b>.</li><li><b>Methylmalonic acid</b> comes from methylmalonyl CoA. The enzyme that clears it, methylmalonyl CoA mutase, needs vitamin B12 and <b>no folate</b>. This person's B12 is normal, so the enzyme works, and <b>methylmalonic acid stays normal</b>. Normal means normal. It does not fall below normal.</li></ul><p><b>The patterns side by side.</b></p><ul><li><b>Folate deficiency:</b> homocysteine high, methylmalonic acid normal.</li><li><b>Vitamin B12 deficiency:</b> homocysteine high, methylmalonic acid high. B12 serves both enzymes, so both stop.</li><li><b>Vitamin B6 deficiency:</b> homocysteine high, methylmalonic acid normal, but with small red cells. B6 is needed for homocysteine's other exit, to cystathionine.</li></ul><p><b>Why this matters at the bedside.</b> Folate deficiency and B12 deficiency give the same blood picture: megaloblastic anemia with large red cells and hypersegmented neutrophils. The methylmalonic acid level is what separates them. So do the nerves. B12 deficiency causes tingling, poor coordination and memory loss. Folate deficiency spares the nerves.</p><p><b>Folate deficiency in brief.</b> It is caused by poor intake of leafy greens, higher need in pregnancy, gut disease, alcoholism, or drugs such as methotrexate. Stores are small, so it develops within weeks. The high homocysteine is a risk factor for cardiovascular disease. In early pregnancy, low folate causes neural tube defects.</p><p><b>The treatment rule.</b> Until the cause of a megaloblastic anemia is known, give both B12 and folic acid. Folic acid alone can correct the anemia of B12 deficiency while the nerve damage goes on.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"80"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":37,"label":"Vegetarian case: diet, not intrinsic factor loss, causes the B12 deficiency","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-47.jpg","cap":"Cobalamin is made by microorganisms and is absent from plants","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 47"},{"src":"../sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"Absorption causes to exclude: low acid, proton pump inhibitors, autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-2-1","s":"Annie, 41 years old, has had a burning sensation and red patches on her tongue and cheeks for more than a year. She had been a strict vegetarian for 2.5 years and had not consumed any animal sources (milk, cheese, fish, meat, or eggs) during that time. She was not taking any medication. She also has paleness, fatigue, glossitis with papillary atrophy, paresthesia in the left mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve and disturbance of memory. Hematologic tests: RBC count 1.63 (normal 3.90–5.03), hemoglobin 7.2 g/dL (12.0–15.5), MCV 144 fL (80–100), RDW 25% (13 ± 1.5), serum folate 7.73 ng/mL (3–16), serum cobalamin 71.8 pmol/L (118–716). Neutrophil nuclei were hyper segmented. Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies were not detected. The possible cause of B12 (cobalamin) deficiency in Annie's case is:","o":["A. Intake of Isoniazid (INH)","B. Strict vegetarian diet","C. Lack of Intrinsic factor","D. Relatively large body cobalamin stores","E. Excess alcohol consumption and folate trap"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Strict vegetarian diet","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Strict vegetarian diet.</b> Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is made only by microorganisms and is found only in animal foods. Annie has eaten no meat, fish, eggs, milk or cheese for 2.5 years. The other main cause of B12 deficiency, failed absorption, has been ruled out: she has <b>no antibodies against intrinsic factor</b> and she takes no medication. So the cause is her diet.</p><p><b>Reading her results.</b></p><ul><li><b>Red cell count and hemoglobin are very low:</b> severe anemia. This explains the paleness and fatigue.</li><li><b>MCV 144 femtoliters:</b> MCV is the average size of a red cell, and normal is 80 to 100. Her cells are very large. This is macrocytic anemia.</li><li><b>RDW 25%:</b> RDW is the red cell distribution width, the spread of cell sizes. A high value means the cells vary a lot in size.</li><li><b>Hypersegmented neutrophils:</b> the nucleus has too many lobes. Together with the large red cells, this means megaloblastic anemia, which comes from slow DNA synthesis.</li><li><b>Serum folate is normal and serum cobalamin is low:</b> the megaloblastic anemia is from B12, not folate.</li><li><b>Sore, smooth tongue (glossitis), burning mouth:</b> the lining of the mouth renews quickly, so it suffers when DNA synthesis is slow.</li><li><b>Tingling in the face and poor memory:</b> nerve damage, which B12 deficiency causes and folate deficiency does not.</li></ul><p><b>Two ways to become B12 deficient.</b></p><ul><li><b>Diet:</b> no animal foods. This is slow, because the liver stores 2 to 5 mg, enough for years. Her 2.5 years without animal foods fits.</li><li><b>Failed absorption:</b> too little stomach acid, loss of intrinsic factor (autoimmune disease or stomach surgery), long use of acid-blocking drugs, pancreatic disease, or disease of the ileum. When the cause is failed absorption, the anemia is called pernicious anemia.</li></ul><p>Her large body stores explain why the deficiency took so long to appear. They are not a cause of it.</p><p><b>Treatment.</b> B12 injections into muscle together with folic acid, and B12-rich food. Her mouth lesions shrank within a week and were gone in two.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":2,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"81"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":37,"label":"Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies → pernicious anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"Autoimmune parietal-cell loss removes intrinsic factor: pernicious anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"},{"src":"../sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells is required for ileal B12 uptake","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-56-1","s":"Which among the following rules out megaloblastic anemia due to malabsorption of B12 ?","o":["A. High red cell width","B. Hyper segmented neutrophil","C. No history of medication","D. Current symptoms more than a year","E. Undetectable Anti Intrinsic factor antibodies"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Undetectable Anti Intrinsic factor antibodies","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Undetectable Anti Intrinsic factor antibodies.</b> The question is how to tell that a B12 deficiency is <b>not</b> caused by failed absorption. The most common cause of B12 malabsorption is an autoimmune disease in which the body makes <b>antibodies against intrinsic factor</b> and destroys the parietal cells of the stomach that make it. If the test for these antibodies is negative, that cause is excluded. This is the only finding on the list that says anything about absorption.</p><p><b>Why intrinsic factor matters.</b> Intrinsic factor is a protein made by the parietal cells of the stomach. B12 from food must bind to it, and the complex is then absorbed in the ileum. Without intrinsic factor, B12 from food passes straight through the gut.</p><p><b>Pernicious anemia.</b> Megaloblastic anemia caused by failed B12 absorption is called pernicious anemia. Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor is its most common cause. It is treated for life with B12 injections, which bypass the gut.</p><p><b>The other causes of B12 malabsorption.</b></p><ul><li>Too little stomach acid, common in the elderly, so B12 is not freed from food.</li><li>Long-term proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole, which block acid.</li><li>Removal of the stomach.</li><li>Pancreatic insufficiency.</li><li>Disease or removal of the ileum, as in Crohn disease.</li></ul><p><b>Findings that every megaloblastic anemia shares.</b> These tell you that the anemia is megaloblastic, but not why.</p><ul><li><b>Large red cells:</b> a high mean corpuscular volume (MCV).</li><li><b>A high red cell distribution width (RDW):</b> the cells vary widely in size.</li><li><b>Hypersegmented neutrophils:</b> more than 5 lobes in the nucleus.</li></ul><p>They are the same whether the cause is diet or malabsorption, and whether the missing vitamin is B12 or folate. How long the symptoms have lasted does not show the cause either.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":72,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty practice","n":"Vitamins questions Fall 2026","sl":21,"f":"G098"},{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":56,"f":"F0319"}],"tier":1,"n":"82"}]},{"l":"BIO-C7","u":37,"label":"B12 deficiency: folate trap → anemia; low SAM → nerve signs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G099-67.jpg","cap":"B12 deficiency: folate trap cuts nucleic acid synthesis; low SAM cuts methylation","where":"2026 Middletown faculty review, slide 67"},{"src":"../sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Without B12, folate is trapped as N5-methyl-THF, and methionine and SAM fall","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"G094-3-3","s":"Annie, a strict vegetarian, has megaloblastic anemia from cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency (MCV 144 fL, hyper segmented neutrophils, serum cobalamin 71.8 pmol/L, normal 118–716) with paresthesia and disturbance of memory. Which among the following best explains the etiopathogenesis of megaloblastic anemia and neurological signs/symptoms respectively in this case?","o":["A. Folate trap and low homocysteine","B. Low niacin and high homocysteine","C. High methionine and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","D. Folate trap and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","E. High aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate trap and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Folate trap and low S-adenosylmethionine (SAM).</b> The question asks for two mechanisms, in order. The <b>megaloblastic anemia</b> comes from the <b>folate trap</b>. The <b>nerve signs</b> are tied to <b>low SAM</b>. Both start at the same enzyme, methionine synthase, which stops working without vitamin B12.</p><p><b>What methionine synthase does.</b> It takes a methyl group from N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate (the methyl form of folate) and, using vitamin B12, gives it to homocysteine. Two useful things come out: <b>methionine</b>, and plain tetrahydrofolate that is free to be used again.</p><p><b>Mechanism 1: the folate trap causes the anemia.</b></p><ol><li>The different forms of folate can be converted into one another, with one exception. The step that makes the methyl form runs one way only.</li><li>The only way out of the methyl form is methionine synthase, which needs B12.</li><li>Without B12, that exit is shut. Folate keeps flowing into the methyl form and cannot get out. Most of the body's folate ends up trapped there.</li><li>The other forms of folate, which make thymidylate and purines for DNA, run dry.</li><li>The marrow cannot copy DNA. Red cell precursors grow but cannot divide, and become megaloblasts.</li></ol><p>The body has plenty of folate, but it is all in a useless form. That is why the blood folate level can look normal, as hers does, while the cells behave as if they had none.</p><p><b>Mechanism 2: low SAM harms the nerves.</b> Methionine is the raw material for S-adenosylmethionine (SAM), the body's main donor of methyl groups. With methionine synthase stopped, less methionine and less SAM are made. Nerve tissue needs methylation reactions to maintain myelin and to make signaling molecules, and these slow down. Result: tingling (paresthesia) and memory problems.</p><p><b>What else changes.</b> Homocysteine cannot be converted, so it is <b>high</b>. Methionine is <b>low</b>. B12's second enzyme, methylmalonyl CoA mutase, also stops, so methylmalonic acid rises, and that adds to the nerve damage by producing abnormal fatty acids for myelin.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 52 (http://lpi.oregonstate.edu/mic/vitamins/folate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Macrocytic anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":3,"f":"G094"}],"tier":1,"n":"83"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":38,"label":"Iron intake is controlled at export from the enterocyte","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-7.jpg","cap":"Uptake: lumen to enterocyte. Systemic absorption: enterocyte to blood through ferroportin.","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 7"},{"src":"../sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"Low hepcidin: ferroportin exports iron. High hepcidin: iron stays and is shed.","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"G106-9-1","s":"The body acquires iron from the diet mainly by:","o":["A. Regulating the movement of iron from the gut lumen into the enterocyte","B. Regulating the movement of iron from the enterocyte into the circulation","C. Regulating the synthesis of ferritin in the liver for storage of excess iron","D. Regulating the synthesis of serum transferrin for the movement of iron in the blood","E. Regulating the removal of iron with the bile pigments"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Regulating the movement of iron from the enterocyte into the circulation","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Regulating the movement of iron from the enterocyte into the circulation.</b> The body has <b>no regulated way to excrete iron</b>. Once iron is in, it stays. So the only way to keep the right amount is to control how much comes in. The control point is the step where iron leaves the intestinal cell (the enterocyte) and enters the blood.</p><p><b>Two steps that are easy to blur.</b></p><ul><li><b>Uptake:</b> iron moves from the gut lumen into the enterocyte. Iron inside an enterocyte is not yet in the body. The cell lives only a few days and is then shed into the stool, and any iron still inside it leaves with it.</li><li><b>Absorption into the body:</b> iron moves from the enterocyte into the blood. This is the regulated step.</li></ul><p><b>The gate and its controller.</b> The exit door is a protein called <b>ferroportin</b>. It is the only known exporter of iron from cells. Its controller is <b>hepcidin</b>, a small hormone made by the liver. Hepcidin binds ferroportin and causes it to be destroyed.</p><ul><li><b>Body iron is high:</b> the liver makes more hepcidin. Ferroportin is removed. Iron stays in the enterocyte and is lost when the cell is shed.</li><li><b>Body iron is low:</b> the liver makes less hepcidin. Ferroportin stays open, and more iron enters the blood.</li></ul><p><b>The same gate controls recycled iron.</b> Ferroportin also lets iron out of macrophages, which recycle the iron of old red cells, and out of liver cells, which store it. This matters, because most of the iron used each day comes from recycling.</p><p><b>The iron budget.</b> The body holds 3 to 4 grams of iron. About 2.5 g is in hemoglobin and about 1 g is stored in the liver as ferritin. Only 1 to 2 mg is absorbed each day, and about the same amount is lost through shed cells and bleeding. The bone marrow uses 20 to 30 mg a day, nearly all of it recycled by macrophages.</p><p><b>Why such tight control.</b> Free iron is toxic. It reacts with hydrogen peroxide to form the hydroxyl radical, which damages membranes and leads to scarring of the liver, pancreas and heart. Ferritin (storage) and transferrin (transport in the blood) handle iron that is already in the body. They do not decide how much comes in.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 6 (Systemic Iron Balance Depends on Iron Export)","L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown quiz","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas practice quiz Fall 2026 (not for credit)","sl":1,"f":"G106"}],"tier":1,"n":"84"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":39,"label":"Hepcidin's target is ferroportin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"Hepcidin binds ferroportin; ferroportin internalized and degraded in lysosomes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G032-12.jpg","cap":"Liver hepcidin removes ferroportin from macrophages and enterocytes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-29-1","s":"Anemia of chronic disease is the result of increased hepcidin synthesis induced by inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6. Hepcidin inhibits iron absorption by enterocytes as well as iron release from storage in liver and macrophages. Which of the following proteins is the target of hepcidin?","o":["A. Divalent metal ion transporter 1 (DMT1)","B. Ferri reductase (Dcytb)","C. Heme oxygenase (HOX1)","D. Transferrin","E. Ferroportin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferroportin","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Ferroportin.</b> Hepcidin is the hormone that controls iron, and it has one target: <b>ferroportin</b>, the protein that lets iron out of cells. Hepcidin binds ferroportin on the cell surface. The cell then pulls the ferroportin inside and destroys it in its lysosomes. With the exit door gone, iron is locked inside the cell.</p><p><b>Where this happens.</b> Ferroportin is the only iron exporter, and it sits on three kinds of cell. Hepcidin acts on all three.</p><ul><li><b>Enterocytes</b> (intestinal cells): iron from food cannot pass into the blood. It is lost when the cell is shed.</li><li><b>Macrophages:</b> iron recycled from old red cells is trapped inside them.</li><li><b>Liver cells:</b> stored iron cannot be released.</li></ul><p>The result in every case is a fall in the iron level of the blood.</p><p><b>About hepcidin.</b> It is a small peptide of 25 amino acids, made by liver cells. The direction is what matters: <b>high hepcidin closes the gate</b>, so blood iron falls. <b>Low hepcidin opens the gate</b>, so more iron is absorbed and released.</p><p><b>Anemia of chronic disease.</b> In long-lasting inflammation, infection or cancer, the signaling molecule interleukin-6 (IL-6) makes the liver produce more hepcidin. Iron is locked inside macrophages. The body has plenty of iron in storage, but the bone marrow cannot get it, so red cell production falls. Lowering blood iron helps starve bacteria of iron. The price is anemia.</p><p><b>What the other proteins do.</b> They are the steps of iron absorption, in order.</p><ol><li><b>Dcytb</b>, a ferrireductase on the surface of the enterocyte, reduces ferric iron (Fe<sup>3+</sup>) from food to ferrous iron (Fe<sup>2+</sup>).</li><li><b>DMT1</b> (divalent metal transporter 1) carries the Fe<sup>2+</sup> into the enterocyte.</li><li><b>Heme oxygenase</b> frees iron from heme, both heme absorbed from meat and heme from old red cells in macrophages.</li><li><b>Ferroportin</b> exports the iron from the cell. This is the step hepcidin blocks.</li><li><b>Transferrin</b> carries the iron in the blood as Fe<sup>3+</sup>.</li></ol>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":7,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":29,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":49,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":193,"f":"F3881"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":13,"f":"F1022"}],"tier":1,"n":"85"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":39,"label":"Interleukin-6 directly raises liver hepcidin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-13.jpg","cap":"Interleukin-6/STAT3 signaling raises hepcidin gene transcription in hepatocytes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13"},{"src":"../sl/G032-30.jpg","cap":"Interleukin-6 raises hepcidin, lowers ferroportin: anemia of chronic disease","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F0295-22-1","s":"Chronic inflammation can lead to microcytic anemia by increased signaling via interleukin-6 (IL-6) leading to decreased acquisition of dietary iron. Which of the following describes the direct effect of IL-6 signaling?","o":["A. Decreased translation of ferritin mRNA by the liver","B. Increased translation of the transferrin receptor mRNA by the liver","C. Increased synthesis of ferroportin by the enterocytes","D. Decreased synthesis of transferrin by the liver","E. Increased synthesis of hepcidin by the liver"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased synthesis of hepcidin by the liver","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Increased synthesis of hepcidin by the liver.</b> Interleukin-6 (IL-6) is a signaling molecule released during inflammation. It acts on liver cells and switches on the gene for <b>hepcidin</b>. Hepcidin is the hormone that shuts down the entry of iron into the blood. So the direct effect of IL-6 is more hepcidin from the liver. Everything else follows from that.</p><p><b>The chain of events.</b></p><ol><li>Chronic inflammation raises IL-6.</li><li>IL-6 makes the liver produce more hepcidin.</li><li>Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the iron exit door of cells, and causes it to be destroyed.</li><li>In the gut, dietary iron cannot leave the intestinal cells. In macrophages, recycled iron is trapped.</li><li>Blood iron falls. The bone marrow cannot get enough iron to make hemoglobin, and anemia develops. This is <b>anemia of chronic disease</b>. It starts with normal-sized red cells and can become microcytic (small cells) over time.</li></ol><p>The stores are full, but the iron is locked away. It is seen in chronic infection, autoimmune disease such as rheumatoid arthritis, cancer and chronic kidney disease.</p><p><b>Why the body does this.</b> Bacteria need iron to grow. Hiding iron is a defense against infection.</p><p><b>What raises and what lowers hepcidin.</b></p><ul><li><b>Raises it:</b> high iron stores (normal negative feedback), inflammation through IL-6, and infection.</li><li><b>Lowers it:</b> iron deficiency, low oxygen, anemia, and a bone marrow that is working hard to make red cells. Each of these means \"the body needs more iron.\"</li></ul><p><b>The lab pattern of anemia of chronic disease.</b> Serum iron is low. Ferritin, the marker of stores, is normal or high. TIBC (total iron-binding capacity, which reflects the amount of transferrin) is low. Compare iron deficiency, where ferritin is low and TIBC is high. Both have low serum iron. In iron deficiency the macrophages are empty. In anemia of chronic disease they are packed with iron.</p><p><b>Treatment.</b> Treat the underlying disease. Iron by mouth works poorly, because the gate in the gut is closed.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 32 (Anemia of Chronic Disease and Functional Iron Deficiency)","L36 Iron slide 13 (Signals That Regulate Hepcidin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Week 15 PE IS 11/12/26: vitamins & iron, post-discussion deck (questions only = G089)","sl":41,"f":"G090"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":80,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":22,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":22,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":27,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"86"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":40,"label":"TIBC reflects how much transferrin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-21.jpg","cap":"TIBC reflects transferrin level: how strongly the body tries to capture iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/F0257-114.jpg","cap":"Serum iron = bound sites, UIBC = free sites, TIBC = total sites","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 114"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-117-1","s":"A 55-year-old man is found to have bronze skin pigmentation and mildly elevated liver enzymes during a routine exam. Further testing shows high serum iron, high serum ferritin, and transferrin saturation >50%. His TIBC is low-normal. Based on the information, which finding best explains why TIBC becomes low or normal in iron excess?","o":["A. The liver decreases transferrin production when iron levels are high","B. Excess iron increases the number of free transferrin binding sites","C. Transferrin molecules are destroyed by iron overload","D. Bone marrow stops using transferrin in high iron states"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The liver decreases transferrin production when iron levels are high","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. The liver decreases transferrin production when iron levels are high.</b> TIBC stands for total iron-binding capacity. It measures how much iron the blood could carry, and that depends on how much <b>transferrin</b>, the iron-carrying protein, is present. Transferrin is made by the liver, and the liver adjusts the amount to the body's iron. When iron is plentiful the liver makes less transferrin, so TIBC is low or normal.</p><p><b>Think of TIBC as \"iron hunger.\"</b></p><ul><li><b>Iron is scarce:</b> the liver makes more transferrin, to catch every bit of iron. TIBC is high.</li><li><b>Iron is plentiful, or there is inflammation:</b> the liver makes less transferrin. TIBC is low or normal.</li></ul><p><b>What each iron test measures.</b></p><ul><li><b>Serum iron:</b> iron circulating on transferrin, ready for use. Normal 60 to 170 micrograms per deciliter.</li><li><b>TIBC:</b> the amount of transferrin. Normal 250 to 450.</li><li><b>Transferrin saturation:</b> how full the transferrin is. It equals serum iron divided by TIBC, times 100. Normal 20 to 50%.</li><li><b>Ferritin:</b> the storage protein for iron inside cells. The blood level reflects the total body stores. Normal 30 to 300 nanograms per milliliter.</li></ul><p><b>His diagnosis is hereditary hemochromatosis.</b> A fault in the liver's iron-sensing genes (most often the gene HFE) keeps hepcidin too low. Hepcidin is the hormone that limits iron absorption. Without enough of it, the gut absorbs too much iron for decades. His results fit: high serum iron, high ferritin (full stores) and a saturation above 50%. The saturation is high for two reasons at once. There is more iron, and there is less transferrin to carry it.</p><p><b>Why his skin and liver are affected.</b> When transferrin is full, the extra iron circulates loosely bound and settles in the organs. It damages them by producing oxygen radicals. Skin turns bronze. The liver is injured (raised liver enzymes, later cirrhosis). The pancreas is injured (diabetes), and so are the heart and the joints.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 23 (| Marker | What it reflects |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":85,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Updated review of lectures 34-35 (fat- and water-soluble vitamins, Dr. Kamath) Fall 2026","sl":77,"f":"G099"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":117,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":117,"f":"F0938"}],"tier":1,"n":"87"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":40,"label":"Hemochromatosis labs: ↑iron, ↓TIBC, ↑ferritin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Hemochromatosis row: high iron, normal-low TIBC, high ferritin and saturation","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-30-1","s":"Which of the following serum values is most consistent with the diagnosis of hemochromatosis in a patient?","o":["A. Low iron, low TIBC (total iron-binding capacity), and low ferritin","B. Increased iron, normal TIBC, and low ferritin","C. Low iron, increased TIBC, and decreased ferritin","D. Low iron, low TIBC, and increased ferritin","E. Increased iron, low TIBC, and increased ferritin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased iron, low TIBC, and increased ferritin","a":"E","why":"<p><b>E. Increased iron, low TIBC, and increased ferritin.</b> Hemochromatosis is a disease of <b>iron overload</b>. Work out each test from what it measures.</p><ul><li><b>Serum iron is high:</b> too much iron is entering the blood.</li><li><b>Ferritin is high:</b> ferritin is the storage protein, and its blood level reflects total body stores. The stores are overfull.</li><li><b>TIBC is low or normal:</b> TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) reflects the amount of transferrin, the iron carrier in blood. The liver makes less transferrin when iron is plentiful.</li><li><b>Transferrin saturation is high,</b> above 45 to 50%. This is the earliest abnormal test, because iron inflow is high from the start. Ferritin rises later, as the stores fill.</li></ul><p><b>The cause.</b> Hereditary hemochromatosis comes from mutations in genes the liver uses to sense iron. The most common is in the gene HFE. The liver is blind to the body's iron, so it makes too little <b>hepcidin</b>, the hormone that limits iron entry. With hepcidin low, ferroportin (the iron exit door of gut cells) stays open, and the gut absorbs too much iron every day. The body cannot excrete iron, so it builds up over decades.</p><p><b>Who and what.</b> It shows in middle-aged adults and is more severe in men, because menstruation removes iron in women. Iron deposits in organs and damages them: the liver (cirrhosis), the pancreas (diabetes), the heart (cardiomyopathy), the skin (bronze color) and the joints. The combination is called \"bronze diabetes.\"</p><p><b>Treatment.</b> Regular removal of blood (phlebotomy). Each unit removed takes iron with it, and the marrow draws on the stores to replace the red cells.</p><p><b>The four classic iron panels.</b></p><ul><li><b>Iron deficiency:</b> iron low, TIBC high, ferritin low.</li><li><b>Anemia of chronic disease:</b> iron low, TIBC low, ferritin normal or high.</li><li><b>Hemochromatosis:</b> iron high, TIBC low or normal, ferritin high.</li><li><b>Transfusion overload:</b> the same panel as hemochromatosis. The history of many transfusions tells them apart. It is treated with iron-binding drugs (chelators), not phlebotomy, because these patients are already anemic.</li></ul>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 self-assessment","n":"Week 15 Vitamins and Iron Canvas self-assessment quiz Fall 2026 (graded)","sl":8,"f":"G120"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":30,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":15,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":51,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":16,"f":"F2435"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":195,"f":"F3881"}],"tier":1,"n":"88"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":41,"label":"Low ferritin labs → iron deficiency anemia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Pattern table: low ferritin = true iron deficiency, unlike other disorders","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G032-24.jpg","cap":"Three-question method: serum iron, then TIBC, then ferritin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0295-24-1","s":"A 25-year-old mother of two who is consulting her primary care physician for fatigue, dizziness, thinning of nails and hair loss. She has pale conjunctivae, pale gums, swollen tongue, several chipped teeth and pale nailbeds. No yellowing of the sclera observed. Blood samples were drawn and the results of initial laboratory work-up are shown below: MCV 61 (80–98 fL); RBC 3.5 (4.2–5.9 x 10^6 cells/µL); Serum iron 25 (50–150 µg/dL); Serum ferritin (female) 5 (11–307 ng/mL); Total iron binding capacity 450 (250–310 µg/dL); % transferrin saturation 5 (20–50 %). What is your probable diagnosis?","o":["A. Obstructive Jaundice","B. B6 deficiency","C. Lead poisoning","D. Iron deficiency","E. Iron overload"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron deficiency","a":"D","why":"<p><b>D. Iron deficiency.</b> Every number points the same way.</p><ul><li><b>MCV 61:</b> MCV is the mean corpuscular volume, the average size of a red cell. Hers is far below 80, so the cells are small. This is a microcytic anemia.</li><li><b>Serum iron 25:</b> low. Little iron is circulating.</li><li><b>Ferritin 5:</b> very low. Ferritin reflects the body's iron stores, so her stores are empty. This is the decisive result. A low ferritin means true iron deficiency.</li><li><b>TIBC 450:</b> high. TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) reflects the amount of transferrin, the iron carrier. The liver makes more transferrin when iron is scarce, to capture as much as it can.</li><li><b>Transferrin saturation 5%:</b> very low. There is a lot of transferrin and almost no iron on it.</li></ul><p><b>Why the cells are small.</b> Iron is needed to make heme, and heme is needed for hemoglobin. With too little iron, each red cell gets less hemoglobin, and the cells come out small and pale.</p><p><b>The order in which things fall.</b> Stores go first, so ferritin drops first. Then serum iron falls. Hemoglobin production fails last. So ferritin is low before anemia even appears.</p><p><b>Her signs.</b> Fatigue and dizziness come from carrying less oxygen. Pale conjunctivae, gums and nailbeds come from having less hemoglobin, the red pigment. Thin nails, hair loss and a swollen tongue are also typical. Her sclerae are not yellow, so she is not breaking down red cells.</p><p><b>Causes of iron deficiency.</b> Blood loss is the most common: heavy periods or bleeding in the gut. Then increased need (pregnancy, growth), poor absorption (celiac disease, stomach surgery) and poor intake. A young mother of two has had the iron cost of two pregnancies on top of her periods.</p><p><b>Treatment.</b> Iron by mouth, usually ferrous sulfate. It works well because her hepcidin is low, so the gut's iron gate is wide open. Iron is given into a vein if she cannot absorb or tolerate it. Always look for the source of blood loss.</p><p><b>Other small-cell anemias, and how they differ.</b> Vitamin B6 deficiency and lead poisoning also give small cells, because they block heme synthesis. But in both, iron is not missing, so ferritin is normal or high.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown faculty review","n":"Exam 2 GI review Fall 2026 (Dr. Bagh)","sl":83,"f":"G096"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":24,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":24,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":26,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":30,"f":"F2421"}],"tier":1,"n":"89"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":42,"label":"Iron deficiency → microcytic, hypochromic RBCs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-28.jpg","cap":"Smear of small, pale red cells in iron deficiency anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G032-26.jpg","cap":"Microcytic anemia from reduced hemoglobin synthesis; red cell size drawings","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-4-1","s":"A 25-year-old female presents to the outpatient clinic complaining of progressive fatigue, generalized weakness, and shortness of breath over the past several weeks. She reports that even light physical activity, such as climbing stairs or walking quickly, makes her unusually tired. She has also noticed that her skin looks paler than usual, and her fingernails have become thin and spoon-shaped. Laboratory findings: Serum iron 30 µg/dL (normal 60–170); TIBC 450 µg/dL (normal 250–370); Ferritin 10 ng/mL (normal 12–150); MCV 70 fL (normal 80–100). What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","o":["A. Macrocytic","B. Microcytic, hypochromic","C. Normocytic","D. Hemolytic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic, hypochromic","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Microcytic, hypochromic.</b> \"Microcytic\" means small red cells. \"Hypochromic\" means pale red cells, with less hemoglobin in each. Her MCV (mean corpuscular volume, the average red cell size) is 70 femtoliters, below the normal 80 to 100, so her cells are small. The rest of her labs show the cause: <b>iron deficiency</b>.</p><p><b>Reading the iron tests.</b></p><ul><li><b>Serum iron 30:</b> low.</li><li><b>Ferritin 10:</b> low. Ferritin reflects iron stores, so the stores are empty.</li><li><b>TIBC 450:</b> high. TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) reflects the amount of transferrin, the protein that carries iron. The liver makes more of it when iron is scarce.</li><li><b>Transferrin saturation:</b> serum iron divided by TIBC, times 100. Here 30 ÷ 450 × 100 is about 7%. Normal is 20 to 50%.</li></ul><p><b>Why iron deficiency makes cells small and pale.</b> Iron is the center of heme, and heme is the working part of hemoglobin. Without enough iron, the developing red cell cannot make enough hemoglobin. It ends up small, and it has a wide pale center because hemoglobin is what gives the cell its red color.</p><p><b>Her symptoms.</b> Less hemoglobin carries less oxygen, so she is tired, weak and short of breath on the stairs. She is pale. Thin, spoon-shaped nails (koilonychia) are a classic sign of long-standing iron deficiency. In a 25-year-old woman the likely cause is menstrual blood loss.</p><p><b>The types of anemia by cell size.</b></p><ul><li><b>Microcytic (MCV below 80):</b> a problem making hemoglobin. Iron deficiency is the most common cause.</li><li><b>Normocytic (MCV 80 to 100):</b> for example early anemia of chronic disease.</li><li><b>Macrocytic (MCV above 100):</b> a problem making DNA. Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency.</li></ul><p><b>Hemolytic anemia</b> means red cells are being destroyed. The cells are usually normal in size, the patient is often jaundiced, and iron is not lost, because the iron from the broken cells is recycled. So ferritin is not low.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":4,"f":"G092"}],"tier":1,"n":"90"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":42,"label":"Iron deficiency labs: ↓iron, ↑TIBC, ↓ferritin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Iron deficiency row: low iron, high TIBC, low ferritin, saturation, MCV","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/F0257-119.jpg","cap":"Iron deficiency markers incl. negative marrow iron stain and low hemoglobin","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 119"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-5-1","s":"Which laboratory finding pattern is most consistent with Iron Deficiency Anemia?","o":["A. Low ferritin, High TIBC","B. High ferritin, High TIBC","C. Low ferritin, Low TIBC","D. High ferritin, Low TIBC","E. Normal ferritin, Normal TIBC"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Low ferritin, High TIBC","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Low ferritin, High TIBC.</b> These two results together are the signature of iron deficiency anemia. Each one follows from what the test measures.</p><ul><li><b>Ferritin is low.</b> Ferritin is the protein that stores iron inside cells. A little leaks into the blood in proportion to the stores, so the blood level shows how much iron the body has in reserve. In iron deficiency the reserve is empty. A ferritin below 30 nanograms per milliliter means iron deficiency.</li><li><b>TIBC is high.</b> TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) shows how much transferrin, the iron-carrying protein, is in the blood. When iron is scarce, the liver makes more transferrin to capture whatever iron it can. You can think of a high TIBC as iron hunger.</li></ul><p><b>The rest of the panel.</b> Serum iron is low. Transferrin saturation (serum iron divided by TIBC) is low. The red cells are small, so the MCV (mean corpuscular volume) is low. Hepcidin, the hormone that blocks iron absorption, is low, which lets the gut absorb as much iron as possible.</p><p><b>Three questions for any iron panel.</b></p><ol><li><b>Is serum iron low or high?</b> Low means too little circulating iron, from true deficiency or from iron being locked away. High raises the question of overload.</li><li><b>Is TIBC high?</b> High means iron hunger, which points to deficiency. Low or normal points to chronic disease or overload.</li><li><b>What is the ferritin?</b> Low confirms empty stores. Normal or high fits chronic disease or overload.</li></ol><p><b>The patterns.</b></p><ul><li><b>Low ferritin, high TIBC:</b> iron deficiency.</li><li><b>High or normal ferritin, low TIBC, low serum iron:</b> anemia of chronic disease. Inflammation raises hepcidin, and iron is trapped in macrophages. The stores are full but out of reach.</li><li><b>High ferritin, low or normal TIBC, high serum iron and high saturation:</b> iron overload, as in hemochromatosis.</li></ul><p>Low ferritin with low TIBC, or high ferritin with high TIBC, do not occur as usual patterns, because empty stores and iron hunger go together.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 22 (Iron Panel Markers: What’s Normal?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":5,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":5,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":14,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"91"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":42,"label":"MCV separates microcytic from macrocytic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-26.jpg","cap":"MCV classes: microcytic below 80 fL, macrocytic above 100 fL, with causes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-6-1","s":"Which finding best differentiates microcytic anemia from macrocytic anemia?","o":["A. Microcytic: Low MCV, Low ferritin; Macrocytic: High MCV, Normal/High ferritin","B. Microcytic: High MCV; Macrocytic: Low MCV","C. Both have High MCV","D. Microcytic: Low MCV; Macrocytic: Normal MCV","E. Both have Low ferritin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic: Low MCV, Low ferritin; Macrocytic: High MCV, Normal/High ferritin","a":"A","why":"<p><b>A. Microcytic: Low MCV, Low ferritin; Macrocytic: High MCV, Normal/High ferritin.</b> MCV is the mean corpuscular volume, the average size of a red cell. It is the number that sorts anemias by cell size. Microcytic means small cells, with an MCV below 80 femtoliters. Macrocytic means large cells, with an MCV above 100. Normal is 80 to 100.</p><p><b>Why the two kinds differ: two different things are slow.</b></p><ul><li><b>Microcytic anemia: hemoglobin synthesis is slow.</b> The red cell precursor keeps dividing while it waits to fill up with hemoglobin. Each division makes the cells smaller. The most common cause is <b>iron deficiency</b>, because iron is needed for heme. In iron deficiency the stores are empty, so <b>ferritin is low</b>.</li><li><b>Macrocytic anemia: DNA synthesis is slow.</b> The cell keeps growing but cannot divide on time, so it stays large. The causes are <b>vitamin B12 or folate deficiency</b>. Iron is not involved. Red cell production is held up, so iron is not being used, and <b>ferritin is normal or high</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Causes of small red cells.</b> Iron deficiency (low ferritin). Later stages of anemia of chronic disease (ferritin normal or high). Vitamin B6 deficiency and lead poisoning, which block heme synthesis (ferritin normal or high).</p><p><b>Causes of large red cells.</b> Vitamin B12 deficiency and folate deficiency. The marrow shows megaloblasts, and the blood shows neutrophils with too many nuclear lobes. B12 deficiency also damages nerves.</p><p><b>A rule for iron disorders.</b> Iron problems give small or normal-sized cells. They never give large cells. So a high MCV sends you to B12 and folate, and a low MCV sends you to the iron panel.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":6,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":6,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":16,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"92"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":42,"label":"Iron absorbed as Fe²⁺ through DMT1","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Apical ferrireductase makes Fe²⁺; DMT1 carries Fe²⁺ into the duodenal cell","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-7-1","s":"Which form of iron is primarily used by the body for absorption and incorporation into hemoglobin?","o":["A. Ferric iron (Fe³⁺)","B. Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺)","C. Metallic iron (Fe⁰)","D. Heme iron only","E. Stored iron in ferritin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺)","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺).</b> Iron exists in the body in two forms, and each has its own jobs.</p><ul><li><b>Ferrous iron, Fe<sup>2+</sup>:</b> the reactive form. It is the form that <b>crosses membranes</b> and the form that <b>goes into heme</b>.</li><li><b>Ferric iron, Fe<sup>3+</sup>:</b> the stable form. It is the form that travels in the blood on transferrin and the form that is stored in ferritin.</li></ul><p>A short rule: Fe<sup>2+</sup> to cross and to enter heme, Fe<sup>3+</sup> to travel and to rest. Enzymes switch iron between the two forms at almost every step.</p><p><b>How iron is absorbed, step by step.</b> This happens mainly in the duodenum.</p><ol><li>Most iron in food is non-heme iron in the ferric form, Fe<sup>3+</sup>. It cannot cross the surface of the intestinal cell.</li><li>An enzyme on the cell surface, the ferrireductase Dcytb, reduces it to Fe<sup>2+</sup>. Stomach acid and vitamin C help with this.</li><li>The transporter <b>DMT1</b> (divalent metal transporter 1) carries Fe<sup>2+</sup> into the cell. It only carries the ferrous form.</li><li>Heme iron from meat enters whole through its own carrier. Inside the cell, heme oxygenase releases its iron as Fe<sup>2+</sup>. Heme iron is the best absorbed.</li><li>Inside the cell, iron is either stored in ferritin or exported into the blood through <b>ferroportin</b>, as Fe<sup>2+</sup>.</li><li>Outside the cell, a copper-containing enzyme, hephaestin, oxidizes it to Fe<sup>3+</sup>. Only Fe<sup>3+</sup> binds <b>transferrin</b>, which carries it in the blood.</li></ol><p><b>Into hemoglobin.</b> Red cell precursors in the marrow take up transferrin through transferrin receptors. Inside the cell the iron is reduced to Fe<sup>2+</sup> again. The enzyme ferrochelatase then inserts Fe<sup>2+</sup> into the ring called protoporphyrin to make heme. Heme iron must stay Fe<sup>2+</sup> to bind oxygen.</p><p><b>Practical points.</b> Iron tablets are given as a ferrous salt, such as ferrous sulfate, because that is the form DMT1 takes up. Vitamin C improves absorption. Tea, coffee, calcium and plant phytates reduce it.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)","L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":7,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":7,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":18,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"93"}]},{"l":"BIO-C8","u":42,"label":"Pallor: less hemoglobin made","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G032-28.jpg","cap":"Low iron cuts hemoglobin synthesis, giving small, pale red cells","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-8-1","s":"Why do patients with iron deficiency anemia often appear pale?","o":["A. Increased breakdown of hemoglobin","B. Decreased hemoglobin production reduces oxygen-carrying capacity","C. Increased melanin synthesis in the skin","D. Excess iron stored in tissues","E. Increased red blood cell size"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased hemoglobin production reduces oxygen-carrying capacity","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Decreased hemoglobin production reduces oxygen-carrying capacity.</b> Hemoglobin is the red pigment of blood. The pink color of the skin, lips, nailbeds and the inside of the eyelids comes from the hemoglobin in the small vessels beneath them. In iron deficiency the body cannot make enough hemoglobin, so these tissues look pale.</p><p><b>Why hemoglobin falls.</b> Hemoglobin is the protein globin plus heme, and each heme has an iron atom at its center. That iron is what binds oxygen. Without enough iron, the red cell precursors cannot make enough heme. They produce cells that are small (microcytic) and pale (hypochromic), with less hemoglobin in each one.</p><p><b>What else less hemoglobin does.</b> Blood carries less oxygen. Tissues get less oxygen, especially during effort. This causes fatigue, weakness, dizziness and shortness of breath on exertion. Long-standing deficiency also gives thin, spoon-shaped nails and hair loss.</p><p><b>Skin color as a clue to the cause of anemia.</b></p><ul><li><b>Pale:</b> too little hemoglobin is being made, as in iron deficiency.</li><li><b>Yellow (jaundice):</b> red cells are being broken down too fast. Their heme is turned into bilirubin, which is yellow.</li><li><b>Bronze:</b> iron overload, as in hemochromatosis. This is the opposite problem.</li></ul><p><b>Treatment and how to tell it is working.</b> Iron by mouth, usually ferrous sulfate. Within about 7 to 10 days the reticulocyte count rises. Reticulocytes are newly made red cells, so a rise shows that the marrow has started producing again. The most important step is to find and treat the cause, which is most often blood loss.</p>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency anemia clinical case Fall 2026, post-discussion (with answers)","sl":8,"f":"G092"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":20,"f":"F0131"}],"tier":1,"n":"94"}]},{"l":"BIO-C9","u":43,"label":"Menkes: kinky hair, lax skin → lysyl oxidase","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G031-68.jpg","cap":"Menkes: copper-containing lysyl oxidase cross-links collagen; kinky hair, loose skin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 68"}],"q":[{"id":"G031-70-1","s":"A 2 year-old child is hospitalized for evaluation of poor growth and low muscle tone. The most striking physical finding is brittle unruly hair, but the child also has increased joint laxity and sagging thin skin. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely?","o":["A. High ceruloplasmin","B. Low serum copper","C. Low serum zinc","D. Low Serum selenium","E. Low serum Iodine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Low serum copper","a":"B","why":"<p><b>B. Low serum copper.</b> Brittle, kinky hair in a very young child, together with loose joints, thin sagging skin, poor growth and low muscle tone, is <b>Menkes disease</b>. It is a disease of <b>copper deficiency</b>. The child eats copper, but it cannot get out of the cells of the intestine into the blood. So the copper level in the blood is low. Ceruloplasmin, the copper-carrying protein of the blood, is low too.</p><p><b>The defect.</b> A copper pump called <b>ATP7A</b> moves absorbed copper out of intestinal cells into the blood and helps deliver it to tissues such as the brain. In Menkes disease the gene for ATP7A is mutated. Copper is stuck in the intestinal lining, while the blood, liver and brain go short. The gene is on the X chromosome, so the disease affects mostly boys, and it shows in infancy.</p><p><b>Why the hair, skin and joints are abnormal.</b> Several enzymes need copper. The key one is <b>lysyl oxidase</b>. It changes certain lysines in collagen so that neighboring collagen strands can link to each other. These cross-links are what make collagen fibers strong. Without copper there are no cross-links, and collagen is weak. Skin sags, joints are loose, blood vessels and bone are fragile, and the hair is coarse, kinky, brittle and pale or steel-colored. The brain is also short of copper, which gives poor growth and low muscle tone.</p><p><b>Compare Wilson disease, the other copper disease.</b> A related pump, <b>ATP7B</b>, works in the liver. It loads copper onto ceruloplasmin and sends extra copper into the bile, which is the body's main way of getting rid of copper.</p><ul><li><b>Menkes:</b> ATP7A, X-linked, starts in infancy. Copper is stuck in the gut, so the body is copper <b>deficient</b>. Kinky hair, loose skin and joints.</li><li><b>Wilson:</b> ATP7B, autosomal recessive. Copper cannot leave in the bile, so the body is copper <b>overloaded</b>. Liver disease in the teens, then movement and psychiatric problems at age 20 to 30, and brown copper rings at the edge of the cornea (Kayser–Fleischer rings). It is treated with penicillamine, a drug that binds copper so that it can be excreted.</li></ul><p>Ceruloplasmin is low in <b>both</b> diseases. In Menkes there is no copper to load onto it. In Wilson the pump that loads it is broken.</p><p><b>What a lack of the other minerals does.</b> Low zinc gives a rash around the mouth and other body openings. Low selenium gives heart muscle disease. Low iodine gives thyroid problems. None of them weakens collagen.</p>","ex":"","fl":["Option B is printed as \"High serum copper\", which leaves no right option: Menkes disease lowers serum copper and ceruloplasmin, so B reads \"Low serum copper\" here."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 72 (Review of Objective 5)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 lecture question","n":"Water-soluble vitamins lecture Fall 2026 (Dr. Kamath): self-assessment questions","sl":70,"f":"G031"}],"tier":1,"n":"95"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"BIO-C1","h":"Fuel values, fuel stores and BMI","p":"","s":"Fuel stores and body size","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C1","h":"Energy balance and what sets BMR","p":"","s":"Daily energy expenditure","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C1","h":"Estimating BMR and total expenditure","p":"","s":"Daily energy expenditure","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Carbohydrates and the glycemic index","p":"","s":"Carbohydrates and fiber","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Dietary fiber","p":"","s":"Carbohydrates and fiber","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Protein-energy malnutrition","p":"","s":"Protein","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Dietary fats","p":"","s":"Fats and eating patterns","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C2","h":"Diet patterns and guidelines","p":"","s":"Fats and eating patterns","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Blood glucose control and the islets","p":"","s":"Blood glucose control and insulin synthesis","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Insulin synthesis and the C-peptide","p":"","s":"Blood glucose control and insulin synthesis","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"How the β cell secretes insulin","p":"","s":"Insulin secretion","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"How insulin acts","p":"","s":"Insulin action and recombinant insulin","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Glucagon: structure, release and effects","p":"","s":"Glucagon","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"The cAMP–PKA cascade in the liver","p":"","s":"Glucagon","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"BIO-C3","h":"Defenses, symptoms, types and alcohol","p":"","s":"Hypoglycemia","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Vitamin families and fat-soluble absorption","p":"","s":"What vitamins are","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Forms, storage and retinoic acid","p":"","s":"Vitamin A","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Deficiency and toxicity","p":"","s":"Vitamin A","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Making calcitriol and what it does","p":"","s":"Vitamin D","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Rickets, osteomalacia and toxicity","p":"","s":"Vitamin D","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C4","h":"Vitamin K: clotting, warfarin and deficiency","p":"","s":"Vitamins K and E","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Vitamin C and scurvy","p":"","s":"Working forms and vitamin C","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Why the PDH complex needs five cofactors","p":"","s":"The PDH complex and thiamine","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Thiamine (B1): beriberi and Wernicke–Korsakoff","p":"","s":"The PDH complex and thiamine","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Riboflavin (B2)","p":"","s":"The electron carriers: riboflavin and niacin","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Niacin (B3) and pellagra","p":"","s":"The electron carriers: riboflavin and niacin","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C5","h":"Pantothenic acid (B5) and biotin (B7)","p":"","s":"Pantothenic acid and biotin","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP)","p":"","s":"The porphyrias and lead poisoning","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Porphyria cutanea tarda and the other porphyrias","p":"","s":"The porphyrias and lead poisoning","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Transport, liver handling and the gut","p":"","s":"Breaking heme down to bilirubin","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Measuring bilirubin and pre-hepatic jaundice","p":"","s":"Jaundice","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C6","h":"Hepatic and post-hepatic jaundice","p":"","s":"Jaundice","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Vitamin B6 and sideroblastic anemia","p":"","s":"Anemia by cell size and vitamin B6","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Folic acid and the one-carbon pool","p":"","s":"Folic acid","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Supply, absorption and pernicious anemia","p":"","s":"Vitamin B12","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"B12's two reactions and the folate trap","p":"","s":"Vitamin B12","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Telling B12 from folate deficiency","p":"","s":"B12 versus folate deficiency","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"BIO-C7","h":"Nerve damage, folate masking and a vegetarian case","p":"","s":"B12 versus folate deficiency","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Why iron needs a gate","p":"","s":"Iron's gate: ferroportin and hepcidin","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Hepcidin, the master regulator","p":"","s":"Iron's gate: ferroportin and hepcidin","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Reading the iron panel","p":"","s":"The iron panel and iron deficiency","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Iron deficiency anemia","p":"","s":"The iron panel and iron deficiency","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"BIO-C8","h":"Case: fatigue and spoon nails","p":"","s":"The iron panel and iron deficiency","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"BIO-C9","h":"Copper: Wilson versus Menkes","p":"","s":"Copper and zinc","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"}]};